// The rigor layer: why each result is actually true.
//
// This is the part a procedures course leaves out and an elite one does not.
// Every entry is a real argument — not a restatement, not a mnemonic — written
// so a motivated beginner can follow every line.
//
// q = the question the argument answers · a = the intuition, shaped like the
// proof · s = the argument, one line per step · m = what having it buys you
export const WHY = {

signed: {
 q: 'Why do two negatives make a positive?',
 a: 'Multiplying by −1 reflects the whole number line about zero. Reflect twice and every point is back where it started — so the second flip has to undo the first. The algebra below is that picture, made airtight.',
 s: ['Start from something nobody disputes: anything times zero is zero. So a × 0 = 0.',
   'Zero can be written as b + (−b), for any b at all. Substituting: a × (b + (−b)) = 0.',
   'Multiplication distributes over addition, so that expands to ab + a(−b) = 0.',
   'Read that as a statement about a(−b): it is the number you add to ab to get zero. That is exactly what “−ab” means, so a(−b) = −ab. One negative flips the sign.',
   'Now run the same argument again with (−a) in place of a: (−a)(−b) = −((−a)b) = −(−ab) = ab. The second flip undoes the first.'],
 m: 'Nothing here was a rule handed to you — it followed from “zero times anything is zero” plus distribution. That is what it means for arithmetic to be forced rather than chosen, and it is why no one can define (−1)(−1) = −1 and keep the rest of algebra.'
},

'order-ops': {
 q: 'Why multiplication before addition, rather than plain left to right?',
 a: 'The convention is not arbitrary etiquette. It is chosen so that the way we write polynomials means what we want without a forest of brackets.',
 s: ['We want to write 3x² + 2x + 7 and have it mean “three x-squareds, plus two xs, plus seven”.',
   'Read strictly left to right, that expression would mean (((3x² + 2)x) + 7) — a completely different function.',
   'To recover the intended reading under left-to-right rules you would have to write it as ((3 × x²) + (2 × x)) + 7, with brackets on every term, forever.',
   'So the precedence rule is the choice that makes the common case bracket-free: the tighter operation binds first. Exponent, then multiply and divide, then add and subtract.',
   'Every programming language inherits the same choice for the same reason, which is why a + b * c parses as a + (b * c) in C, Python, Java and the rest.'],
 m: 'Precedence is a compression scheme for notation. Once you see it as “which brackets are we agreeing to leave out”, operator precedence tables in a language reference stop being trivia to memorise.'
},

fractions: {
 q: 'Why must denominators match before you can add, and why does dividing by a fraction flip it?',
 a: 'A fraction is a count of a unit: 3/8 is three eighths, the way 3 metres is three metres. You can add three metres to two metres. You cannot add three metres to two seconds until you convert.',
 s: ['a/b means “a copies of the unit 1/b”. Addition counts copies, and counting only works when the copies are the same size.',
   'So a/b + c/d needs both written in one unit. The unit 1/(bd) works for both: a/b is ad copies of it, c/d is cb copies.',
   'Hence a/b + c/d = (ad + cb)/(bd). The common denominator is not a ritual — it is a unit conversion.',
   'Now division. a/b ÷ c/d asks: how many c/d fit inside a/b?',
   'Scale both by d/c. Fitting c/d into a/b is the same count as fitting 1 into (a/b)(d/c), and fitting 1 into something is just that something. So a/b ÷ c/d = (a/b)(d/c) — the flip is forced, not a trick.'],
 m: 'Both rules come from one idea: a fraction names a unit. That idea returns for units in physics, for dimensional analysis in engineering, and for normalising probabilities.'
},

exponents: {
 q: 'Why is a⁰ = 1, and why is a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ? Nothing is being multiplied.',
 a: 'They are not extra definitions bolted on. They are the only values that let one rule keep working, and mathematicians chose them the way you would choose the only key that still fits the lock.',
 s: ['Start with the one rule that is obvious from counting factors: aᵐ × aⁿ = aᵐ⁺ⁿ, because you are just putting m copies next to n copies.',
   'Now ask what a⁰ must be if that rule is to survive. Set n = 0: aᵐ × a⁰ = aᵐ⁺⁰ = aᵐ.',
   'So a⁰ is the number that leaves aᵐ unchanged under multiplication. There is exactly one such number: 1. Hence a⁰ = 1, forced.',
   'Same move for negatives. Set n = −m: aᵐ × a⁻ᵐ = aᵐ⁻ᵐ = a⁰ = 1.',
   'So a⁻ᵐ is whatever you multiply aᵐ by to get 1 — the reciprocal. Hence a⁻ᵐ = 1/aᵐ, also forced.'],
 m: 'This is a pattern you will meet repeatedly: a definition is extended past its original meaning by demanding that an existing law keep holding. It is how 0! = 1 is settled, and how exponents are later stretched to fractions and then to the complex numbers.'
},

factors: {
 q: 'Why does adding a number’s digits tell you whether 9 divides it?',
 a: 'Because in the eyes of 9, every power of ten is just 1. Ten is nine-and-one, a hundred is ninety-nine-and-one, and so on — the nines fall away and only the digits are left.',
 s: ['Write any number by its digits: for example 4726 = 4×1000 + 7×100 + 2×10 + 6.',
   'Every power of ten is one more than a run of nines: 10 = 9+1, 100 = 99+1, 1000 = 999+1.',
   'Substitute: 4726 = 4(999+1) + 7(99+1) + 2(9+1) + 6 = [4×999 + 7×99 + 2×9] + [4+7+2+6].',
   'Every term in the first bracket is a multiple of 9. So the whole number is a multiple of 9, plus the digit sum.',
   'Therefore the number is divisible by 9 exactly when its digit sum is. The same argument with 10 = 9+1 replaced by 10 = 3×3+1 gives the rule for 3.'],
 m: 'You have just done modular arithmetic without the notation — the statement is 10 ≡ 1 (mod 9). The same reasoning is what makes hash functions, check digits on a bank card, and the RSA lesson later in this course work.'
},

decimals: {
 q: 'Why does 0.999… equal 1 exactly, rather than being just short of it?',
 a: 'People hear 0.999… as a number creeping toward 1 and never arriving. But it is not a journey — it is a single fixed number, and the question is only which one.',
 s: ['Give it a name: let x = 0.999… — a fixed quantity, not a process.',
   'Multiply by ten: 10x = 9.999… The digits after the point are the same endless run of nines.',
   'Subtract the first from the second: 10x − x = 9.999… − 0.999…, and the endless tails cancel exactly, leaving 9x = 9.',
   'So x = 1.',
   'A second check: if 0.999… and 1 were different numbers, some number would have to lie strictly between them. Name it. You cannot — and on the real line, two numbers with nothing between them are the same number.'],
 m: 'The lesson is that decimal notation is a name for a number, and one number can have two names. Confusing a name with the thing named is behind a lot of floating-point confusion later.'
},

ratios: {
 q: 'Why is cross-multiplication allowed?',
 a: 'It looks like a licence to move numbers diagonally across an equals sign, which would be alarming if it were true. It is really one ordinary move done twice.',
 s: ['Start with the proportion a/b = c/d, with b and d not zero.',
   'You may multiply both sides of any equation by the same non-zero quantity. Multiply both sides by b: a = (c/d)b.',
   'Do it again, this time by d: ad = cb.',
   'That is cross-multiplication. Nothing travelled diagonally — both sides were multiplied by bd, and the denominators cancelled.',
   'It fails precisely when b or d is zero, which is exactly when the original fractions were meaningless.'],
 m: 'Naming the legal move — “do the same thing to both sides” — is what turns a memorised shortcut into something you can re-derive when you have forgotten which way the arrows point.'
},

expressions: {
 q: 'Why can you collect 3x + 2x into 5x, but not 3x + 2y?',
 a: 'The same reason you can add three apples to two apples and get five apples, but adding three apples to two oranges leaves you holding a description rather than a number.',
 s: ['3x means x + x + x, and 2x means x + x. Together that is five copies of x.',
   'Formally it is the distributive law read backwards: 3x + 2x = (3 + 2)x = 5x.',
   'The law only applies when the two terms share the same factor to pull out. x is shared; that is what “like terms” means.',
   'With 3x + 2y there is no common factor to extract, so there is nothing to collect and the expression is already as simple as it gets.',
   'The same test decides harder cases: 3x²y and 5x²y are like terms, 3x²y and 3xy² are not, because the shared factor is not the same.'],
 m: '“Simplify” is not an instruction to make an expression shorter by any means available. It means: apply the distributive law until no factor can be pulled out. That precision is what stops algebra from feeling like guesswork.'
},

inequalities: {
 q: 'Why does multiplying an inequality by a negative reverse it?',
 a: 'An inequality is a claim about direction on the number line. Multiplying by a negative reflects the line about zero — and a reflection turns every “to the right of” into “to the left of”.',
 s: ['Say what a < b actually means: b − a is positive.',
   'Take a negative number c. Multiplying a positive by a negative gives a negative, so c(b − a) is negative.',
   'Expand it: cb − ca is negative.',
   'A quantity being negative means the thing subtracted is the larger one, so cb < ca — equivalently ca > cb.',
   'We began with a < b and ended with ca > cb. The relation reversed, and it reversed because c was negative. Had c been positive, step two would have kept the sign and the relation would have survived unchanged.'],
 m: 'This is the most-missed rule in school algebra, and it is missed because it is taught as a rule. Once you have seen that it follows from “negative times positive is negative”, you cannot really forget it — and you can rebuild it in the exam if you do.'
},

coordinates: {
 q: 'Why is the distance between two points √((x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)²)?',
 a: 'It is not a new formula. It is Pythagoras, applied to a triangle you draw yourself out of the two points.',
 s: ['Take the two points and complete a right triangle: travel horizontally from the first, then vertically to the second.',
   'The horizontal leg has length |x₂ − x₁| and the vertical leg |y₂ − y₁| — each is just how far you moved along that axis.',
   'The straight-line distance you want is the hypotenuse of that triangle.',
   'Pythagoras says hypotenuse² = leg² + leg², so d² = (x₂−x₁)² + (y₂−y₁)².',
   'Take the positive square root. The absolute values vanish on their own, because squaring already discards the sign.'],
 m: 'This is the first appearance of a pattern that runs to the end of the course: distance is a square root of a sum of squares. In three dimensions you add a z term. In a few thousand dimensions it is the length of an embedding vector, and it is still this triangle.'
},

'word-problems': {
 q: 'Why insist on writing “let x = …” when you already know what you are solving for?',
 a: 'Because the sentence is doing two jobs at once — deciding what the unknown is, and committing to its units — and skipping it is where most wrong answers are actually born.',
 s: ['A word problem is a translation task: English in, algebra out. Translation needs a dictionary, and “let x = the number of hours” is the dictionary entry.',
   'Without it, the same symbol quietly changes meaning mid-solution — x starts as a speed and ends as a time — and the algebra stays valid while the answer becomes nonsense.',
   'Naming the unknown also fixes its units, which gives you a free correctness check: if the two sides of your equation carry different units, the equation is wrong before you solve it.',
   'It converts each English relation into one symbolic one. “Five more than twice the number” has exactly one reading once x is pinned down: 2x + 5.',
   'Finally it tells you when you are finished. You solved for x; the question may have asked for something else, and the definition line is what catches that.'],
 m: 'This is the same discipline as declaring a variable with a type and a name that means something. The bugs it prevents in algebra are precisely the bugs bad naming causes in code.'
},

transformations: {
 q: 'Why does f(x − 3) move the graph to the right when the sign says minus?',
 a: 'The minus is not moving the graph. It is compensating: the new function has to be handed a larger input before it will pass the old function the value it wants. Chase a single point through the substitution and the direction stops being a rule to remember.',
 s: ['A graph is nothing but a set of points (input, output). So to compare two graphs, compare their points.',
   'Let g(x) = f(x − h), and take any point on the graph of f — call it (t, f(t)).',
   'Feed t + h into g: g(t + h) = f(t + h − h) = f(t). So (t + h, f(t)) sits on the graph of g — same height, h further along the x-axis.',
   'Every point of g arises this way and no others do: an arbitrary point (x, g(x)) is (x, f(x − h)), which is exactly the point (x − h, f(x − h)) of f moved right by h. The two graphs match point for point under “shift right by h”.',
   'The direction now has a reason rather than a mnemonic. To reach a given value, g must be given an input h larger than the one f needed — the graph lags behind by h, which on the page is a move to the right. Changes made outside, like f(x) + k, act on the output after the fact and so read forwards.'],
 m: 'This is the whole content of “inside acts backwards”, and it survives into composition, into inverse functions and into code. When a chart maps data to pixels, or a shader slides a sampled image, the offset that moves the picture one way is subtracted from the coordinate handed to the lookup — the same sign flip, for exactly this reason.'
},

exponential: {
 q: 'Why does 2ⁿ eventually beat every polynomial, no matter how big the polynomial’s powers and constants are?',
 a: 'An exponential is not merely a fast-growing function; it contains every polynomial inside it. Expanding 2ⁿ as (1 + 1)ⁿ shows this literally: one of the terms in that expansion is already a polynomial in n of any degree you care to name, and the rest of the terms are still waiting.',
 s: ['Start with the binomial theorem: 2ⁿ = (1 + 1)ⁿ = C(n,0) + C(n,1) + ⋯ + C(n,n). Every term is positive, so 2ⁿ is at least as large as any single term you pick out.',
   'Fix the polynomial degree k and pick the term j = k+1, valid once n ≥ k+1: 2ⁿ ≥ C(n, k+1) = n(n−1)(n−2)⋯(n−k) / (k+1)!, a numerator of exactly k+1 factors.',
   'Every one of those factors is at least n − k, and once n ≥ 2k we have n − k ≥ n/2. So 2ⁿ ≥ (n/2)^(k+1) / (k+1)!.',
   'Divide by nᵏ to compare growth: 2ⁿ / nᵏ ≥ n^(k+1) / (2^(k+1)(k+1)! · nᵏ) = n / (2^(k+1)(k+1)!). With k fixed, that denominator is a constant, so the ratio grows at least linearly in n and passes every bound.',
   'Hence 2ⁿ / (C·nᵏ) also runs off to infinity for any constant C: a constant only divides through and postpones the crossing. For every polynomial there is an n beyond which 2ⁿ is larger, and stays larger.'],
 m: 'This is the honest reason exponential algorithms are not merely slow but hopeless. A faster machine multiplies your throughput by a constant, and the argument just showed a constant cannot win — it only shifts the crossover a little. It also explains the shape of the pain: going from n = 20 to n = 30 multiplies the work by 2¹⁰ ≈ 1000, and the same ten items again costs another factor of a thousand.'
},

logarithms: {
 q: 'Why can Big-O write O(log n) without ever saying which base?',
 a: 'Because there is really only one logarithm. Changing the base does not change the shape of the function at all — it multiplies the whole thing by a fixed number, the same number at every input. Constant multiples are precisely what Big-O throws away.',
 s: ['Take the definition as the only starting point: log_b x = y means bʸ = x. Nothing else is assumed.',
   'First the power rule, which the argument needs. Let u = log_c m, so cᵘ = m. Then mʸ = (cᵘ)ʸ = c^(uy), and reading that back through the definition gives log_c(mʸ) = uy = y·log_c m.',
   'Now set y = log_b x, so bʸ = x. Apply log base c to both sides: log_c(bʸ) = log_c x, and by the power rule that left side is y·log_c b.',
   'So y·log_c b = log_c x, and dividing by the non-zero number log_c b gives log_b x = log_c x / log_c b.',
   'The factor 1/log_c b does not depend on x. Every logarithm is therefore a constant multiple of every other: log₂ n = ln n / ln 2 ≈ 1.4427 · ln n, for every n at once.'],
 m: 'An algorithm that does log₂ n steps and one that does log₁₀ n steps differ by a fixed factor of about 3.32, which is exactly the kind of difference Big-O is built to ignore — so O(log n) is unambiguous and the base is genuinely not missing information. The same identity is why a calculator carrying only ln can produce any base you ask for, and why a plot of log n on log paper is a straight line whatever base you chose.'
},

sequences: {
 q: 'Why does 1 + ½ + ¼ + ⅛ + ⋯ add up to exactly 2 rather than growing forever?',
 a: 'Multiply the whole sum by the ratio and you get back nearly the same sum — shifted along by one term. Subtracting the two versions annihilates everything in the middle, so an infinite pile of terms collapses to a short piece of algebra. That collapse is the proof.',
 s: ['Work with a finite piece first, because a finite sum is something we already know how to handle: S = a + ar + ar² + ⋯ + arⁿ⁻¹, which is n terms.',
   'Multiply by r: rS = ar + ar² + ⋯ + arⁿ. This is the same list of terms as S, minus the first one and plus one new one at the end.',
   'Subtract: S − rS = a − arⁿ, since every term between cancels. So S(1 − r) = a(1 − rⁿ), and for r ≠ 1, S = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r). This part is exact for every n, with no limits involved.',
   'Now let n grow, with |r| < 1. Write 1/|r| = 1 + t where t > 0; expanding (1 + t)ⁿ gives 1 + nt plus further positive terms, so (1/|r|)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nt, which passes any bound. Hence |r|ⁿ ≤ 1/(1 + nt), squeezed down to zero.',
   'So the partial sums a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) settle on a/(1 − r) and on no other number — and that is what the infinite sum means. With |r| ≥ 1 step four fails, the terms never shrink, and there is nothing to settle on.'],
 m: 'The finite formula is the useful half in computing. When a dynamic array doubles, the copies done over its whole life are n + n/2 + n/4 + ⋯, which the same argument bounds by 2n — a constant amount of copying per insert, no matter how many times the array grew. That is where amortised O(1) appending actually comes from, and why halving rather than doubling would ruin it.'
},

piecewise: {
 q: 'Why is making the pieces meet enough for continuity, but not enough to remove the corner in |x| or ReLU?',
 a: 'Continuity is a condition on values; differentiability is a condition on slopes. Making two pieces meet closes the gap in height and says nothing about the two directions of travel arriving at the seam. Compute the difference quotient from each side and the mismatch is visible immediately.',
 s: ['Continuity at a seam c means three numbers agree: what the left piece approaches, what the right piece approaches, and the value assigned at c. For f(x) = |x| at 0 all three are 0, so |x| is continuous there — no jump, nothing torn.',
   'The derivative asks a stricter question. It asks whether the ratio [f(0 + h) − f(0)]/h closes in on one single number as h shrinks to zero, approaching from both sides.',
   'Take h > 0: |h| = h, so the ratio is h/h = 1, exactly 1 for every positive h however small. Shrinking h changes nothing.',
   'Take h < 0: |h| = −h, so the ratio is (−h)/h = −1, exactly −1 for every negative h.',
   'The ratio is pinned at 1 on one side and −1 on the other, so it approaches no single value and the derivative at 0 does not exist. Continuity forbade a jump in the value; it never promised anything about the slope. ReLU is the same picture with slopes 0 and 1.'],
 m: 'This is why deep learning frameworks have to *choose* a value for ReLU′(0) — usually 0 — and why that is a convention rather than a derivation: the mathematics genuinely supplies no answer there. It costs nothing in practice because a single point has no width, and every other point of ReLU has an honest derivative, which is the whole reason the kink is tolerable.'
},

binomial: {
 q: 'Why are the coefficients in (a + b)ⁿ combination counts rather than something you look up?',
 a: 'Multiplying out n brackets is not a mysterious operation — it is making one choice per bracket and adding up every way of choosing. Terms that look alike are then piled together, and the size of each pile is a count of choices. The coefficients are counts because collecting like terms is counting.',
 s: ['(a + b)ⁿ means n copies of (a + b) multiplied together. Distribution, applied repeatedly, says the expansion is: from each bracket take either the a or the b, multiply your n picks, and sum over every possible way to pick. Nothing is lost and nothing extra appears.',
   'Each such product is a string of as and bs, and multiplication does not care about order, so the product depends only on how many bs were taken. If k brackets gave a b, the product is aⁿ⁻ᵏbᵏ.',
   'So collecting like terms means grouping all the choice-patterns that took the same number of bs. The coefficient of aⁿ⁻ᵏbᵏ is therefore the number of ways to select which k of the n brackets contributed a b — which is what C(n,k) means by definition.',
   'Hence (a + b)ⁿ = Σ from k = 0 to n of C(n,k) aⁿ⁻ᵏ bᵏ, with every coefficient earned rather than tabulated.',
   'Two consequences drop out for free. Put a = b = 1: 2ⁿ = Σ C(n,k), because choosing a b from each bracket or not is choosing a subset of n things. And Pascal’s rule C(n,k) = C(n−1,k−1) + C(n−1,k) comes from splitting the choices by what the last bracket did — it gave a b, or it did not.'],
 m: 'Reading the coefficients as counts is what links three things that otherwise look unrelated: Pascal’s triangle, the number of subsets of an n-element set, and the binomial distribution, whose probabilities are these same counts weighted by pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ. Pascal’s rule is also a genuine dynamic program — each entry from two already computed — which is how C(n,k) is calculated in code without ever forming n!.'
},

integrals: {
 q: 'Why does an antiderivative — a question about slopes — compute an area?',
 a: 'Track the area under the curve as a running total, measured from a fixed left edge out to a right edge you are free to move. Nudge that right edge a hair further along and the total gains a thin sliver, near enough a rectangle whose height is the curve there and whose width is the nudge. So the rate at which accumulated area grows is exactly the height of the curve — which is to say the area function is an antiderivative of the curve. Everything below is that sliver, made airtight.',
 s: ['Let f be continuous, and define A(x) to be the area under f from a fixed left edge a out to x. Then A(a) = 0, and A(b) is the area we are after.',
   'Move the right edge from x to x + h, taking h > 0 for the moment. The area gained, A(x + h) − A(x), is a sliver sitting over the strip [x, x + h], so it is at least (the smallest height of f on that strip) × h and at most (the largest height) × h.',
   'Divide through by h. The difference quotient (A(x + h) − A(x))/h is trapped between the smallest and largest values f takes on a strip of width h. As h → 0 the strip closes onto the single point x, and because f is continuous both bounds close onto f(x); the same argument with the strip on the left handles h < 0. So A′(x) = f(x), and A is an antiderivative of f.',
   'Now take any antiderivative F of f — anything at all with F′ = f. Then (F − A)′ = f − f = 0 across the whole interval, and a function with zero derivative everywhere on an interval cannot change value, since the mean value theorem from Calculus I says any change between two points forces a non-zero slope somewhere between them. Hence F = A + C for some constant C.',
   'Therefore F(b) − F(a) = (A(b) + C) − (A(a) + C) = A(b) − 0, which is the area itself. That is ∫ₐᵇ f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a): the fundamental theorem, and the reason two substitutions can stand in for a sum of infinitely many rectangles.'],
 m: 'This is what makes calculus a calculus rather than an endless numerical exercise: every accumulation question — total error over a run, expected value over a continuous distribution, work done — collapses to finding one function whose derivative is the integrand. It also tells you precisely what to do when no such function exists in closed form, which is the common case in real work: go back to step two and add the slivers yourself. That is all a numerical integrator is, and the width of your slices is the accuracy you are buying.'
},

techniques: {
 q: 'Why can you treat du = g′(x) dx as though the dx cancels, when dx is not a number you can divide by?',
 a: 'Substitution is not sleight of hand with infinitesimals. It is the chain rule read from right to left: differentiating a composition always leaves behind an extra factor of the inner function’s derivative, so an integrand that carries such a factor is advertising that it came from a composition. The du notation is bookkeeping for that fact, and the argument below never divides by anything.',
 s: ['Suppose F is an antiderivative of f, so F′ = f. That is the only thing assumed, and it is what the symbol ∫ f(u) du means.',
   'Differentiate the composition F(g(x)) with the chain rule: d/dx F(g(x)) = F′(g(x))·g′(x) = f(g(x))·g′(x).',
   'Read that line backwards. A function whose derivative is f(g(x))g′(x) is F(g(x)), so ∫ f(g(x))g′(x) dx = F(g(x)) + C.',
   'Separately, ∫ f(u) du = F(u) + C by the definition of F, and putting u = g(x) into that gives F(g(x)) + C — the very same answer as step three. So the two integrals genuinely agree, and “cancelling the dx” is shorthand for this agreement rather than a claim about arithmetic on dx.',
   'Integration by parts is the identical move applied to the product rule instead: (uv)′ = u′v + uv′, so integrating both sides gives uv = ∫u′v dx + ∫uv′ dx, and rearranging gives ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du.'],
 m: 'Every technique in this lesson is a differentiation rule run in reverse, which is why the catalogue is short and why recognition beats memorisation: a composition with its inner derivative loitering beside it is a chain rule that has left fingerprints. It also explains the LIATE ordering rather than leaving it as a mnemonic — you pick u to be the factor that gets simpler when differentiated, because ∫v du is the integral you are still going to have to do, and the whole point was to trade a hard one for an easy one.'
},

series: {
 q: 'Why does adding infinitely many terms come out to the finite number a/(1 − r)?',
 a: 'You never add infinitely many things. You add n of them, get an exact answer valid for every n, and then watch what that answer does as n grows. For a geometric series the finite answer has a closed form because multiplying the sum by r shifts the whole list along by one place, so subtracting annihilates everything in the middle and leaves two terms standing.',
 s: ['Add only n terms — an ordinary finite sum with nothing infinite in it: Sₙ = a + ar + ar² + ⋯ + arⁿ⁻¹.',
   'Multiply the whole thing by r: rSₙ = ar + ar² + ⋯ + arⁿ. That is the same list shifted one place along, missing a at the front and gaining arⁿ at the back.',
   'Subtract the second from the first. Every middle term appears in both and cancels, leaving Sₙ − rSₙ = a − arⁿ, so Sₙ = a(1 − rⁿ)/(1 − r) — exact, for every n, whenever r ≠ 1.',
   'The sum of the series means the number Sₙ approaches. Only rⁿ depends on n, so everything hangs on that one factor: if |r| < 1, each step multiplies by something smaller than one in size, so rⁿ → 0 and Sₙ → a/(1 − r).',
   'If |r| ≥ 1 then arⁿ does not shrink to zero, so every new term adds at least a fixed amount and the partial sums march off without ever settling. The condition |r| < 1 is not fine print attached to the formula; it is exactly the case in which there is anything for the formula to be equal to.'],
 m: 'Amortised analysis is this argument wearing a hat: a dynamic array that doubles copies 1 + 2 + 4 + ⋯ + n elements, a geometric sum below 2n, so n appends cost O(n) altogether and O(1) each on average despite the occasional expensive copy. The converse warning is worth as much — the harmonic series Σ1/n has terms shrinking to zero and diverges anyway, so “the terms go to zero” is a test a series must pass, never a reason to believe it converges.'
},

taylor: {
 q: 'Where does the n! in the Taylor coefficients come from?',
 a: 'Suppose for a moment that the function can be written as an infinite polynomial. Then its coefficients are not free to choose — you can fish them out one at a time, because differentiating a few times and then setting x = a kills every term but one. The factorial is simply the debris that repeated differentiation of a power piles up out front, and dividing by n! is clearing that debris away.',
 s: ['Assume f can be written as a power series about a, f(x) = c₀ + c₁(x − a) + c₂(x − a)² + c₃(x − a)³ + ⋯, and that it may be differentiated term by term. Whether a particular function admits such a series is a separate question; here we ask only what the coefficients would have to be if it does.',
   'Set x = a. Every term carrying a factor (x − a) vanishes, leaving f(a) = c₀.',
   'Differentiate once: f′(x) = c₁ + 2c₂(x − a) + 3c₃(x − a)² + ⋯. Setting x = a kills all but the first term, so f′(a) = c₁. Differentiating again and repeating gives f″(a) = 2c₂.',
   'Do it n times and follow the single term cₙ(x − a)ⁿ. Each differentiation drops the exponent by one and drags the old exponent out in front, so after n rounds it has become cₙ · n(n−1)(n−2)⋯1 = n!cₙ, a bare constant. Every earlier term has by then been differentiated down to zero, and every later term still carries a factor (x − a), which vanishes at x = a.',
   'So f⁽ⁿ⁾(a) = n!cₙ, which rearranges to cₙ = f⁽ⁿ⁾(a)/n!. The factorial in the Taylor formula is there to undo the factorial that differentiation manufactured.'],
 m: 'This argument pins down the coefficients; it does not prove the series equals f, nor over what range — that is what the remainder term and the convergence tests are for, and the gap is real: the series for 1/(1 − x) reproduces the function only for |x| < 1, and there are smooth functions whose Taylor series converges to something else entirely. What it does explain is why a polynomial can imitate sin x or eˣ at all — it is constructed to match every derivative at a point, so it agrees with the function to every order there. Library implementations start from exactly these coefficients and then re-tune them, trading perfect accuracy at one point for error spread evenly across the whole interval.'
},

applications: {
 q: 'Why does (1/(b − a))∫ₐᵇ f dx deserve to be called an average?',
 a: 'An average is a sum divided by a count, and a function on an interval has no count — it takes a whole continuum of values. So the honest move is to average finitely many samples, where the schoolroom definition applies with nothing to argue about, and then take more and more of them. What falls out is a Riemann sum with the width of the interval divided back out.',
 s: ['The average of n numbers is their sum divided by n. Nothing about a function changes that definition; the only difficulty is deciding which n numbers to average.',
   'Sample f at n equally spaced points x₁, …, xₙ across [a, b], with spacing Δx = (b − a)/n. Their ordinary average is (1/n)(f(x₁) + ⋯ + f(xₙ)).',
   'Rewrite the 1/n as Δx/(b − a). It is the same number, since nΔx = b − a. The average becomes (1/(b − a))·(f(x₁)Δx + ⋯ + f(xₙ)Δx).',
   'The bracketed sum is precisely a Riemann sum for f on [a, b]: rectangles of width Δx and height f(xᵢ). For continuous f it converges to ∫ₐᵇ f dx as n → ∞ and Δx → 0.',
   'So sampling ever more finely drives the ordinary average to (1/(b − a))∫ₐᵇ f dx. The formula is not an analogy to averaging — it is the everyday average of n samples, with n taken to infinity.'],
 m: 'Rearranged as ∫ₐᵇ f dx = f̄·(b − a), it says the area under any curve equals the area of a single rectangle of the average height, which is a quick sanity check on an integral you have just computed. The same slice–approximate–sum move is every other application in this lesson: make each slice something you can already measure — a rectangle of height (top − bottom), a disk of area πf(x)² — then sum and take the limit. It is also why averaging a sampled load curve on a monitoring dashboard approaches the true mean as the sampling rate rises: the dashboard is computing step three, and the integral is what step five says it is heading for.'
},

improper: {
 q: 'Why does ∫₁∞ 1/x² dx have a finite area while ∫₁∞ 1/x dx does not, when both curves fall to zero?',
 a: 'Spreading a shrinking height across an infinite width is a race. The width grows without limit, so the height must fall fast enough to beat it, and “falls to zero” says nothing at all about speed. Computing the area only as far as a movable right edge b turns the race into one expression in b, and the exponent is what decides which side wins.',
 s: ['An infinite upper limit is not a number you can substitute, so ∫₁∞ f dx is defined as lim(b→∞) ∫₁ᵇ f dx: do an ordinary integral out to a finite edge b, then ask whether those answers settle on a number as b runs away.',
   'For f(x) = 1/xᵖ with p ≠ 1, the power rule gives the antiderivative x¹⁻ᵖ/(1 − p), so ∫₁ᵇ x⁻ᵖ dx = (b¹⁻ᵖ − 1)/(1 − p).',
   'Only b¹⁻ᵖ depends on b, so watch that alone. If p > 1 the exponent 1 − p is negative, so b¹⁻ᵖ = 1/bᵖ⁻¹ → 0, and the area settles on (0 − 1)/(1 − p) = 1/(p − 1), a finite number. For p = 2 that is exactly 1.',
   'If p < 1 the exponent 1 − p is positive, so b¹⁻ᵖ → ∞ and the accumulated area grows without bound. The curve is still falling to zero; it simply is not falling fast enough to outrun the width it is being spread over.',
   'The boundary p = 1 has to be checked on its own, because there the antiderivative changes species: ∫₁ᵇ dx/x = ln b, and ln b has no ceiling — it grows ever more slowly and still passes every number eventually. So the tail is finite exactly when p > 1, strictly.'],
 m: 'The same threshold governs the p-series Σ1/nᵖ, and for the reason you would guess: the rectangles of the sum and the area under the curve trap one another between them, so the two stand or fall together. It also has teeth in practice — if request latencies have a density falling off like 1/x², the mean is ∫x·(1/x²) dx = ∫(1/x) dx, which diverges, so the running average of your latency data drifts upward forever instead of converging on a value. A heavy tail is not merely a large number; it is a divergent integral, and no amount of extra data will settle it.'
},

'right-triangles': {
 q: 'Why does the ratio of two sides depend only on the angle, when the triangle itself can be any size at all?',
 a: 'A right triangle with a given acute angle is not one triangle but a whole family, and every member is a photographic enlargement of every other. Enlarging multiplies all three sides by one and the same factor — and a ratio of two sides cannot notice a factor that appears above and below the line, because it cancels. The coordinates below turn that picture into arithmetic.',
 s: ['Put the angle’s vertex at the origin with the adjacent side along the positive x-axis. The other side of the angle is then a ray heading out of the origin, and the angle is nothing more than a name for which ray.',
   'A straight ray out of the origin is exactly the set of points (tx₁, ty₁) for t ≥ 0, where (x₁, y₁) is any one point on it. That is what “straight, through the origin” means: every point on it is a scaled copy of every other.',
   'Take the point Pₜ = (tx₁, ty₁) and drop a perpendicular to the x-axis. The opposite side has length ty₁, the adjacent side tx₁, and the hypotenuse √((tx₁)² + (ty₁)²) = t√(x₁² + y₁²), since t ≥ 0.',
   'So moving out along the ray multiplies all three sides by the same t. Every ratio of two sides therefore cancels it: opposite/hypotenuse = ty₁ / (t√(x₁²+y₁²)) = y₁/√(x₁²+y₁²), with no t left anywhere.',
   'The three ratios depend on the ray alone — that is, on the angle alone — so writing them as sin θ, cos θ and tan θ is legitimate: they are genuinely functions of θ. Choosing the t that makes the hypotenuse 1 puts the point at (cos θ, sin θ), which is the unit circle waiting for the next lesson.'],
 m: 'This is what earns trigonometry the right to exist: without the cancellation there would be no function sin, only a table of particular triangles. It is also exactly why normalising a vector — dividing by its length — throws away nothing but size: the direction, and every angle you can ask about it, survives the division untouched.'
},

'unit-circle': {
 q: 'Why do radians beat degrees, when degrees are the units everyone actually thinks in?',
 a: 'Near zero, three things become indistinguishable: the arc along the circle, the vertical drop sin θ, and the tangent line’s rise tan θ. Radians are the unit that measures the arc directly, so in radians sin θ and θ agree to first order and the sine curve leaves the origin at slope exactly 1. Degrees measure the same arc on a rescaled ruler, and that rescaling factor never goes away. The squeeze below makes the “indistinguishable” precise.',
 s: ['Define the radian by arc length: the angle θ cuts an arc of length θ off the unit circle, so a full turn is 2π because the circumference is 2π. (Circles of different radii about the same vertex are scaled copies, by the previous lesson, so arc/radius is the same number for all of them — the definition is consistent.)',
   'For 0 < θ < π/2 look at three nested regions between the ray at angle 0 and the ray at angle θ: the triangle with vertices (0,0), (1,0), (cos θ, sin θ), which has base 1 and height sin θ; the circular sector; and the triangle (0,0), (1,0), (1, tan θ), base 1 and height tan θ. Each region sits inside the next.',
   'Their areas are ½ sin θ, ½θ (the sector is the fraction θ/2π of a disc of area π), and ½ tan θ. A region contained in another cannot have greater area, so sin θ ≤ θ ≤ tan θ.',
   'Divide throughout by the positive number sin θ: 1 ≤ θ/sin θ ≤ 1/cos θ. As θ → 0 the right-hand bound tends to 1, so θ/sin θ is squeezed to 1 — that is, sin θ/θ → 1, and sin θ = θ to first order near zero.',
   'Only radians give the clean 1. An angle of x degrees is πx/180 radians, so the function “sine of x degrees” is sin(πx/180), whose slope at 0 is π/180 ≈ 0.01745. Measure in degrees and that factor is dragged through every derivative, every series, every integral, forever; radians are precisely the unit chosen to make it disappear, which is why d/dθ sin θ = cos θ comes out with no stray constant.'],
 m: 'This is the argument behind Math.sin taking radians in every standard library, and behind the small-angle approximation sin θ ≈ θ that physics engines and pendulum models lean on — you now know the error is third order, not merely “small”. It is also the single limit that the derivative of sine rests on later, so the calculus track owes this lesson a debt.'
},

identities: {
 q: 'Where does the minus sign in cos(a + b) = cos a cos b − sin a sin b actually come from?',
 a: 'Adding angles is not an algebraic accident; it is what rotation does. So rotate the point that sits at angle a by a further angle b and it must land at angle a + b. Write the starting point in terms of the two grid directions, rotate each of them, add the results back up, and the two coordinates of where you land are the two addition identities. The minus sign arrives from one place only: the rotated vertical direction leans backwards, into negative x.',
 s: ['The point at angle a on the unit circle is P(a) = (cos a, sin a). Split it along the grid directions: P(a) = cos a·(1,0) + sin a·(0,1).',
   'Let R be rotation by b about the origin. R slides every point b of arc along the unit circle, so it carries the point at angle a to the point at angle a + b: R(P(a)) = P(a + b). That is what it means for the angle to measure arc.',
   'R is rigid and fixes the origin, so it carries the parallelogram built on u and v to the parallelogram built on Ru and Rv, and carries the point c of the way along a ray to the point c of the way along the rotated ray. In symbols: R(u + v) = Ru + Rv and R(cu) = cRu.',
   'R(1,0) = (cos b, sin b), by the definition of the angle b. And (0,1) is the unit vector one right angle anticlockwise from (1,0), so R(0,1) is the unit vector one right angle anticlockwise from (cos b, sin b) — namely (−sin b, cos b). (Check the right angle without any trigonometry: both tips are distance 1 from the origin, and the squared distance between them is (cos b + sin b)² + (sin b − cos b)² = 2 = 1² + 1², so the converse of Pythagoras makes the corner at the origin square.)',
   'Now push P(a) through R using step 3: P(a + b) = cos a·(cos b, sin b) + sin a·(−sin b, cos b) = (cos a cos b − sin a sin b, sin a cos b + cos a sin b). Reading off the x-coordinate gives the cosine identity, the y-coordinate the sine identity — and the minus is simply the −sin b in the rotated vertical direction.'],
 m: 'The four numbers cos b, −sin b, sin b, cos b are the 2×2 rotation matrix, and this argument is the proof that multiplying two rotation matrices adds their angles — the fact graphics, robotics and complex-number arithmetic all run on. It also hands you the rest of the identity table free: b = a gives the double-angle formulas, and b = −a (with cos(−a) = cos a, sin(−a) = −sin a) gives cos²a + sin²a = cos 0 = 1, the Pythagorean identity.'
},

waves: {
 q: 'Why does adding two waves of the same frequency always give another wave of that same frequency, just shifted?',
 a: 'A wave at a fixed frequency has only two dials worth of freedom: how tall and how shifted. So the family of all waves at that frequency ought to behave like the plane — two numbers, added the way arrows add. The argument below shows the correspondence exactly: every such wave is a pair (a, b) of coefficients, adding waves adds the pairs, and converting the pair back to amplitude-and-phase is nothing but reading the point (b, a) in polar coordinates.',
 s: ['Any wave at frequency ω can be written a cos(ωt) + b sin(ωt): angle addition expands A sin(ωt + C) into (A sin C)cos ωt + (A cos C)sin ωt, so that two-coefficient form is fully general. Adding two such waves plainly gives a third of the same form, with the coefficients added.',
   'So the only thing to prove is that the form is reversible: given a and b, not both zero, find an amplitude R > 0 and a phase φ with a cos ωt + b sin ωt = R sin(ωt + φ) for every t. Expanding the right side by angle addition gives R sin φ·cos ωt + R cos φ·sin ωt.',
   'Test at two instants to see what R and φ must be. At ωt = 0 the left side is a and the right is R sin φ; at ωt = π/2 the left side is b and the right is R cos φ. So (R cos φ, R sin φ) must be the point (b, a).',
   'Every point of the plane other than the origin has polar coordinates, so such an R and φ exist: R = √(a² + b²), and φ the angle of the point (b, a), which is atan2(a, b). Substituting them back, R sin(ωt + φ) = R cos φ·sin ωt + R sin φ·cos ωt = b sin ωt + a cos ωt for every t — so the two functions agree everywhere, not merely at the two instants tested.',
   'Therefore the sum of any two waves at frequency ω is one wave at frequency ω, with amplitude √(a² + b²) and a phase read off as an angle in the plane. No new frequency can appear, and the amplitude combines by Pythagoras rather than by simple addition — which is why two equal waves half a period apart cancel to nothing.'],
 m: 'This is the licence for phasors: represent a wave by the single point (b, a), or the complex number b + ai, and superposition becomes vector addition. It is also why a linear filter can do only two things to a pure tone — scale it and delay it — which is what makes an audio equaliser describable by one gain and one phase per frequency, and what makes Fourier analysis worth doing at all.'
},

'law-sines': {
 q: 'Why is the correction in the law of cosines exactly −2ab cos C, and not some other function of the angle?',
 a: 'Pythagoras is the distance formula applied to two perpendicular sides. Drop the perpendicularity and nothing else changes: it is still the distance formula, but now squaring the difference along one axis throws off a cross-term that no longer vanishes. That cross-term is the whole correction, and computing it takes one line of algebra plus sin² + cos² = 1.',
 s: ['Place the vertex carrying angle C at the origin with side b along the positive x-axis; you may do this because sliding and turning the picture changes no length and no angle. Then the far end of side b is the vertex A = (b, 0).',
   'Vertex B lies at distance a from the origin in the direction C, so by the unit-circle definition of cosine and sine its coordinates are B = (a cos C, a sin C).',
   'Side c is the distance from A to B, so by the distance formula c² = (a cos C − b)² + (a sin C − 0)².',
   'Expand the first bracket: a²cos²C − 2ab cos C + b², and add a²sin²C. Grouping gives c² = a²(cos²C + sin²C) + b² − 2ab cos C.',
   'Since cos²C + sin²C = 1, this is c² = a² + b² − 2ab cos C. The correction is exactly the cross-term thrown off by squaring the difference along the x-axis — so it is −2ab cos C for the same reason (p − q)² carries a −2pq, and it vanishes precisely when cos C = 0, which is to say when C is a right angle and Pythagoras returns.'],
 m: 'That cross-term is the dot product in disguise: the identity is c² = |A|² + |B|² − 2(A·B), and comparing the two forms is precisely how the vectors track later earns u·v = ‖u‖‖v‖cos θ. The same coordinate picture also settles the law of sines in one line — the triangle’s height above side b is a sin C, so its area is ½ab sin C, and by symmetry also ½bc sin A and ½ca sin B; divide all three by ½abc and you get sin A/a = sin B/b = sin C/c.'
},

'inverse-trig': {
 q: 'Why can’t a single arctangent of y/x recover the angle, when tan θ is exactly y/x?',
 a: 'The trouble is not with arctangent but with the ratio it is handed. Forming y/x collapses a whole line through the origin — both halves of it — into one number, and two opposite directions arrive indistinguishable. No function can undo a step that has already destroyed the information, and the proof of that is short enough to be worth doing properly.',
 s: ['The task is: given a nonzero vector (x, y), recover the angle θ in (−π, π] that it points along. The pair (x, y) genuinely determines θ, so the task is possible in principle.',
   'Now suppose some function f of the single number r = y/x returned the correct angle for every vector. Feed it (1, 1), which points at π/4, and feed it (−1, −1), which points at −3π/4 — the exact opposite direction.',
   'Both inputs give r = 1. So f(1) would have to equal π/4 and −3π/4 at once, which no function does. Therefore no single-argument recipe applied to y/x can be correct for all vectors — the impossibility is about the ratio, not about which formula you choose.',
   'tan⁻¹ copes by settling for correctness on half the plane: its output is restricted to (−π/2, π/2), so it always returns the answer in the right-hand half, x > 0. Arcsine makes the same compromise for the same reason — a function can be inverted only on a stretch where it takes each value once, and sine and tangent repeat forever, so every inverse trig function ships with a declared range.',
   'atan2(y, x) takes the two numbers separately and never forms the lossy ratio, so the signs survive: it returns tan⁻¹(y/x) when x > 0, that value plus π when x < 0 and y ≥ 0, minus π when x < 0 and y < 0, and ±π/2 when x = 0 from the sign of y. That covers the full (−π, π], which is what the pair (x, y) always determined.'],
 m: 'This is why sin⁻¹(sin θ) need not be θ, and why every library’s inverse trig documentation states a range rather than leaving it to taste: the range is the branch that was chosen to make the inverse a function at all. Practically, it is the reason to write atan2(y, x) and never atan(y/x) in graphics, robotics or anything that turns a vector into a heading — the second form silently points half your objects backwards, and additionally divides by zero on the vertical axis.'
},

logic: {
 q: 'Why does “if p then q” count as true whenever p is false?',
 a: 'An implication is a promise, and a promise is broken in exactly one situation: the condition came about and the thing promised did not. Every other row of the table leaves the promise intact — including the rows where the condition never arose and the promise was never put to the test. Pinning down that single false row forces the whole truth table, and everything else about implication falls out of it.',
 s: ['Two compound statements are equivalent when they agree on every combination of truth values of their parts. For a connective, the truth table is the whole story — there is nothing else to know about it.',
   'Ask what the rule “every multiple of 4 is even” should say about 7. It says nothing about 7: seven is not a case the rule speaks about, and it would be perverse for an irrelevant number to falsify a true rule. So when the premise fails, the implication cannot be counted false, which leaves only true.',
   'That leaves exactly one way for p → q to fail: p true and q false. In symbols, p → q ≡ ¬(p ∧ ¬q).',
   'Apply De Morgan to the right-hand side: ¬(p ∧ ¬q) ≡ ¬p ∨ ¬(¬q) ≡ ¬p ∨ q. The implication has been rewritten with no arrow left in it.',
   'Now read ¬p ∨ q the other way round: it is ¬(¬q) ∨ ¬p, which is (¬q) → (¬p) — the contrapositive, equivalent because it is literally the same expression. The converse q → p unpacks to ¬q ∨ p instead, which differs in the row p false, q true, and that one row is the entire reason converse and original are not interchangeable.'],
 m: 'Once an implication is just ¬p ∨ q, conditions become things you can manipulate rather than memorise: the guard if (!ready || done) is an implication, negating a condition is De Morgan, and swapping a test for its contrapositive is free and safe. Mistaking an implication for its converse is the same error as concluding from a passing check that its cause must have happened.'
},

sets: {
 q: 'Why does a set with n elements have exactly 2ⁿ subsets?',
 a: 'A set records only membership, so choosing a subset is walking down the list of elements and answering one question at each: in, or out. The answers are independent and there are n of them, so counting subsets is counting answer sheets — and each answer sheet is a string of bits.',
 s: ['A subset of A is completely determined by one decision per element: in, or out. Two subsets that answer every one of those questions the same way contain the same elements, so they are the same subset.',
   'Fix an order for the elements once, and record the answers as a string of n bits: 1 for in, 0 for out, one position per element.',
   'The pairing loses nothing in either direction. Different subsets give different strings, because if two subsets differ then some element is in one and not the other, and that position’s bit differs. Different strings give different subsets, by reading the bits back. So subsets and n-bit strings correspond exactly, one to one.',
   'Counting the strings is the product rule: n positions, 2 independent choices at each, giving 2 × 2 × ⋯ × 2 = 2ⁿ.',
   'Check the extremes to see that nothing is missed or double-counted: all zeros is the empty set, all ones is A, and n = 0 gives 2⁰ = 1, the empty set being the only subset of the empty set. Hence |P(A)| = 2ⁿ.'],
 m: 'The correspondence is not merely a counting trick — it is how subsets are enumerated in code. Looping i from 0 to 2ⁿ − 1 and reading the bits of i visits every subset exactly once, and that same 2ⁿ is why brute force over subsets is instant at n = 20 and hopeless at n = 60.'
},

proofs: {
 q: 'Why does assuming the opposite and reaching nonsense count as a proof?',
 a: 'A statement and its negation cannot both hold, so ruling out the negation is as good as establishing the statement. Correct reasoning cannot manufacture a falsehood out of truths — so when a chain of correct steps starting from “not P” ends somewhere plainly false, the fault has to lie in the one thing that was assumed rather than known.',
 s: ['Every step of a valid argument preserves truth: from true premises you can never correctly derive a false conclusion. Turn that around and it says that an argument ending in something plainly false must have had at least one false premise.',
   'Proof by contradiction engineers exactly that situation. You add ¬P to premises you already accept, reason correctly, and arrive at a falsehood. Since the accepted premises are true, the false premise must be ¬P — so P is true.',
   'Here is the machine running. Suppose √2 were rational: √2 = a/b with a and b integers, b ≠ 0, sharing no common factor, which any fraction can be reduced to. Squaring and clearing the denominator gives a² = 2b², so a² is even.',
   'A small lemma, proved by contrapositive: if a were odd, say a = 2k + 1, then a² = 4k² + 4k + 1, which is odd. So a² even forces a even, and we may write a = 2c. Substituting, 4c² = 2b², so b² = 2c², and the same lemma makes b even too.',
   'Now a and b are both even, contradicting the fact that they share no common factor. Every step after the opening assumption was forced, so the opening assumption is what is false: √2 is not a ratio of integers.'],
 m: 'Contradiction is the standard tool for claims that nothing of a certain kind exists — no algorithm beats this bound, no schedule satisfies these constraints — because it lets you reason about an object you can never exhibit. The cheap direction is worth holding onto too: a single counterexample refutes a universal claim outright, which is exactly what one failing test does to a specification, and exactly why passing tests cannot return the favour.'
},

induction: {
 q: 'Why is the domino argument a proof and not merely a persuasive picture?',
 a: 'The picture says every domino falls; the proof says no domino could be the first one left standing. If the claim failed anywhere there would have to be an earliest place it failed, and the two ingredients of induction between them forbid an earliest failure — the base case rules out the first position, and the inductive step rules out every other one.',
 s: ['Suppose you have the two ingredients: P(1) is true, and for every k ≥ 1, P(k) being true forces P(k+1) to be true. The claim is that P(n) then holds for every n ≥ 1.',
   'Accept one fact about the positive integers, the fact that distinguishes them from the reals: any non-empty collection of positive integers has a least member. You cannot descend through them forever.',
   'Suppose the claim failed. Then the collection of counterexamples — those n for which P(n) is false — is non-empty, so it has a least member. Call it m.',
   'm cannot be 1, because P(1) is true. So m ≥ 2, which makes m − 1 a positive integer smaller than m; being smaller than the least counterexample, m − 1 is not a counterexample, so P(m − 1) is true.',
   'Apply the inductive step at k = m − 1: it forces P(m) to be true. But m was chosen as a case where P fails. The contradiction destroys the supposition, so there are no counterexamples at all and P(n) holds for every n ≥ 1.'],
 m: 'This is why “assume it already works for smaller inputs” is not circular when you argue a recursive function correct — you are not assuming your conclusion, you are ruling out a smallest failing input. The base case is the recursion’s stopping condition, the inductive step is the recursive call, and strong induction, where you assume all of P(1) … P(m−1) at once, costs nothing extra: choosing m least already handed you every earlier case.'
},

quantifiers: {
 q: 'Why is the negation of “every element is positive” one bad element, rather than every element being bad?',
 a: 'A universal claim is a promise about a whole collection, and such a promise is broken the moment a single member breaks it — the rest are under no obligation to join in. Spelled out over a finite domain the for-all is one long chain of ANDs, and the negation rule is De Morgan’s law walked down that chain.',
 s: ['∀x P(x) asserts that P holds at every x of the domain. For that claim to be false is simply for the assertion to fail, and it fails as soon as one x has ¬P(x).',
   'Check both directions. If ∀x P(x) is false there must be at least one offending x, since if no x offended, P would hold everywhere and the claim would be true. Conversely, one x with ¬P(x) makes the claim false. So ¬∀x P(x) ≡ ∃x ¬P(x).',
   'The same reading settles the other rule: ¬∃x P(x) says that no x has P, which is precisely the statement that every x has ¬P — that is, ∀x ¬P(x).',
   'Over a finite domain {x₁, …, xₙ}, ∀x P(x) is P(x₁) ∧ ⋯ ∧ P(xₙ) and ∃x P(x) is P(x₁) ∨ ⋯ ∨ P(xₙ), so the two rules are De Morgan’s laws applied n times over. They hold on the empty domain too, where an empty AND is true and an empty OR false: “every element of the empty array is positive” is true, and “some element is not” is false, exactly as the rules require.',
   'Negation therefore walks inwards, flipping each quantifier it passes and negating the predicate at the end: ¬∀x ∃y P(x,y) ≡ ∃x ¬∃y P(x,y) ≡ ∃x ∀y ¬P(x,y).'],
 m: 'A failure condition is a negated specification, and getting the negation wrong is how an assertion ends up testing something nobody meant: the opposite of “all requests succeeded” is “one failed”, not “all failed”. The rule also explains the asymmetry of testing — a run can refute a for-all with a single case, and can never establish one.'
},

boolean: {
 q: 'Why can any behaviour you can write as a truth table be built out of AND, OR and NOT gates?',
 a: 'A truth table is just a list of the input rows on which the output is supposed to be 1. So if you can build one gate arrangement that fires on exactly one chosen row and stays silent on all the others, you can OR one of those together for each row on your list, and the result fires on exactly the rows listed and nowhere else.',
 s: ['A Boolean function of n inputs is nothing more than its table: 2ⁿ rows, each stamped 0 or 1. Two expressions agreeing on all 2ⁿ rows are the same function, so matching the table is the whole job.',
   'Fix one row and build its minterm: AND together each variable that is 1 in that row, and the complement of each variable that is 0. For the row x = 1, y = 0, z = 1 that is x·ȳ·z.',
   'A minterm outputs 1 on exactly its own row. An AND demands that every factor be 1, and by construction each factor is 1 precisely when that input matches the row, so changing any single input drops some factor to 0.',
   'Now OR together the minterms of every row stamped 1. Feed in any input at all: at most one minterm can be 1, namely the one built for that input’s row, so the OR is 1 exactly when that row was stamped 1 and 0 otherwise. The expression agrees with the table on all 2ⁿ rows, so it is the function. If no row is stamped 1 the function is the constant 0, an empty OR.',
   'So AND, OR and NOT reach every Boolean function whatsoever. De Morgan then shrinks the toolkit to one gate: x̄ = x NAND x, x·y = (x NAND y) NAND (x NAND y), and x + y = (x NAND x) NAND (y NAND y), so NAND alone builds anything a table can describe.'],
 m: 'This is why a chip can be fabricated from a single kind of gate, and why an FPGA implements arbitrary logic out of small lookup tables — the table is the specification and the circuit is a mechanical translation of it. It also fixes what minimisation is: absorption and Karnaugh maps hunt for a cheaper expression with the same table, so a rule like x + xy = x is safe precisely because it changes no row, never because it changes what the circuit means.'
},

relations: {
 q: 'Why does an equivalence relation chop a set into clean, non-overlapping groups?',
 a: 'Group each element with everything it relates to. Reflexivity puts every element inside its own group, so nothing is left out; symmetry and transitivity together mean that two groups sharing even one member must share all of them. Covering everything, with no partial overlaps allowed, is exactly what being a partition means.',
 s: ['Let R be reflexive, symmetric and transitive on a set S, and for each a define its class [a] = {x ∈ S : a R x} — everything that a relates to.',
   'Reflexivity gives a R a, so a ∈ [a]. Every element lies in at least one class, so the classes between them cover S.',
   'Now suppose two classes overlap: some c lies in both [a] and [b], meaning a R c and b R c. Symmetry turns the second into c R b, and transitivity chains a R c with c R b into a R b.',
   'Take any x ∈ [b], so b R x. Combined with a R b, transitivity gives a R x, so x ∈ [a] — that is, [b] ⊆ [a]. Symmetry gives b R a, and running the same argument with a and b swapped gives [a] ⊆ [b]. So the two classes are equal.',
   'Any two classes therefore either share nothing or coincide, and together they cover S: that is a partition. Each property earned its keep, and dropping one breaks it — “differs by less than 1” on the numbers is reflexive and symmetric but not transitive, and its classes overlap without being equal, since 0.5 relates to both 0 and 1.2 while those two do not relate to each other.'],
 m: 'Equality in a program is meant to be an equivalence relation, and this theorem is why it matters: hash sets, deduplication, union–find components and the residue classes mod n all depend on “same group” being well defined. An equals method that is not transitive destroys the partition, and the symptom is a container holding two elements it insists are equal.'
},

recurrence: {
 q: 'Why does guessing aₙ = rⁿ and solving a quadratic give every solution of a two-term recurrence?',
 a: 'Two facts carry the method. Geometric sequences are the ones on which the recurrence collapses to a single equation about r, because every term carries the same factor rⁿ⁻² and it divides straight out. And the recurrence is deterministic, so a solution is completely pinned down by its first two values — which means matching two numbers is enough to match the whole sequence.',
 s: ['Take aₙ = c₁aₙ₋₁ + c₂aₙ₋₂ and try aₙ = rⁿ with r ≠ 0. Substituting gives rⁿ = c₁rⁿ⁻¹ + c₂rⁿ⁻²; divide through by rⁿ⁻² and every n vanishes, leaving r² = c₁r + c₂. A geometric sequence solves the recurrence exactly when r is a root of that quadratic.',
   'The recurrence is linear, so combinations survive it. If sₙ and tₙ each satisfy it, then c₁(Asₙ₋₁ + Btₙ₋₁) + c₂(Asₙ₋₂ + Btₙ₋₂) regroups as A(c₁sₙ₋₁ + c₂sₙ₋₂) + B(c₁tₙ₋₁ + c₂tₙ₋₂) = Asₙ + Btₙ. So with distinct roots r₁ and r₂, every A r₁ⁿ + B r₂ⁿ is a solution.',
   'Why there is nothing else: the recurrence computes each term from the two before it, so once a₀ and a₁ are fixed every later term is forced. Two solutions that agree at n = 0 and n = 1 agree at every n.',
   'So it suffices to hit the starting values. That means solving A + B = a₀ and A r₁ + B r₂ = a₁ — two linear equations in A and B whose determinant is r₂ − r₁, non-zero precisely because the roots are distinct, so exactly one pair (A, B) works.',
   'That pair’s combination agrees with the true sequence at n = 0 and n = 1, and by step 3 it therefore agrees forever. The closed form is not a lucky guess but the unique solution — and when the two roots coincide, that 2×2 system becomes singular, which is the honest reason the repeated-root case needs a second solution of the form n rⁿ.'],
 m: 'Fibonacci’s characteristic equation is r² = r + 1, whose larger root is the golden ratio φ ≈ 1.618, so Fₙ grows like φⁿ and the naive recursive implementation makes about that many calls before returning. In general the root largest in size dictates the growth rate, which is how a recurrence for an algorithm’s cost turns into the exponential base you actually care about.'
},

'advanced-counting': {
 q: 'Why does pigeonhole guarantee a crowded box rather than merely making one likely?',
 a: 'Suppose every box stayed under the claimed load. Adding the boxes up then gives a ceiling on how many items can exist at all — and that ceiling comes out below the number of items you are holding. No assumption about how the items were placed is used anywhere; the arithmetic alone forbids it, which is why the conclusion is a guarantee and not a probability.',
 s: ['The claim: put n items into k boxes, and some box holds at least ⌈n/k⌉ of them, where ⌈·⌉ rounds up to the next whole number.',
   'Suppose otherwise — that every box holds at most ⌈n/k⌉ − 1 items.',
   'Then the total across all k boxes is at most k(⌈n/k⌉ − 1). Rounding up raises a number by less than a whole unit, so ⌈n/k⌉ < n/k + 1, which rearranges to ⌈n/k⌉ − 1 < n/k.',
   'Multiply that by k: the total is strictly less than k · (n/k) = n. But the total is exactly n, and n < n is impossible, so the supposition fails.',
   'Hence some box holds at least ⌈n/k⌉ items. The plain version is the case n > k, where ⌈n/k⌉ ≥ 2: more items than boxes forces a repeat. And notice what is not claimed — not which box, not that the loads are anywhere near even, and nothing at all about how the items were distributed.'],
 m: 'A thousand keys into a hundred buckets forces some bucket to hold ten, however clever the hash function is: collisions are arithmetic, not bad luck. The same counting kills the dream of a lossless compressor that shrinks every input — there are 2ⁿ inputs of n bits but fewer than 2ⁿ strings shorter than n, so two inputs must share an encoding and at least one of them can never be recovered.'
},

automata: {
 q: 'Why can no finite state machine — and so no true regular expression — match balanced parentheses?',
 a: 'A finite machine remembers only which state it is in, so its entire memory is a choice among finitely many labels. Feed it more distinct depths than it has states and two different depths must leave it in the same state — and from that moment it cannot tell them apart, so it will demand the same closing brackets from both.',
 s: ['Suppose some machine with m states accepts exactly the balanced strings. Feed it the m + 1 strings “(”, “((”, and so on up to m + 1 opening brackets.',
   'Each of those leaves the machine in some state. There are m + 1 strings and only m states, so by pigeonhole two different ones — (ⁱ and (ʲ with i < j — end in the same state.',
   'Everything the machine does from then on depends only on the state it is currently in; that is what “finite state” means, since the input read so far is stored nowhere else. So for any continuation w, the machine accepts (ⁱw exactly when it accepts (ʲw.',
   'Choose w to be i closing brackets. Then (ⁱ)ⁱ is balanced and so is accepted, which forces (ʲ)ⁱ to be accepted too. But that string has j > i openers against only i closers, so it is not balanced — and the machine was supposed to accept exactly the balanced strings.',
   'The contradiction rules the machine out. The obstruction is memory rather than cleverness: recognising balance means distinguishing unboundedly many depths, and m states can distinguish at most m things. Give the machine a stack and depth becomes countable, which is precisely the step up from a regular language to a context-free one.'],
 m: 'This is the exact reason you cannot parse HTML, JSON or nested expressions with a regular expression, and why a compiler splits into a lexer — a finite state machine over tokens — and a parser, a stack machine over structure. Engines that advertise backreferences and recursion have left the regular languages behind to do it, and the backtracking blow-ups they are prone to are the receipt.'
},

limits: {
 q: 'Why is it legal to cancel (x − 3) out of (x² − 9)/(x − 3), when at x = 3 that factor is zero and cancelling it looks like dividing by zero?',
 a: 'A limit is a claim about the neighbourhood of a point with the point itself cut out. The definition deliberately refuses to look at x = a, so what the function does there — a value, a hole, nothing at all — cannot affect the answer. Two functions agreeing at every point except a are therefore interchangeable as far as the limit at a is concerned, and cancelling a factor changes the function at exactly that one excluded point. The definition below makes the refusal explicit.',
 s: ['Write the definition out fully: lim(x→a) f(x) = L means that for every ε > 0 there is a δ > 0 such that every x satisfying 0 < |x − a| < δ also satisfies |f(x) − L| < ε. The clause 0 < |x − a| is the whole point — it shuts x = a out, so f(a) is never consulted and need not even exist.',
   'An immediate consequence: suppose f and g agree at every x ≠ a, and differ at a or are undefined there. Then any pair (ε, δ) that passes the test for f passes it word for word for g, because the test only ever inspects points with x ≠ a. So the two have the same limit at a, or neither has one.',
   'Now the example. For every x ≠ 3, (x² − 9)/(x − 3) = (x − 3)(x + 3)/(x − 3) = x + 3, and that cancellation is ordinary arithmetic, since x − 3 is a non-zero number at every x it was applied to. The two expressions differ only at x = 3, where the first is undefined and the second is 6 — precisely the excluded point.',
   'So by step two they share a limit at 3, and the limit of x + 3 is easy: given any ε, take δ = ε; then 0 < |x − 3| < δ gives |(x + 3) − 6| = |x − 3| < ε. Hence lim(x→3) (x² − 9)/(x − 3) = 6, established without dividing by zero anywhere.',
   'Continuity is the strictly stronger demand that the limit and the value agree: lim(x→a) f(x) = f(a), which quietly requires three things — that the limit exists, that f(a) exists, and that they match. The cancelled function x + 3 satisfies all three at 3; the original fails the second. Defining the value at 3 to be 6 repairs it, and it is the only value that can, since the limit is 6 and nothing else. That is exactly what a removable discontinuity is.'],
 m: 'Once you see that the definition inspects a punctured neighbourhood, 0/0 stops being an answer and becomes a signal: the formula and the function it describes disagree at one point, and the limit is asking about the function. The quantifier shape is worth keeping too, because Big-O is the same sentence aimed at infinity — “there is a constant c and a threshold n₀ such that for every n ≥ n₀ …” discards every finite stretch of input the way “0 < |x − a| < δ” discards the point itself. Both are built so that a claim about eventual behaviour is unmoved by any amount of local mess, which is why 3n² + 5n and 3n² are the same to Big-O for the same structural reason that a hole is invisible to a limit.'
},

derivatives: {
 q: 'Why does differentiating xⁿ drop the n out in front and knock the power down by one?',
 a: 'Expand (x + h)ⁿ and watch what survives. The term with no h in it is xⁿ, and the subtraction in the difference quotient removes it exactly. There are then n terms of the form xⁿ⁻¹h — one for each bracket that could have contributed the single h — and after dividing by h those leave n xⁿ⁻¹ standing with no h attached, while every other term still carries at least one h and is extinguished as h shrinks. The n out front is a count of brackets, and the reduced power is the brackets that gave an x.',
 s: ['Start from the definition: f′(x) = lim(h→0) [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h. This is the slope of a chord across a run of width h, and the derivative is the number those chord slopes approach as the run shrinks.',
   'Put f(x) = xⁿ and expand with the binomial theorem: (x + h)ⁿ = xⁿ + n xⁿ⁻¹h + C(n,2) xⁿ⁻²h² + ⋯ + hⁿ. The coefficient n on the second term is C(n,1), the number of ways one bracket out of n could have supplied the h while the other n − 1 supplied an x. Take n ≥ 2 from here on; n = 1 is the line f(x) = x, whose chord slope is exactly 1 = 1·x⁰ for every h, which is the rule already.',
   'Subtract f(x) = xⁿ. The leading term cancels exactly, leaving (x + h)ⁿ − xⁿ = n xⁿ⁻¹h + C(n,2) xⁿ⁻²h² + ⋯ + hⁿ, in which every surviving term carries at least one factor of h.',
   'Divide by h — permitted, because the definition only ever uses h ≠ 0 — to get n xⁿ⁻¹ + h·[C(n,2) xⁿ⁻² + C(n,3) xⁿ⁻³h + ⋯ + hⁿ⁻²]. The first term contains no h at all; everything else has been collected into h times a bracket.',
   'Let h → 0 with x held fixed. The bracket is a polynomial in h with fixed coefficients, so it settles on the finite number C(n,2) xⁿ⁻², and h times a finite number goes to zero. What is left is f′(x) = n xⁿ⁻¹, with the n earned as a count and the exponent n − 1 as the powers of x that came along with it.'],
 m: 'The rule stops being a symbol-shuffling instruction and becomes a limit of chord slopes, which is what you are actually computing when you estimate a gradient numerically: a finite-difference derivative is [f(x + h) − f(x)]/h with h stopped short of zero, and the error you are left with is exactly the piece discarded in step five — h multiplied by a bracket that settles on C(n,2) xⁿ⁻², so an error proportional to h. Halving h halves the error, which is what “first-order accurate” means, and it also warns you off shrinking h too far, since the numerator is a subtraction of two nearly equal floating-point numbers and loses significant digits precisely as h gets small. One honest limit on the scope: the binomial theorem used here needs n to be a whole number, so this argument establishes the power rule for whole-number powers only. The same formula does hold for negative and fractional n, but each is earned separately — x⁻ᵐ = 1/xᵐ through the quotient rule, which calls on the power rule only for the positive exponent m and so does not lean on the case it is being used to prove; fractional powers through implicit differentiation.'
},

'chain-rule': {
 q: 'Why does the chain rule multiply the two derivatives, rather than combining them some other way?',
 a: 'A derivative is a scaling factor: near a point, the function takes a small nudge and hands back that nudge multiplied by a fixed number, plus an error too small to matter. Send a nudge through two functions in a row and it is scaled twice — by the inner factor, then by the outer one — and two successive magnifications multiply, exactly as two lenses or two gears do. The algebra below is that sentence with the two error terms tracked rather than waved away.',
 s: ['Restate the derivative as a scaling statement. Assume g is differentiable at x; saying g′(x) = A is the same as writing g(x + h) = g(x) + Ah + E(h), where the leftover E(h) = g(x + h) − g(x) − Ah satisfies E(h)/h → 0 as h → 0. That is just the definition rearranged: [g(x + h) − g(x)]/h → A says exactly that [g(x + h) − g(x) − Ah]/h → 0. Near x, g is “multiply the nudge by A”, plus an error that is small even compared with the nudge.',
   'Write the same thing for f at the point u = g(x), assuming f differentiable there, with B = f′(u): f(u + k) = f(u) + Bk + F(k), where F(k)/k → 0 as k → 0, and where putting k = 0 in the defining formula gives F(0) = 0. Give that error a tidier name: let φ(k) = F(k)/k for k ≠ 0 and φ(0) = 0. Then F(k) = φ(k)·k for every k, the k = 0 case reading 0 = 0, and φ(k) → 0 = φ(0) as k → 0 — which says precisely that φ is continuous at 0.',
   'Now push a nudge h through the composition. The inner function moves its output by k = g(x + h) − g(x) = Ah + E(h), and the outer function is handed precisely that k. So f(g(x + h)) = f(u + k) = f(u) + Bk + φ(k)·k = f(g(x)) + B·(Ah + E(h)) + φ(k)·k.',
   'Divide by h, which the definition allows because h is never 0: [f(g(x + h)) − f(g(x))]/h = BA + B·E(h)/h + φ(k)·(k/h). The first piece is already the answer we want; the remaining job is to show the other two vanish.',
   'The middle term goes to zero because E(h)/h → 0 and B is a fixed number. For the last, k/h = A + E(h)/h → A, so that factor stays bounded; and k → 0 as h → 0, because a function differentiable at x is continuous there. Since φ is continuous at 0 with φ(0) = 0, feeding it a quantity heading to 0 gives φ(k) → 0, so φ(k)·(k/h) → 0·A = 0. Nothing was ever divided by k, so it makes no difference whether the inner nudge happens to be zero. The difference quotient therefore tends to BA = f′(g(x))·g′(x) — the derivatives multiply because each function contributes one factor by which the nudge is scaled.'],
 m: 'This is the whole of backpropagation. A gradient in a deep network is the derivative of a composition of many layers, so by this argument it is a long product of local derivatives, one per layer, computed at the values that layer actually saw on the forward pass — which is why the forward activations must be kept in memory to differentiate at all. It also explains the failure mode honestly: a product of many factors each a little below 1 in size shrinks geometrically and the gradient reaching the early layers vanishes, while factors above 1 explode it. Residual connections, careful initialisation and gradient clipping are all attempts to keep the factors in this product near 1, and they are aimed at a product because step five says a product is what it is.'
},

optimization: {
 q: 'Why must the slope be exactly zero at a maximum — and why does a zero slope not guarantee one?',
 a: 'At an interior high point there is no profitable direction left. If the slope were positive you could step a little to the right and rise; if negative, step left and rise; either way the point was not a maximum after all. So the slope must be a number that offers no direction of profit, and only zero qualifies. Taking the difference quotient from each side separately turns that into two inequalities pointing opposite ways, which pin the derivative to zero — and notice that the argument only ever forces zero, never delivers a maximum, which is exactly the gap the second derivative later fills.',
 s: ['Suppose f has a local maximum at an interior point c and that f′(c) exists. Being a local maximum means f(c + h) ≤ f(c) for every h small enough in size, so the numerator f(c + h) − f(c) is ≤ 0 for all small h, whichever sign h has.',
   'Take h > 0. A number ≤ 0 divided by a positive number is ≤ 0, so every one of those difference quotients is ≤ 0, and they approach f′(c) because that derivative was assumed to exist. A limit of quantities that are all ≤ 0 cannot be strictly positive: were the limit some L > 0, the definition with ε = L would force every quotient close to c to lie within L of L and so be strictly positive, which none of them is. Hence f′(c) ≤ 0.',
   'Take h < 0. The same non-positive numerator is now divided by a negative number, so every quotient is ≥ 0, and the mirror-image argument gives f′(c) ≥ 0.',
   'We assumed f′(c) exists, which means both one-sided limits are that one number. A number that is both ≤ 0 and ≥ 0 is 0, so f′(c) = 0. Reversing every inequality gives the identical conclusion at a local minimum.',
   'Be precise about what was proved. Being an extremum forced the slope to be zero; nothing here runs the other way, and f(x) = x³ has f′(0) = 0 while climbing straight through the origin without any extremum. So f′(c) = 0 nominates candidates and settles nothing. Two hypotheses did real work and each can fail: at an endpoint only one side of c exists, so step 2 or step 3 is simply unavailable and the extremum can sit at a non-zero slope, as f(x) = x does on [0, 1]; and where f′(c) does not exist, as with |x| at 0, step 4 has nothing to combine, though the minimum is perfectly real. That is why the candidate list has three entries — f′ = 0, f′ undefined, and the endpoints — rather than one.'],
 m: 'This theorem is the licence for the entire method: it says that hunting for a zero derivative is the right thing to do, because every interior extremum is guaranteed to be sitting at one and none can hide elsewhere. It equally licenses gradient descent, which stops when the gradient is zero — and it tells you plainly what stopping there does and does not mean, since step five is the reason a training run can settle at a saddle point that is neither a minimum nor anything you wanted. The missing half is supplied by the second derivative: f″(c) > 0 makes the quotient [f′(c + h) − f′(c)]/h positive for all small h ≠ 0, because a limit that is positive keeps the quantity positive nearby, so with f′(c) = 0 the slope f′(c + h) carries the same sign as h — negative just left of c, positive just right. Turning that pointwise sign into “the function falls into c and rises out of it” takes one further theorem, the mean value theorem of the next lesson, and with it the point is a genuine minimum. That is why the test looks at curvature rather than at more slopes.'
},

'product-quotient': {
 q: 'Why is the derivative of a product not simply the product of the derivatives?',
 a: 'Picture fg as the area of a rectangle of width f and height g. Advance x a little and both sides grow, so the area gains a strip along the top, a strip along the side, and a small corner square where the two growths overlap. The two strips are the two terms of the rule — each is a full-sized side multiplied by the other side’s growth. The corner is a growth times a growth, which is negligible beside them once the growth is small. Multiplying the derivatives keeps only the corner and throws away the strips, which is why it is not merely inaccurate but wrong.',
 s: ['Assume f and g are both differentiable at x, and start from the definition: (fg)′(x) = lim(h→0) [f(x + h)g(x + h) − f(x)g(x)]/h. The obstacle is that both factors changed at once, while a difference quotient can only speak about one function changing at a time.',
   'Separate the two changes by adding and subtracting the mixed term f(x + h)g(x), which adds zero and so changes nothing: f(x + h)g(x + h) − f(x)g(x) = f(x + h)[g(x + h) − g(x)] + g(x)[f(x + h) − f(x)]. The first piece is g changing with f held fixed, the second is f changing with g held fixed.',
   'Divide by h: the quotient becomes f(x + h)·[g(x + h) − g(x)]/h + g(x)·[f(x + h) − f(x)]/h. Each bracket is now an ordinary difference quotient of a single function, which is something we already know how to take a limit of.',
   'Let h → 0. The two bracketed quotients tend to g′(x) and f′(x) by definition. The stray factor f(x + h) tends to f(x), because a function with a derivative at x is continuous there: f(x + h) − f(x) = h·[f(x + h) − f(x)]/h → 0·f′(x) = 0. Hence (fg)′ = f g′ + g f′, the two strips and nothing else.',
   'The corner square is hiding in step two: the first term was built with the new width f(x + h), not the old f(x), and the gap between those two is exactly the corner. It rides along through step three and evaporates in step four — the algebra’s way of saying that a growth times a growth is negligible beside a growth times a full-sized factor. Check the result somewhere the answer is already known: f = g = x gives 1·x + x·1 = 2x, which is indeed (x²)′, whereas multiplying the derivatives gives the constant 1 — a different function altogether, which happens to agree with 2x at the single point x = ½ and is wrong at every other point on the line.'],
 m: 'The quotient rule then costs nothing new, once one small fact is in hand: for u ≠ 0 the reciprocal has derivative −1/u², straight from the definition, since [1/(u + k) − 1/u]/k = −1/(u(u + k)) → −1/u² as k → 0. At a point where g(x) ≠ 0, write f/g as f·(1/g); the chain rule applied to the reciprocal gives (1/g)′ = −g′/g², and the product rule assembles f′·(1/g) + f·(−g′/g²) = (f′g − fg′)/g². So the awkward-looking formula is two rules you already have, and no step of it leans on the power rule for negative exponents, which is the thing the quotient rule is later used to establish. More broadly, this is what automatic differentiation implements: a machine that carries a value and its derivative side by side needs one such local rule per operation, and the product rule is the one for multiplication. Forward-mode systems encode the argument literally as dual numbers a + bε with ε² = 0, since (a + bε)(c + dε) = ac + (ad + bc)ε — the discarded ε² term is precisely the corner square, and the rule that kills it is step four written as an algebraic axiom.'
},

'curve-sketching': {
 q: 'Why does knowing the slope at each separate point tell you the function rises across a whole interval?',
 a: 'The derivative is a statement at a single point, while “increasing on [a, b]” is a statement about pairs of points that can be far apart, and no amount of pointwise information joins up on its own. The bridge is the mean value theorem: across any stretch, the average slope of the chord is actually attained as an instantaneous slope somewhere inside. Grant that, and any rise or fall between two points must be witnessed by a derivative of matching sign in between — so if no derivative has that sign, no such change can occur.',
 s: ['Here is the claim to be earned: if f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b) with f′ > 0 throughout, then x₁ < x₂ in [a, b] forces f(x₁) < f(x₂).',
   'The mean value theorem says that under those hypotheses there is a point c strictly between x₁ and x₂ with f′(c) = [f(x₂) − f(x₁)]/(x₂ − x₁). A sketch of why, with the one assumption named plainly: tilt the picture by subtracting the chord, setting d(x) = f(x) − [f(x₁) + (chord slope)(x − x₁)], so that d(x₁) = d(x₂) = 0. A continuous function on a closed bounded interval attains a greatest and a least value somewhere on it — this is the extreme value theorem, and it is the one thing here taken on trust, since proving it requires the completeness of the real numbers and belongs to a later analysis course. If both extremes of d occur only at the endpoints then the greatest and least values of d are both 0, so d is 0 throughout and d′ vanishes everywhere inside; otherwise some extreme sits at an interior point, where the previous lesson’s argument forces d′ = 0 there. Either way d′(c) = 0 for some interior c, and since d′ = f′ − (chord slope), that says f′(c) is the chord slope.',
   'Now apply it. Rearranged, f(x₂) − f(x₁) = f′(c)(x₂ − x₁) for some c in between. The second factor is positive because x₁ < x₂, and the first is positive by hypothesis, so the product is positive and f(x₂) > f(x₁) — for every such pair at once. A sign held at every point separately has become a single global statement, which is exactly what was missing. The same line with f′ < 0 gives decreasing, and with f′ = 0 throughout gives f(x₂) = f(x₁) for all pairs: a function with zero derivative on an interval is constant.',
   'The second derivative needs no new theorem, only this one applied a second time — to f′ instead of f. Where f″ exists, f′ is differentiable and therefore continuous, so step three applies to f′ word for word: f″ > 0 across an interval makes f′ increasing there, meaning the slope itself is climbing. That is the whole content of “concave up”, and it is why a curve that is falling with f″ > 0 is decelerating rather than plunging — still falling, but by less and less each step. What it does not promise is that the fall ever flattens out: f(x) = −2x + e⁻ˣ has f′ = −2 − e⁻ˣ, which is increasing and yet never rises above −2, so the curve descends forever at a rate that improves without ever approaching level.',
   'The second derivative test follows from the definition directly, so it survives even where f″ exists at the single point c alone. f″(c) > 0 says the limit of [f′(c + h) − f′(c)]/h is positive, and a quantity whose limit is positive is itself positive for all small enough h ≠ 0; with f′(c) = 0 that makes f′(c + h) carry the same sign as h — negative just left of c, positive just right — so by step three f falls into c and rises out of it, a local minimum. When f″(c) = 0 the test is genuinely silent rather than coy: x⁴, −x⁴ and x³ all have f′ = f″ = 0 at the origin, and are respectively a minimum, a maximum and neither.'],
 m: 'This is the theorem that licenses every conclusion the lesson draws from a sign chart, and it is the same one the integrals lesson leans on when it argues that two antiderivatives of the same function differ by a constant — that step is exactly the f′ = 0 case of step three. Practically, it is the difference between sampling and knowing: a profiler trace or a loss curve measured at a dozen points tells you nothing about the gaps, whereas a sign on the derivative across a range settles every pair of points in that range at once. It is also the honest precondition for searching a continuous quantity by bisection — hunting for the smallest thread pool that meets a latency target is only valid if the latency is genuinely monotone in the pool size, and a derivative sign on a model is how you establish that rather than hope it.'
},

modular: {
 q: 'Why is it safe to take the remainder early — at every step of a calculation — rather than only at the end?',
 a: 'Congruence mod n is a claim about a difference being a multiple of n, and both addition and multiplication treat such a difference gently: whatever you throw away by replacing a number with its remainder, the arithmetic only ever multiplies that discarded piece by another whole number, so it stays a multiple of n and stays invisible. Check that once for + and once for ×, and reducing early stops being an optimisation you hope is safe — it is forced to give the same answer.',
 s: ['Write down what congruence means and nothing more: a ≡ a′ (mod n) means n divides a − a′, that is a − a′ = nk for some integer k. Replacing a number by its remainder is exactly this situation, since a and a mod n differ by a whole number of copies of n.',
   'Addition. Suppose a ≡ a′ and b ≡ b′, so a − a′ = nk and b − b′ = nl. Then (a + b) − (a′ + b′) = nk + nl = n(k + l), a multiple of n, so a + b ≡ a′ + b′ (mod n). Subtraction is the same line with a minus sign.',
   'Multiplication needs one extra move — add and subtract the same quantity so that only one factor changes at a time: ab − a′b′ = ab − a′b + a′b − a′b′ = b(a − a′) + a′(b − b′) = b·nk + a′·nl = n(bk + a′l). A multiple of n again, so ab ≡ a′b′ (mod n).',
   'Those two facts are the entire permission slip: in any expression built from additions, subtractions and multiplications, any input may be swapped for anything congruent to it without changing the answer mod n. Fixed powers come along free, since aᵐ is repeated multiplication and step 3 applied m − 1 times gives a ≡ a′ ⇒ aᵐ ≡ a′ᵐ. The exponent m is a count of factors rather than one of the numbers being combined, so it is not something you may reduce mod n.',
   'Hence (a + b) mod n = ((a mod n) + (b mod n)) mod n, and likewise for products: reduce whenever you like. What is conspicuously absent is division. From ab ≡ ac (mod n) you may not cancel a — 2×3 ≡ 2×0 (mod 6) while 3 ≢ 0 — because nothing above ever produced a way to undo a multiplication. That missing operation is the whole subject of the modular inverse lesson.'],
 m: 'Every practical use of mod rests on this. A hash can be folded into a bucket at any point in the pipeline and give the same bucket; a rolling checksum can reduce after each byte instead of first building an astronomical integer; and unsigned machine arithmetic is literally this theorem — a 32-bit register computes mod 2³², so overflow is not corruption but an exact answer to a slightly different question. It is also what keeps modular exponentiation affordable: reducing after every squaring holds intermediate values below n and their products below n², which is the difference between numbers that fit in a few registers and numbers with millions of digits.'
},

gcd: {
 q: 'Why does replacing a with its remainder on division by b leave the greatest common divisor untouched?',
 a: 'Do not compare the two gcds directly — compare the lists they are chosen from. A number divides both a and b exactly when it divides both b and the remainder, because the remainder is built out of a and b by whole-number operations and a is built back out of b and the remainder the same way. The two pairs therefore have one and the same list of common divisors, so the largest entry on that list is the same too. Nothing about “greatest” is used until the final line; all the work is in showing the lists coincide.',
 s: ['Take a and b not both zero, with b ≠ 0, and divide with remainder: a = qb + r, where q is the whole number of times b fits into a and r = a mod b, with 0 ≤ r < b. That q and r are integers is the only fact about them the argument needs.',
   'Suppose d divides a and d divides b. Then d divides qb, and r = a − qb is a difference of two multiples of d, so d divides r. Every common divisor of (a, b) is therefore a common divisor of (b, r).',
   'Now the other direction. Suppose d divides b and d divides r. Then d divides qb, and a = qb + r is a sum of two multiples of d, so d divides a. Every common divisor of (b, r) is therefore a common divisor of (a, b).',
   'The two sets of common divisors contain exactly the same numbers. Each is non-empty, since 1 is in it, and each is bounded, since no divisor of a non-zero number exceeds it in size — so each set genuinely has a largest member, and identical sets have identical largest members. Hence gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, r) = gcd(b, a mod b), which is Euclid’s step.',
   'The identity becomes an algorithm because r < b: each round replaces the pair with one whose second entry is strictly smaller, and a strictly decreasing sequence of non-negative integers cannot run forever, so some remainder is eventually 0. The pair is then (g, 0) with g the last non-zero remainder, and since every number divides 0 the common divisors of (g, 0) are just the divisors of g, making the gcd g itself. The g you halt on is the gcd you began with, carried through every step unchanged.'],
 m: 'The speed is worth pinning down, because it is what makes this usable at cryptographic sizes. Take a ≥ b, which costs nothing since otherwise the first round merely swaps them. If b ≤ a/2 then a mod b < b ≤ a/2; and if b > a/2 then a holds only one copy of b, so a mod b = a − b < a/2. Either way the larger entry is more than halved every two rounds, so the whole computation takes at most about 2log₂ a divisions — a few thousand for the 2048-bit numbers RSA uses, and in practice noticeably fewer. What the algorithm never does is factorise anything, which matters enormously, since factoring numbers that size is precisely the problem RSA bets nobody can solve. gcd is cheap and factoring is dear, even though the schoolroom method computes the first out of the second.'
},

primes: {
 q: 'Why can a number not break into primes in two genuinely different ways?',
 a: 'Existence of a factorisation is the easy half: keep splitting composites and you must stop, because the pieces get smaller. Uniqueness is the hard half, and all of it hangs on one property of primes that looks obvious and is not — that a prime dividing a product must divide one of the factors. Grant that, and two rival factorisations can be matched off against each other one prime at a time until neither has anything left. So the real content is a proof of that one property, and it comes from the Euclidean algorithm rather than from anything about primes being unbreakable.',
 s: ['Existence first, by strong induction. Take n > 1 and assume every integer strictly between 1 and n is a product of primes. If n is itself prime it is a one-prime product and there is nothing to do; otherwise n = uv with 1 < u < n and 1 < v < n, and both u and v are products of primes by the assumption, so stringing those two lists together factors n.',
   'Uniqueness needs Euclid’s lemma: if a prime p divides a product ab, then p divides a or p divides b. This is the one non-obvious input, and it genuinely needs p to be prime — 6 divides 4 × 9 while dividing neither 4 nor 9.',
   'Proving it needs Bézout’s identity: for any a and b there are integers x, y with ax + by = gcd(a, b). That is not an article of faith, because Euclid’s algorithm builds the pair as it runs. Write the two starting numbers as combinations of themselves, a = 1·a + 0·b and b = 0·a + 1·b. Every later remainder is one earlier number minus a whole multiple of another, and a combination of a and b minus a whole multiple of another such combination is again a combination of a and b. So every remainder the algorithm produces carries a pair (x, y) with it — the last non-zero one included, and the previous lesson showed that one is the gcd.',
   'Now suppose p divides ab but does not divide a. gcd(p, a) divides p, and p’s only positive divisors are 1 and p; it cannot be p, since p does not divide a; so gcd(p, a) = 1 and Bézout gives px + ay = 1. Multiply that equation through by b: pbx + aby = b. p divides the first term outright, and p divides ab so it divides the second; a number dividing both terms divides their sum, so p divides b. That is Euclid’s lemma proved.',
   'Uniqueness now follows by induction on n. Suppose n = p₁p₂⋯p_r = q₁q₂⋯q_s, two lists of primes. p₁ divides the right-hand product, so applying the lemma repeatedly — p₁ divides q₁ or it divides the rest, and so on down — it divides some qⱼ; and since qⱼ has only 1 and itself as divisors and p₁ is not 1, p₁ = qⱼ. Cancel that shared factor from both sides and repeat on the smaller number n/p₁. Neither list can be exhausted while the other still holds primes, because a product of primes is greater than 1 and the empty product is 1. So the lists have the same length and the same primes with the same multiplicities: identical up to the order they were written in.'],
 m: 'Uniqueness is what licenses the phrase “the prime factorisation”, and that is the object RSA keeps secret: n = pq has exactly one factorisation, so recovering p and q is a well-posed question with a single answer, and the security claim is that finding it is slow rather than that it is ambiguous. The way factors pair off also settles the trial-division bound — if n = uv with both factors above √n then uv > n, so every composite has a factor at or below √n and testing that far is enough. And the contrast with the previous lesson is the crux of the whole subject: Euclid delivers a gcd for numbers of this size in a few thousand divisions without ever learning one prime factor of either number.'
},

rsa: {
 q: 'Why does squaring your way up cost only about log b multiplications, when the exponent b may be astronomically large?',
 a: 'An exponent counts factors, and you are under no obligation to produce those factors one at a time. Squaring doubles the count for a single multiplication, so the question to ask is not “how do I get from aᵏ to aᵏ⁺¹” but “how do I get from a^⌊b/2⌋ to a^b” — which takes one squaring and, when b is odd, one more multiplication. An exponent can only be halved about log₂ b times before it hits zero, and that count is the entire cost.',
 s: ['Split on parity, using nothing but the meaning of a power as repeated multiplication. If b = 2k then a^b = a^(k+k) = aᵏ·aᵏ = (aᵏ)². If b = 2k + 1 then a^b = a·(aᵏ)². Either way k = ⌊b/2⌋.',
   'So computing a^b reduces to computing aᵏ for the halved exponent, plus one squaring and at most one further multiplication. With a⁰ = 1 as the stopping case that is a complete method, and it is correct by strong induction: each line above is an identity, and every call is made on a strictly smaller exponent, so the descent reaches 0 and the identities compose back up.',
   'Count the rounds. After m halvings the exponent is ⌊b/2ᵐ⌋, which is 0 as soon as 2ᵐ > b — that is, after ⌊log₂ b⌋ + 1 rounds. Each round costs at most two multiplications, so the whole computation costs at most 2(⌊log₂ b⌋ + 1). For b = 1024 that is at most 22, and ten if you count only the squarings, against 1023 for the naive loop.',
   'Compare that with the size of the problem as it is actually written down. Writing b takes about log₂ b bits, so this method costs a number of multiplications proportional to the length of the exponent. The naive loop costs b multiplications, and b is roughly 2 raised to that bit count — exponential in the size of the input, which is why a 2048-bit exponent is routine for one method and impossible for the other.',
   'The numbers stay small because congruence permits it. By the modular arithmetic lesson, replacing any factor by its remainder mod n leaves the product’s remainder unchanged, so reducing after every squaring is exact rather than an approximation: every stored value stays below n and every product below n², no matter how enormous b is.'],
 m: 'This is the whole reason public-key cryptography runs: encryption and decryption are one modular exponentiation each, and the security argument would be worthless if the honest parties could not afford to perform it. What the method plainly does not do is run backwards — it turns m into mᵉ mod n cheaply and hands you no route from mᵉ mod n back to m — but that is an observation about this algorithm, not a proof that no cheap route exists; RSA rests on the belief that none does, and that belief is an assumption rather than a theorem. One practical sting: the “if b is odd” branch makes the running time depend on the bits of the exponent, and when that exponent is a private key the timing leaks it, which is why real implementations use a ladder performing the same operations whatever each bit says.'
},

'mod-inverse': {
 q: 'Why does a have an inverse mod n exactly when a and n share no common factor?',
 a: 'Multiplying by a, seen mod n, either shuffles the residues thoroughly or collapses them onto a sparse subset, and gcd(a, n) is what decides which. Every value multiplying by a can reach has the form ax − kn, and every such number is a multiple of that gcd — so if the gcd exceeds 1 then 1 is simply out of reach and no inverse can exist. When the gcd is 1, Bézout hands you the inverse outright, and that identity is not an existence claim to be taken on trust, because the Euclidean algorithm constructs it as a by-product of divisions it was performing anyway.',
 s: ['An inverse of a mod n is a number x with ax ≡ 1 (mod n). It is what you multiply by in place of dividing: from ax ≡ 1 you get (ab)x ≡ b, so multiplying by x undoes multiplying by a.',
   'The easy direction first. If such an x exists then ax − 1 is a multiple of n, say ax − 1 = ny, so ax − ny = 1. Any positive d dividing both a and n divides the left-hand side, hence divides 1, hence is 1 — so gcd(a, n) = 1. Coprimality is therefore necessary, which is why 4 has no inverse mod 8: every 4x − 8y is a multiple of 4, and 1 is not.',
   'For the converse, Bézout: for any a and n there are integers x, y with ax + ny = gcd(a, n). It drops out of Euclid. Begin by writing the two starting numbers as combinations of themselves, a = 1·a + 0·n and n = 0·a + 1·n. Every later remainder is formed as one earlier number minus a whole multiple of another, and subtracting a whole multiple of one combination of a and n from another such combination leaves a combination of a and n. So every remainder the algorithm produces carries a pair (x, y) with it, including the last non-zero one — which the previous lesson identified as the gcd.',
   'Take gcd(a, n) = 1. Bézout gives ax + ny = 1, so ax − 1 = −ny is a multiple of n, which says precisely ax ≡ 1 (mod n). The x that extended Euclid produced is the inverse, found in the same logarithmic number of divisions as the gcd itself.',
   'The inverse is also unique mod n, which is what earns it the notation a⁻¹: if ax ≡ 1 and ax′ ≡ 1 then x ≡ x(ax′) = (xa)x′ ≡ x′. And when the modulus is a prime p the condition costs almost nothing — p’s only divisors are 1 and p, so gcd(a, p) = 1 for every a that p does not divide, and every non-zero residue is invertible. That is what makes arithmetic mod p a system in which you may divide by anything except zero.'],
 m: 'This is the step that turns an RSA public exponent into a private one: d is e⁻¹ mod φ(n), which extended Euclid finds in milliseconds for anyone who knows φ(n) and nobody who does not. Invertibility mod p is also the ground under finite-field arithmetic — error-correcting codes, secret sharing and elliptic-curve cryptography all need division to exist. The theorem has a mundane consequence you have already relied on, too: machine integers are arithmetic mod 2³², whose coprime residues are exactly the odd ones, so when a division by an odd constant is known to come out exact — subtracting two pointers and dividing by the element size, say — a compiler can replace it with a multiplication by that constant’s inverse mod 2³² and get the exactly right answer.'
},

'fermat-euler': {
 q: 'Why should raising a number to the power p − 1 bring you back to 1, of all things?',
 a: 'Multiplying every non-zero residue mod p by a fixed a neither creates nor destroys anything — it shuffles the list into a new order, because multiplying by a can always be undone by multiplying by a⁻¹. So the product of the shuffled list is the product of the original list. One side of that equation has collected a factor of a for each of the p − 1 entries and the other side has not, so cancelling the common part leaves a^(p−1) ≡ 1. The exponent is p − 1 for the plainest reason imaginable: it counts the entries on the list.',
 s: ['Fix a prime p and an a that p does not divide, and take the list of non-zero residues 1, 2, …, p − 1. By the previous lesson every entry on that list is invertible mod p, and so is a, since p being prime makes gcd(p, a) = 1 for anything p does not divide.',
   'Multiply the list through by a and reduce mod p. No entry lands on 0: if ai ≡ 0 then multiplying by a⁻¹ gives i ≡ 0, false for 1 ≤ i ≤ p − 1. And no two collide: ai ≡ aj gives i ≡ j after multiplying by a⁻¹, and two residues drawn from 1…p−1 that are congruent mod p are equal.',
   'So a·1, a·2, …, a·(p − 1), reduced mod p, are p − 1 distinct values taken from a set with exactly p − 1 members. A list that long with no repeats must use every member once — it is the original list in a different order.',
   'Multiply everything on the list together, which the multiplication rule from the first lesson makes legitimate mod p. The shuffled product is (a·1)(a·2)⋯(a·(p−1)) = a^(p−1)·(p−1)!, and the original product is (p−1)!. Same members, so the two agree: a^(p−1)·(p−1)! ≡ (p−1)! (mod p).',
   '(p−1)! is a product of invertible residues, and a product of invertibles is invertible — (uv)⁻¹ = v⁻¹u⁻¹ undoes it — so multiply both sides by that inverse and the factorial vanishes, leaving a^(p−1) ≡ 1 (mod p). Primality entered nowhere except to guarantee invertibility, so run the identical five lines mod any n over the residues coprime to n, assuming now that gcd(a, n) = 1. That set really is permuted by multiplication by a: if gcd(u, n) = 1 then gcd(au, n) = 1, since a prime dividing both n and au would by Euclid’s lemma divide a or u and so share a factor with n; and reducing mod n changes nothing, because au and au mod n differ by a multiple of n and so share exactly the same factors with n. That set has φ(n) members, and the count of members is once again the exponent: a^φ(n) ≡ 1 (mod n), Euler’s theorem.'],
 m: 'This is why RSA decryption returns the message. The keys satisfy ed ≡ 1 (mod φ(n)), so ed = 1 + kφ(n) for some whole k ≥ 0, and (mᵉ)ᵈ = m^(1+kφ(n)) = m·(m^φ(n))ᵏ ≡ m·1ᵏ = m whenever gcd(m, n) = 1 — decryption is not a separate mechanism but the same exponentiation, with an exponent chosen so that the two powers compose to the identity. The leftover case, a message sharing a factor with n, does hold but needs its own argument mod p and mod q separately; it is a second step rather than a corollary, and pretending otherwise would be cheating. Run backwards, the theorem is a primality test: if a^(n−1) ≢ 1 (mod n) then n is definitely composite, decidable in a number of multiplications proportional to log n for a number of any size. The converse fails, and how it fails matters — 561 = 3 × 11 × 17 passes for every base coprime to it, and such Carmichael numbers are why the standard test is Miller–Rabin, which strengthens this one rather than trusting it.'
},

linear: {
 q: 'Why is “do the same thing to both sides” allowed to change the equation without changing its answers?',
 a: 'An equation is not a sentence to be tidied for its own sake — it is a question: which values of x make this true? Two equations that look nothing alike can be asking the same question, and solving means walking from a hard phrasing of the question to an easy one without ever changing what is being asked. An operation is safe exactly when it is defined everywhere it needs to be and can be undone: being defined means no answer is lost, and being undoable means no false one gets in. Those two tests decide every step of school algebra, and they are why squaring both sides is dangerous and multiplying by zero is fatal.',
 s: ['Say what solving means. The solution set of an equation in x is the collection of numbers that turn it into a true statement. Two equations are equivalent when they have the same solution set — that, and nothing about how they are written, is what a legal step must preserve.',
   'No answers are lost, provided the operation is defined where it matters. Apply a rule g to both sides, turning L = R into g(L) = g(R). If s is a solution of the first, then at x = s the two sides L and R are literally the same number, and one rule handed the same number twice returns the same result — so g(L) = g(R) holds at s too. The proviso is not decoration: taking the logarithm of both sides of 2x = x − 3 throws away its solution x = −3, because at that value both sides are −6 and log is not defined there.',
   'Answers can still be gained, because g may send two different numbers to the same place. The equation x = −3 has solution set {−3}. Square both sides and you get x² = 9, whose solution set is {3, −3}: the number 3 was not an answer before and is one now. Nothing was misreported — squaring sends 3 and −3 to the same place, so the new equation can no longer tell them apart, and every extraneous root has exactly this shape.',
   'If g can be undone, that cannot happen. Apply the undoing rule to g(L) = g(R) and you are back at L = R, so every solution of the new equation was already a solution of the old one. Adding c is undone by subtracting c; multiplying by a non-zero m is undone by dividing by m. Those are precisely the moves permitted, and their reversibility is the licence — not tradition.',
   'Now the method is a chain of equivalences rather than a ritual. From ax + b = c with a ≠ 0: subtract b, undone by adding b, to get ax = c − b; divide by a, reversible precisely because a ≠ 0, to get x = (c − b)/a. Every step preserved the solution set, and the final equation plainly has exactly one solution, so the original had exactly one too. The moves that demand a check are the ones where what you apply depends on x itself, so it is a different operation at every value: multiplying both sides by (x − 1) is reversible only where x ≠ 1, and it manufactures x = 1 as a root; cancelling (x − 1) from both sides is that same move backwards, and it deletes x = 1 if it was there; multiplying by zero collapses everything to 0 = 0, true for every x, which is reversibility failing as completely as it can.'],
 m: 'You no longer need a memorised list of which steps require checking your answers — you need two questions: is this step defined everywhere the solutions live, and could it have been undone? Squaring, cancelling a factor containing the unknown, taking a logarithm of something that might not be positive, multiplying up by a denominator that might vanish: each fails one test somewhere, and each demands that you substitute your answers back. The same discipline is what makes rearranging arithmetic in code safe or unsafe. Inverting a formula — turning a byte count into an element count, a frame index into a timestamp — is exactly this chain of reversible steps, and the places where it breaks are the places where the real bug lives: integer division is not reversible, so multiplying it back does not return what you started with.'
},

quadratics: {
 q: 'Where does the quadratic formula actually come from, and why is there a b² − 4ac hiding under the square root?',
 a: 'There is only one kind of equation we can solve outright: (something)² = number, because a square root undoes a square. So the entire job is to force ax² + bx + c = 0 into that shape, and there is exactly one way to do it. The pieces x² + (b/a)x are the opening of the expansion of (x + b/(2a))², short by one constant — so you supply the missing constant and subtract it again, which changes nothing. Do that manoeuvre in general instead of on a particular example and the formula falls out with no choices left to make; the discriminant is simply whatever ends up on the right-hand side of the square.',
 s: ['Start from ax² + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, and divide through by a: x² + (b/a)x + c/a = 0. Dividing by a non-zero number is reversible, so this asks the same question as the original.',
   'Look at what a perfect square expands to: (x + p)² = x² + 2px + p². To make its middle term match ours we need 2p = b/a, so p = b/(2a) and no other value. Then (x + b/(2a))² = x² + (b/a)x + b²/(4a²) — our first two terms exactly, plus a spare b²/(4a²).',
   'So x² + (b/a)x is (x + b/(2a))² − b²/(4a²). Substituting that into step one gives (x + b/(2a))² − b²/(4a²) + c/a = 0, that is (x + b/(2a))² = b²/(4a²) − c/a = (b² − 4ac)/(4a²). The discriminant has appeared, and it has appeared in exactly one place: as the thing a square is being asked to equal.',
   'A square equals a given number only when that number is not negative, and the numbers whose square it is then form one pair differing only in sign — collapsing to a single number when that number is 0. Taking square roots gives |x + b/(2a)| = √(b² − 4ac)/|2a|, since √(4a²) = |2a|, and because the ± already covers both signs, writing x + b/(2a) = ±√(b² − 4ac)/(2a) names the same pair whichever way 2a points. Subtracting b/(2a) from both sides and putting the two fractions over the common denominator 2a gives x = (−b ± √(b² − 4ac))/(2a).',
   'Everything usually asserted about the discriminant is now something you can see. If b² − 4ac > 0 the ± produces two different numbers, so two real roots; if it is 0 the ± produces the same number twice, so one; if it is negative, no real number squares to it and there are none. Step three also rewrites the whole quadratic as ax² + bx + c = a(x + b/(2a))² + (c − b²/(4a)), and the squared term is zero at x = −b/(2a) and strictly positive everywhere else, so from that point the curve moves away from the constant c − b²/(4a) in one direction only — upwards when a > 0, downwards when a < 0. That is the vertex: a minimum for an upward parabola, a maximum for a downward one, sitting exactly midway between the two roots because the ± is symmetric about it.'],
 m: 'Completing the square is not a trick kept for quadratics; it is the standard way of isolating a variable that appears squared, and you will meet the same manoeuvre in the vertex form of a parabola, in the exponent of the normal distribution, and in deriving closed-form solutions to least-squares problems. Nearer to hand, the derivation shows that the discriminant is computable before the square root is, which is a real optimisation: a ray–sphere intersection test computes b² − 4ac, and if it is negative the ray misses, so the routine returns without ever calling √. The derivation also warns you where floating point will betray it — when b² is far larger than 4ac and b > 0, the root (−b + √Δ)/(2a) subtracts two nearly equal numbers and loses most of its significant digits; since the two roots multiply to c/a, that root is better computed as 2c/(−b − √Δ), which adds instead of subtracting. Knowing why the formula has the shape it has is what lets you rewrite it without breaking it.'
},

systems: {
 q: 'Why does a single number, ad − bc, decide whether two equations pin down one point?',
 a: 'Push elimination through with letters instead of numbers and watch what happens. Both variables come out with the same coefficient standing in front of them, and that coefficient is ad − bc. When it is not zero you divide by it and get one answer; when it is zero it cannot be divided away, and the variables have vanished from the left-hand side entirely, taking the possibility of a unique answer with them. The geometry says the same thing — two lines with equal slopes are parallel and either never meet or coincide completely — and the two accounts turn out to be one fact, because equal slopes is precisely the statement ad = bc.',
 s: ['Eliminate in general. From ax + by = e and cx + dy = f, multiply the first by d and the second by b and subtract: (ad − bc)x = ed − bf. Now multiply the first by c and the second by a and subtract the other way: (ad − bc)y = af − ce. The same number now sits in front of both unknowns, and it got there by itself.',
   'Suppose ad − bc ≠ 0. Then you may divide, and step one leaves exactly one candidate: x = (ed − bf)/(ad − bc) and y = (af − ce)/(ad − bc). That argument shows any solution must be this pair; it does not by itself show the pair works, so check it. In the first equation, a(ed − bf) + b(af − ce) = ade − abf + abf − bce = e(ad − bc), which divided by ad − bc is e; in the second, c(ed − bf) + d(af − ce) = cde − bcf + adf − cde = f(ad − bc), which divided by ad − bc is f. Necessary and sufficient: exactly one solution.',
   'Suppose instead ad − bc = 0. The left-hand sides of step one collapse to 0, leaving the two conditions 0 = ed − bf and 0 = af − ce. These say nothing about x or y — they are demands on the constants. Fail either one and the elimination has produced “0 equals something non-zero”, so no pair (x, y) can exist at all.',
   'And if a solution does exist when ad − bc = 0, there are immediately infinitely many, because some non-zero direction is then invisible to both left-hand sides. If a and b are not both zero, take the direction (−b, a), which is not the zero pair: feeding it in gives a(−b) + b(a) = 0 and c(−b) + d(a) = ad − bc = 0. If instead a = b = 0, the first left-hand side is 0 for every direction, so take (−d, c) when c and d are not both zero — the second gives c(−d) + d(c) = 0 — and any non-zero direction whatever if all four coefficients vanish. Adding any multiple t of such a direction (u, v) changes neither left-hand side, so (x₀ + tu, y₀ + tv) solves both equations for every t. One solution breeds a whole line of them; exactly one solution is the single outcome a vanishing ad − bc rules out.',
   'The geometry is the same statement. When b and d are non-zero the two lines have slopes −a/b and −c/d, and those agree exactly when ad = bc — parallel lines, which either never meet or are the same line, matching steps three and four. There is also a picture that covers every case: read the system as x·(a, c) + y·(b, d) = (e, f), so you are trying to reach the target (e, f) by combining two arrows. The number ad − bc is, up to sign, the area of the parallelogram those two arrows span, and it is zero exactly when the arrows are parallel — lying along one line through the origin, or one of them being the zero arrow — so their combinations sweep out at most that single line, missing most targets entirely and reaching the ones on it in infinitely many ways.'],
 m: 'That number is the determinant, and this is why a linear solver or a constraint layout engine reports “singular matrix” rather than returning a wrong answer: the system genuinely has no unique answer to return, and the division in step two is the operation that cannot be carried out. Step two is also Cramer’s rule in full, for the 2×2 case. The floating-point lesson is the one that bites in practice: a determinant that is merely close to zero divides by a tiny number, so small errors in a, b, c, d, e or f are magnified enormously in x and y. The answer exists, but it is untrustworthy — which is why numerical libraries report a condition number rather than a yes-or-no verdict on singularity, and why a layout that “jitters” under a one-pixel resize is usually a nearly-parallel pair of constraints rather than a bug in the solver.'
},

functions: {
 q: 'Why is “no two inputs share an output” exactly the right condition for a function to be undoable, rather than just a handy warning sign?',
 a: 'An inverse is a machine that is handed an output and must name the input that produced it. So ask what could stop it. If two inputs produced the same output, the machine is being asked for two different answers to one question and there is nothing it can say. If no two inputs ever collide, then each output has one and only one input behind it, and “name that input” is a complete, unambiguous instruction — which is to say, a function. There is nothing else to check, because the condition is not a symptom of invertibility but a restatement of it. The argument below proves both halves, and the second half does not merely establish that an inverse exists: it builds it.',
 s: ['Fix what is being claimed. An inverse of f is a function g, defined on the outputs f actually produces, satisfying g(f(x)) = x for every input x and f(g(y)) = y for every such output y. Undoing, exactly, in both directions.',
   'Necessity. Suppose f has an inverse g, and suppose two inputs share an output: f(x₁) = f(x₂). Apply g to both sides — the same number goes in, so the same number comes out — and x₁ = g(f(x₁)) = g(f(x₂)) = x₂. The two inputs were never different. So an invertible function cannot have a collision; being one-to-one is forced, not merely advisable.',
   'Sufficiency, which is the half that does the work. Suppose no two inputs share an output, and take any y that f actually produces. “Produces” means at least one x has f(x) = y; the assumption means at most one does. At least one and at most one is exactly one — so the instruction “send y to the unique x with f(x) = y” names a definite number for every y, and that is precisely what it takes to be a function. Call it g.',
   'Check that g does the job. For any input x, g(f(x)) is the unique input mapping to f(x), and x is such an input, so g(f(x)) = x. For any produced y, g(y) was chosen so that f(g(y)) = y. Both requirements of step one are met, so being one-to-one was not just necessary but enough.',
   'Watch it fail, to see that nothing was smuggled in. f(x) = x² sends 3 and −3 both to 9, so step three breaks at “at most one”: g(9) would have to be 3 to satisfy g(f(3)) = 3 and −3 to satisfy g(f(−3)) = −3. No choice works, and no cleverer formula can rescue it — the information distinguishing 3 from −3 is not somewhere else in the output, it is gone. Restrict the domain to x ≥ 0 and one of the two culprits is no longer an input at all; the theorem then applies unchanged and hands you √ as the inverse. The domain is part of the function, and shrinking it is what turns a non-invertible function into an invertible one.'],
 m: 'This is the exact line between compression and destruction. An encoder — base64, a serialisation format, a lossless codec — must be one-to-one, or the bits genuinely cannot come back, and no decoder however clever will change that. A hash function is deliberately not one-to-one, and its collisions are not a flaw to be engineered away but an arithmetic certainty: more possible inputs than possible fixed-width digests means some digest must be shared, which is the pigeonhole argument from the discrete maths track. The same theorem explains the practical habits around it too. Truncating a float, converting to greyscale, and integer division all destroy information, so any “undo” for them is a guess dressed up as an inverse. And the reason every library documents a range for asin, acos and atan — rather than leaving it to taste — is step five: the trigonometric functions repeat, so a domain had to be chosen before an inverse could exist at all.'
},

polynomials: {
 q: 'Why does setting each factor to zero find every root, and why can a degree-n polynomial never have more than n of them?',
 a: 'Two things are happening here and only one of them is obvious. That (x − 3)(x − 4) is zero when x is 3 or 4 is immediate. That it is zero in no other way is a genuine property of the number system, not a triviality — it fails in arithmetic modulo 12, where 3 × 4 = 0 with neither factor zero at all. Once you have that property, the root count follows by peeling: every root lets you pull out a linear factor, each pull costs one degree, and there is only so much degree available to spend.',
 s: ['The zero product property, proved rather than assumed. Suppose ab = 0 and a ≠ 0. Because a is a non-zero number it has a reciprocal, so multiply both sides by 1/a: b = (1/a)(ab) = (1/a)·0 = 0. So at least one factor is zero, and there is no third possibility. The proof shows exactly what it depends on — the existence of 1/a — which is why it collapses for the integers modulo 12, where 4 has no reciprocal and 3 × 4 ≡ 0 holds with both factors non-zero.',
   'So a factored form is a complete list of roots, not merely a source of some of them. From (x − 3)(x − 4) = 0, step one forces x − 3 = 0 or x − 4 = 0, giving x = 3 or x = 4 and ruling out everything else in one line. That is why factoring solves equations: not because the roots are visible, but because no invisible ones remain.',
   'Where the factors come from — the factor theorem. Divide p(x) by (x − r) by ordinary long division; the process terminates because each step reduces the degree, and it ends with p(x) = (x − r)q(x) + R where the remainder R has degree below 1 and so is a constant. Now put x = r: the first term dies and p(r) = R. So the remainder is p(r), and therefore (x − r) divides p exactly when r is a root — with a quotient q of degree one less, carrying the same leading coefficient and so never the zero polynomial.',
   'Peel repeatedly. Let p have degree n and let r₁, …, r_k be distinct roots. By step three, p(x) = (x − r₁)q₁(x) with deg q₁ = n − 1. Feed in r₂: 0 = p(r₂) = (r₂ − r₁)q₁(r₂), and r₂ − r₁ ≠ 0, so step one forces q₁(r₂) = 0, and step three peels again: q₁ = (x − r₂)q₂ with deg q₂ = n − 2. The same move repeats at every stage — after j peels, 0 = p(r_{j+1}) = (r_{j+1} − r₁)⋯(r_{j+1} − r_j)·q_j(r_{j+1}), every bracket is non-zero because the roots are distinct, so step one leaves q_j(r_{j+1}) = 0. Each peel costs exactly one degree and a degree cannot be negative, so k ≤ n.',
   'Hence a polynomial of degree n has at most n distinct roots. A quadratic therefore crosses zero at most twice — the claim the quadratics lesson made about the shape of a parabola, now earned rather than illustrated — and a polynomial of degree at most n that vanishes at n + 1 different points cannot exist unless it is the zero polynomial, with every coefficient zero.'],
 m: 'The last line is worth more than it looks. If two polynomials of degree at most n agree at n + 1 points, their difference vanishes at n + 1 points and so is identically zero: the two are the same polynomial. That is the engine under a surprising amount of computing. It is why n + 1 sample points determine a unique interpolating polynomial, which is what Lagrange interpolation constructs; it is why Reed–Solomon coding works, since the message polynomial is still pinned down by enough surviving evaluations even after some are corrupted; and it is why Shamir’s secret sharing can hand out shares so that k of them reconstruct the secret and k − 1 constrain it not at all. All of these are done in arithmetic modulo a prime, or modulo an irreducible polynomial, and step one says precisely why: those are the settings where every non-zero element has a reciprocal, so the zero product property survives — modulo 12 it does not, and the whole tower comes down.'
},

rational: {
 q: 'Why does only the leading term matter once x is large, when every other term is still sitting there in the expression?',
 a: 'They are still there; they are being outvoted. Divide the top and bottom by the highest power of x on show — which is multiplying by 1, so nothing is discarded and nothing is approximated — and every term except the two leaders is left carrying an x in its denominator. A quantity with x underneath can be forced below any tolerance you care to name, simply by going far enough out. So the leaders are not the only terms that matter; they are the only terms that still matter at any accuracy you demand, and that is a stronger and more checkable claim.',
 s: ['Take a ratio of two polynomials of the same degree n, y = (aₙxⁿ + ⋯ + a₀)/(bₙxⁿ + ⋯ + b₀) with aₙ ≠ 0 and bₙ ≠ 0, and divide top and bottom by xⁿ. Multiplying by (1/xⁿ)/(1/xⁿ) is multiplying by 1, legitimate for every x ≠ 0, so the function is untouched: y = (aₙ + aₙ₋₁/x + ⋯ + a₀/xⁿ)/(bₙ + bₙ₋₁/x + ⋯ + b₀/xⁿ). Every term is still in the expression; all but the two leaders now carry an x underneath.',
   'Make “outvoted” precise. A term c/xᵏ with k ≥ 1 satisfies |c/xᵏ| ≤ |c|/x for every x ≥ 1, so it drops below any tolerance δ > 0 you name as soon as x > |c|/δ. There are only finitely many such terms, so running that once for each with δ divided by their number gives a single x beyond which the entire leftover on the top — call it u — and the entire leftover on the bottom — call it v — are both smaller than δ in size. Nothing here is a feeling about large numbers: you name the tolerance, the argument names the x.',
   'Now compare y with aₙ/bₙ directly, rather than eyeing the two parts separately. With numerator aₙ + u and denominator bₙ + v, subtracting over a common denominator gives y − aₙ/bₙ = (bₙu − aₙv)/(bₙ(bₙ + v)). Take the δ of step two below |bₙ|/2, as you always may; then |bₙ + v| ≥ |bₙ|/2, so the bottom is at least |bₙ|²/2 in size while the top is at most (|aₙ| + |bₙ|)δ. Hence |y − aₙ/bₙ| ≤ 2(|aₙ| + |bₙ|)δ/|bₙ|², which any prescribed ε beats once δ is chosen small enough and x is taken past the threshold step two names. That is what the horizontal asymptote y = aₙ/bₙ means, and it is the ratio of the leading coefficients because after the division those are the only terms without an x underneath. For y = (3x² + 1)/(x² + 5) the asymptote is y = 3, and here the bound can be sharpened by hand: y − 3 = −14/(x² + 5), which is under ε as soon as x > √(14/ε).',
   'The same single division settles the other cases, so there are no rules to memorise. If the top’s degree is lower than the bottom’s, divide both by the bottom’s leading power: every term of the top is left with an x in its denominator, so by step two the whole top is driven under any tolerance while the bottom stays within any tolerance of its non-zero leading coefficient — the ratio is squeezed to 0 and the asymptote is y = 0. If the top’s degree is higher, that division leaves a positive power of x upstairs, which passes every bound while the bottom settles near a fixed non-zero number, so the ratio passes every bound and there is no horizontal asymptote at all.',
   'Vertical asymptotes are the same reasoning run the other way — near a zero r of the denominator the bottom is being driven towards nothing while the top holds steady at a non-zero value, so the ratio passes every bound — with one qualification that decides real cases. Cancel first. In (x² − 4)/(x − 2), the top factors as (x − 2)(x + 2), so for every x ≠ 2 the expression is just x + 2; the zero of the denominator was cancelled away before it could blow anything up, and x = 2 is a hole rather than a pole. Only the zeros that survive cancellation — where the reduced top is non-zero — are asymptotes.'],
 m: 'This is the Big-O argument in its original habitat, and having done it once you never need to take the Big-O version on faith. Saying that 3n² + 7n + 200 is O(n²) is steps one to three verbatim: divide by n², watch 7/n and 200/n² fall below any tolerance you name, and conclude the ratio settles on the leading coefficient — at which point Big-O throws away even that. The lower-order terms and the constant are not being ignored out of convenience; they are provably swamped, and the proof is what tells you the honest scope of the claim, which is large n only. It says nothing whatever about which of two algorithms wins at n = 10, and the algorithm with the worse asymptotics frequently does. Step five has its own practical edge: a denominator heading to zero is where a float becomes inf, and the hole-versus-pole distinction is the difference between an expression that has a value you must compute another way and one that has no value at all — 0/0 is asking you to cancel first, whereas x/0 with x ≠ 0 has no answer waiting to be found.'
},

counting: {
 q: 'Why do choices in sequence multiply rather than add?',
 a: 'Lay the outcomes out as a tree: one branch for each first choice, and hanging off every one of those branches a copy of everything that can happen afterwards. Counting the tree is then counting equal-sized bunches, and counting equal-sized bunches is exactly what multiplication is. The argument below is that picture, and it exposes the one condition the rule quietly needs — the bunches must all be the same size.',
 s: ['Start with the rule everything else leans on. If a collection is split into groups so that every item lies in exactly one group, the total is the sum of the group sizes, because counting the groups one after another reaches every item once and misses none. That is the sum rule, and the words “exactly one” are what it needs: overlapping groups double-count, which is why |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| only when A and B share nothing.',
   'Now two stages. An outcome is a pair — what you chose first, and what you chose second — so sort all the pairs into groups according to their first component. There are n₁ groups, and no pair belongs to two of them, so the sum rule applies.',
   'Suppose that whichever first choice was made, exactly n₂ second choices remain: the same number every time, though not necessarily the same options. Then every group holds n₂ pairs and the total is n₂ + n₂ + ⋯ + n₂ with n₁ terms, which is n₁n₂.',
   'Extend to r stages by induction on r, with the two-stage case just proved as the base. Group the outcomes by the first choice once more: there are n₁ groups, and each group is a complete set of outcomes for the remaining r − 1 stages, numbering n₂n₃⋯n_r by the inductive hypothesis. The sum rule totals them at n₁(n₂⋯n_r), which is the product rule for r stages. Filling r slots from n options with repetition allowed is the case where every nᵢ is n, giving nʳ.',
   'Step 3 is where the rule is won or lost, and its condition is weaker than it looks. Picking r people in order out of n gives n, then n − 1, then n − 2: which options are left changes with every earlier pick, but how many are left does not, so the product rule still applies. It fails only when the count itself varies. Count the two-digit codes whose second digit is strictly larger than the first: a first digit of 0 leaves nine choices, a first digit of 6 leaves three, so no product of two fixed numbers can be right, and the count has to be got another way — naming the set of two digits already fixes their order, so the answer is C(10,2) = 45.'],
 m: 'Every state-space estimate you will ever make is this rule, and it is what decides whether exhaustive search is a plan or a fantasy: 10⁴ PINs fall in under a second, 26⁸ passwords do not. It also explains why key strength is quoted in bits — adding one character multiplies the space by 26 rather than adding 26, and taking log₂ turns that product into a sum, so each extra character contributes a fixed log₂ 26 ≈ 4.7 bits. The failure case in step five is the everyday warning: when the number of valid continuations depends on what came before, a configuration count cannot be got by multiplying the menu sizes, and treating it as though it can is how test-matrix and capacity estimates come out wrong.'
},

permutations: {
 q: 'Why does dividing by r! turn ordered selections into unordered ones?',
 a: 'Committees are awkward to count and ordered line-ups are easy, so count the line-ups instead. Every committee then gets counted several times over — once for each order in which its members could have been picked — and, crucially, the same number of times as every other committee. A count in which every target is hit exactly d times is d times the count you actually wanted, so dividing by d recovers it.',
 s: ['Ordered selections first, straight from the product rule: the first pick has n options, the second n − 1 whichever was taken first, and so on for r picks, so P(n,r) = n(n−1)⋯(n−r+1). Multiplying and dividing by (n−r)! writes the same number as n!/(n−r)!.',
   'Now the bridge between the two counts. Take an ordered selection and throw the order away, leaving an r-element set. Every r-element set is produced by at least one ordered selection — write its members down in some order — so no set is missed.',
   'Count how many ordered selections flatten to one particular set S. They are precisely the orderings of the r members of S, and step 1 with n = r counts those as r(r−1)⋯1 = r!. The same r! for every S alike, and that uniformity is what makes the next step legal.',
   'The division principle: the ordered selections split into groups according to which set they flatten to. The groups overlap nowhere and cover everything, and each holds exactly r! members, so the sum rule gives P(n,r) = r! × (number of sets). Hence C(n,r) = P(n,r)/r! = n!/(r!(n−r)!).',
   'Two facts drop out as checks. C(n,r) = C(n,n−r), because naming the r who are in is the very same act as naming the n − r who are out — a one-to-one pairing of the two kinds of choice rather than an algebraic coincidence. And r = n gives C(n,n) = n!/(n!·0!) = 1, which comes out right only because 0! is defined as 1, the same “extend the definition so the law keeps working” move that fixed a⁰ = 1.'],
 m: 'Over-count-then-divide is the standard escape whenever the objects you want are hard to enumerate but each one corresponds to a fixed number of things you can enumerate; it is the counting version of deduplicating by canonical form, where you generate every representation and then divide out the copies that name the same object. As written the formula is a trap in code — 52! overflows a 64-bit integer while C(52,5) = 2,598,960 sits comfortably inside one — so you cancel as you go, multiplying and dividing alternately, or build the value from Pascal’s rule instead. And the size of C(n,r) is what tells you whether checking every r-subset is feasible: C(40,20) is over 137 billion.'
},

probability: {
 q: 'Why do only 23 people give an even chance that two of them share a birthday, when there are 365 days?',
 a: 'The question is not how many people match your birthday but how many pairs of people there are at all, and pairs grow like the square of the crowd — 23 people already make 253 pairs. The clean route is to count the arrangements in which everybody differs, which the counting rules do exactly, and then take the complement. One exponential bound then turns that product into something you can read at a glance.',
 s: ['Fix the model, since a probability is only as honest as its model: k people, n = 365 days, each person’s day chosen uniformly and independently, so all nᵏ lists of birthdays are equally likely and P(event) = (favourable lists)/nᵏ. (Real birthdays are not quite uniform; unevenness only raises the chance of a repeat — a fact assumed here, not proved — so the conclusion is safe.)',
   'Count the lists with no repeat rather than the ones with a repeat, because “at least one shared birthday” is a pile of overlapping cases while its complement is one clean event. The first person has n days available, the second n − 1 whichever the first took, and so on, so the product rule gives n(n−1)⋯(n−k+1) such lists.',
   'Divide by nᵏ, one factor of n per person: P(all k differ) = (n/n)·((n−1)/n)⋯((n−k+1)/n) = (1 − 1/n)(1 − 2/n)⋯(1 − (k−1)/n). The chance of a shared birthday is 1 minus that product.',
   'To read the product, use 1 − x ≤ e⁻ˣ. It holds for every x because g(t) = eᵗ − 1 − t has g′(t) = eᵗ − 1, which is negative below 0 and positive above, so g is smallest at t = 0 where it equals 0; that is eᵗ ≥ 1 + t, and putting t = −x gives what is wanted. Every factor here lies between 0 and 1 while k ≤ n, so the inequalities may be multiplied together: P(all differ) ≤ e^(−(1+2+⋯+(k−1))/n) = e^(−k(k−1)/(2n)), and k(k−1)/2 is exactly the number of pairs.',
   'So the chance of a repeat is at least 1 − e^(−k(k−1)/(2n)), which passes ½ as soon as k(k−1)/(2n) ≥ ln 2. With n = 365 that asks for k(k−1) ≥ 730 ln 2 = 505.997, and k = 23 gives 23 × 22 = 506 — over the line, barely. The crossover therefore sits near k = √(2n ln 2) ≈ 1.18√n, nowhere near n, and the exact probability at 23 people is 50.7%, so the bound was not merely sufficient but tight.'],
 m: '√n rather than n is the entire content of the birthday attack: a 64-bit hash resists collisions not until 2⁶⁴ items but until roughly 1.18 × 2³² ≈ 5 billion of them, which is a number a machine reaches, and that is why collision resistance costs twice the bits of preimage resistance — 256 bits of hash to buy 128 bits of collision safety. The same square root says when randomly assigning jobs to servers starts doubling up, and when a “surely that never happens” UUID clash starts happening. The transferable move is smaller and used more often: “at least one” is nearly always counted through its complement, because the complement is one event and the original is a heap of overlapping ones.'
},

'expected-value': {
 q: 'Why is E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y] even when X and Y are tangled up with each other?',
 a: 'An expectation looks like a sum over values, and that is where the trouble seems to lie, because the values X + Y can take depend on how X and Y conspire. But the same number can be rewritten as a sum over outcomes — one term per possible state of the world — and in that form, adding two random variables merely adds two numbers inside each term. The relationship between them never gets an opportunity to matter.',
 s: ['Set the stage finitely: a list of outcomes ω, each carrying a probability P(ω) ≥ 0, with the probabilities summing to 1. A random variable is nothing more than a number attached to each outcome, a function X(ω), and the event “X = x” is the set of outcomes where that number happens to be x.',
   'Rewrite the definition one level down. E[X] = Σ over values x of x·P(X = x), and P(X = x) is the total probability of the outcomes at which X(ω) = x, so x·P(X = x) = Σ over those outcomes of P(ω)X(ω) — legitimate because on exactly those outcomes the number X(ω) is the number x.',
   'Now sum over every value x. Each outcome has one and only one value of X, so each appears in exactly one group and appears once: E[X] = Σ over all outcomes ω of P(ω)X(ω). Same quantity, now written as a single sum over the whole sample space.',
   'X + Y is by definition the variable whose number at ω is X(ω) + Y(ω). So E[X + Y] = Σ_ω P(ω)(X(ω) + Y(ω)) = Σ_ω P(ω)X(ω) + Σ_ω P(ω)Y(ω) = E[X] + E[Y]. One use of distribution, one regrouping of a finite sum, and the result is reached.',
   'Look at what never appeared: no joint probability, no P(X = x and Y = y), no independence. That is why linearity is free — and precisely what E[XY] = E[X]E[Y] lacks, since that one needs the joint probability to factor as P(X = x)P(Y = y), which is what independence means and what dependent variables refuse to do. The same argument gives E[cX] = cE[X], and repeating it extends the sum to any finite number of terms.'],
 m: 'This licenses the indicator trick, the single most useful manoeuvre in average-case analysis: to count something, write the count as a sum of 0/1 variables, one per opportunity, and add up their probabilities. The expected number of birthday-sharing pairs among 23 people is one indicator per pair — 253/365 = 0.69 — even though the pairs are heavily dependent, since Ann matching Bob and Bob matching Cara forces Ann to match Cara. Randomised quicksort’s expected O(n log n) is the same move over the indicator that two given elements are ever compared, and the expected number of occupied hash buckets, of retries before a request succeeds, and of cache misses across a trace are all this and nothing more. Variance is where the free lunch stops: expanding (X + Y)² leaves a cross-term, so Var[X + Y] = Var[X] + Var[Y] + 2(E[XY] − E[X]E[Y]), and the variances add only when that last bracket vanishes — something independence guarantees and dependence gives no reason to expect.'
},

conditional: {
 q: 'Why can a test that is 99% accurate still be wrong most of the times it says yes?',
 a: 'A positive result arrives from two different crowds: the few who have the disease, nearly all of whom test positive, and the many who do not, a small fraction of whom test positive anyway. A small fraction of a large crowd can easily outnumber nearly all of a tiny one. Bayes’ rule is nothing grander than counting the overlap of “has it” and “tested positive” from both sides and setting the two counts equal.',
 s: ['Conditioning is renormalising. Once B is known to have happened, the outcomes outside B are gone and those inside keep their relative weights, so they must be scaled by whatever makes them total 1 — which is dividing by P(B). Hence P(A|B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B), defined whenever P(B) > 0, and rearranged, P(A ∩ B) = P(A|B)P(B).',
   'A ∩ B and B ∩ A are the same event, so that rearrangement can be made with the roles swapped, provided P(A) > 0 so that the swapped conditional means anything: P(A ∩ B) = P(B|A)P(A). Setting the two expressions for the one intersection equal and dividing by P(B) gives Bayes’ rule, P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B). Every line is the definition being read; no assumption enters beyond those two probabilities being non-zero.',
   'The denominator is usually the piece you do not have, and it splits. A and ¬A cover everything without overlapping, so B breaks into the part inside A and the part outside, and the sum rule gives P(B) = P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|¬A)P(¬A) — with both A and ¬A given non-zero probability, which is what lets the two conditionals be written at all.',
   'Put in numbers. Let A be “has the disease” with base rate P(A) = 0.001, and B “tests positive”, with the test catching 99% of the ill, P(B|A) = 0.99, and falsely flagging 1% of the healthy, P(B|¬A) = 0.01. Then P(B) = 0.99 × 0.001 + 0.01 × 0.999 = 0.00099 + 0.00999 = 0.01098.',
   'So P(A|B) = 0.00099/0.01098 ≈ 0.09: about nine positives in ten are false alarms. Count heads to see where that comes from — among 100,000 people, 100 have the disease and 99 of them test positive, while 99,900 do not and 1% of those, 999 people, test positive regardless. The 999 swamp the 99. The two accuracy figures alone could never have answered the question, because neither of them mentions how rare the disease is, and rarity is the quantity doing the work.'],
 m: 'This is why an alert on a rare failure has to be judged against the base rate rather than admired on its own: a detector with a 1% false-positive rate watching for something that happens on one deploy in a thousand will cry wolf ten times for every real catch, and the on-call engineer will correctly learn to ignore it. Bayes is also the update rule itself — posterior proportional to likelihood times prior — which is spam filtering, fraud scoring and every model that says “given the evidence so far”. Independence is the degenerate corner of the same formula: P(A|B) = P(A) says the evidence moved nothing, which is exactly when P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B) may be written, and assuming it for two servers sharing a power supply is how a redundancy estimate ends up a hundredfold too optimistic.'
},

distributions: {
 q: 'Why does the binomial probability multiply a combination count by pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ?',
 a: 'Write down what an outcome of n trials actually is: a string of successes and failures. Independence fixes the probability of any one string, and the striking part is that this probability depends only on how many successes the string contains, never on where they sit. So every string with k successes carries identical weight, and the probability of “exactly k” is that one weight multiplied by how many such strings exist — which is a counting question already answered.',
 s: ['An outcome of the whole experiment is a string of n letters, S or F, one per trial. Independence says the probability of one particular string is the product of the per-trial probabilities: a factor p for each S and a factor 1 − p for each F.',
   'If the string has k successes, that product has k factors of p and n − k factors of 1 − p, and multiplication does not care about order, so every string with k successes has probability pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ. Positions are irrelevant; only the tally survives.',
   'The event “exactly k successes” is the set of all strings with tally k. Distinct strings are mutually exclusive outcomes, so by the sum rule their probabilities add, and since they are all equal the total is (how many such strings) × pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ.',
   'How many strings? Building one means choosing which k of the n positions carry an S, so there are C(n,k) of them. Hence P(exactly k) = C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ. That nothing has been lost or counted twice is checkable in a line: Σ from k = 0 to n of C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ = (p + (1 − p))ⁿ = 1ⁿ = 1, by the binomial theorem — the probabilities of the n + 1 possible tallies account for the whole of certainty.',
   'The mean needs none of that formula. Write X, the number of successes, as X₁ + ⋯ + Xₙ, where Xᵢ is 1 if trial i succeeds and 0 if not. Then E[Xᵢ] = 1·p + 0·(1−p) = p, and linearity of expectation adds the n of them: E[X] = np. The direct sum Σ k C(n,k)pᵏ(1−p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ has the same value, and there is no reason to fight it.'],
 m: 'Reading the formula as count × weight is what makes it usable rather than decorative: 1000 requests each failing independently at 1% has mean np = 10 and standard deviation √(np(1−p)) ≈ 3.15, so 4 failures in a batch is ordinary while 40 sits nearly ten deviations out and is an incident — that is the arithmetic an alerting threshold should be built on instead of a hunch. It also warns you off evaluating it literally, since C(1000,10)·0.01¹⁰·0.99⁹⁹⁰ multiplies an astronomical number by a vanishing one; library implementations work in logarithms, or lean on the Poisson approximation, which is what the binomial becomes when p is tiny and n is huge with np held to a moderate size. The independence in step 1 is the load-bearing assumption: correlated trials keep the mean at np, because linearity never asked for independence, and wreck the variance, which is why a batch of requests queued behind one failing dependency does not look binomial at all.'
},

bigo: {
 q: 'Why is it safe to throw away constants and lower-order terms — surely 1000n is worse than n?',
 a: 'The definition of Big-O has two escape hatches built into it: a constant multiplier you get to choose after seeing the function, and a threshold beyond which the claim must hold. Constants disappear into the first hatch, small inputs into the second. What makes the discarding honest rather than sloppy is the other half of the argument — no constant, however vast, can carry a function across a class boundary. Nothing that could have changed the answer was thrown away.',
 s: ['Write the definition down and use nothing else: f(n) = O(g(n)) means there is a constant c > 0 and a threshold n₀ such that f(n) ≤ c·g(n) for every n ≥ n₀. Two things are yours to pick after the fact: the constant, and how much small-n behaviour to ignore.',
   'Constants are absorbed by the first choice. If f(n) ≤ c·g(n) beyond n₀, then for any fixed k > 0 we have k·f(n) ≤ (kc)·g(n) beyond that same n₀ — the identical statement with a different constant, and the constant was never pinned down. So 1000n and n make exactly the same claim about growth.',
   'Lower terms are absorbed the same way, because past n = 1 every lower power is bounded by the leading one: n ≤ n² and 1 ≤ n². Hence 3n² + 50n + 7 ≤ 3n² + 50n² + 7n² = 60n² for every n ≥ 1, so c = 60 and n₀ = 1 witness 3n² + 50n + 7 = O(n²). The number 60 carries no meaning; the coefficients simply pooled into one constant.',
   'Now the half that makes the discarding honest — that a constant can never repair a worse growth rate. Suppose n² = O(n). Then n² ≤ c·n for all n beyond some n₀, and dividing by the positive number n gives n ≤ c for all n ≥ n₀. But n eventually exceeds any fixed number, so the supposition is impossible however enormous c was chosen.',
   'The two halves fit together exactly. Constants and lower terms never move a function out of its class, by steps 2 and 3; the leading term always does, by step 4. Big-O discards precisely the information that could not have decided the comparison, and keeps precisely the information that does.'],
 m: 'This is the honest reason a faster machine cannot rescue a bad algorithm: hardware multiplies throughput by a constant, and step 4 showed a constant cannot bridge n² and n — it only postpones the crossover. It also turns the sum rule from a slogan into a proof: if f ≤ c₁·h and g ≤ c₂·h past their thresholds, then f + g ≤ (c₁ + c₂)·h past the later of the two, so the slowest part of a program swallows the rest. The one thing to keep in view is n₀, because the threshold is real — it is why a good sorting library still drops to insertion sort on short subarrays, where the constant has not yet been outvoted.'
},

loops: {
 q: 'Why does a loop whose inner bound grows — 1, then 2, then 3, up to n — still count as O(n²), when it plainly does half the work?',
 a: 'The body runs once for each pair (i, j) with j ≤ i, so the running time is the sum 1 + 2 + ⋯ + n and nothing else. Write that sum out forwards, write it out backwards underneath, and add column by column: every column totals the same, because whatever the top line gains the bottom line loses. A ragged sum has become a rectangle, and a rectangle is easy. Half a rectangle is still a rectangle up to a factor of two — and a factor of two is a constant.',
 s: ['Count executions rather than estimating them. The nest “for i in 1..n, for j in 1..i” runs its body once for each pair (i, j) with 1 ≤ j ≤ i ≤ n, and for a fixed i the inner loop contributes exactly i executions. So the total is S = 1 + 2 + ⋯ + n, exactly.',
   'Write S twice, the second copy reversed: S = 1 + 2 + ⋯ + n and S = n + (n−1) + ⋯ + 1. Add the two lines term by term. The k-th column is k + (n + 1 − k) = n + 1, the same in every column, because stepping one place to the right raises the top line by one and lowers the bottom by one.',
   'There are n columns, each totalling n + 1, so 2S = n(n + 1) and S = n(n + 1)/2. Induction confirms it if you want the check: it holds at n = 1, and n(n+1)/2 + (n+1) = (n+1)(n+2)/2, which is the formula at n + 1.',
   'Expand: S = n²/2 + n/2. The ½ is a constant and n/2 is a lower-order term, and the previous lesson showed neither can move a function out of its class. So S = O(n²), and more than that, S is genuinely quadratic: S ≥ n²/2, so no smaller class will hold it. Against the full nest, which runs n² times, the shrinking inner bound saved n² − n(n+1)/2 = n(n−1)/2 executions — just under half — and a factor of two is a constant.',
   'The same counting settles the other loop shapes with no new ideas. Two loops one after the other run n + n executions, so counts add. One nested inside the other runs n copies of n, so counts multiply: O(n²). And a loop that halves its value each pass stops the first time n/2ᵏ ≤ 1, that is at the first k with 2ᵏ ≥ n, so it runs ⌈log₂ n⌉ times — the logarithm is nothing but the count of halvings.'],
 m: 'The triangular sum is the price of every all-pairs comparison. Written so that each unordered pair is visited once — “for i in 1..n, for j in 1..i−1” — the count is 1 + 2 + ⋯ + (n−1) = n(n−1)/2, which is C(n,2): the same triangle, read as a count of pairs rather than as a sum of rows. So a deduplication pass over a million records that compares every pair does about 5×10¹¹ comparisons, which is why the fix is to hash rather than to make the comparison faster. The proof also tells you when optimising is pointless — halving the work inside an O(n²) loop leaves an O(n²) loop, and the only changes that move the class are ones that change the shape of the nest.'
},

recurrences: {
 q: 'Why does splitting a problem in half and merging come out to n log n, rather than something worse?',
 a: 'Draw the recursive calls as a tree. Going down one level doubles how many pieces you are holding and halves the size of each, so the combining work on every level comes to the same n — the doubling and the halving cancel exactly. That leaves one question: how many levels there are. Halving from n down to 1 takes log₂ n steps, so the total is one level’s work times the number of levels. The algebra below is that picture, with the “and so on” replaced by an induction so it is not doing any hidden work.',
 s: ['Take merge sort’s recurrence: T(1) = 0, and T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n — two subproblems of half the size, plus n work to merge their results. Assume n is a power of two so the halves are exact. That assumption is worth naming rather than hiding, and here it is cheap: T is non-decreasing in n, and every n sits below the next power of two, which is less than 2n, so T(n) ≤ 2n·log₂(2n) — the same class, reached for every n and not only the convenient ones.',
   'Unroll once: T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n. Unroll the inner call: T(n/2) = 2T(n/4) + n/2, so T(n) = 4T(n/4) + n + n. The pattern is visible — after d rounds there are 2ᵈ subproblems of size n/2ᵈ, and together they do 2ᵈ × (n/2ᵈ) = n units of combining work, the same n at every depth.',
   'Prove the pattern rather than trusting the dots. Claim: T(n) = 2ᵈ·T(n/2ᵈ) + d·n for every d from 0 up to log₂ n. It holds at d = 0. If it holds at d, substitute the recurrence into each of the 2ᵈ copies: 2ᵈ(2T(n/2ᵈ⁺¹) + n/2ᵈ) + dn = 2ᵈ⁺¹T(n/2ᵈ⁺¹) + n + dn, which is the claim at d + 1. Induction supplies every d in between.',
   'Run d down to the base case. Subproblems reach size 1 when 2ᵈ = n, that is d = log₂ n. Substituting gives T(n) = n·T(1) + n log₂ n = n log₂ n. The total is literally (work per level) × (number of levels), and the logarithm appears because it counts halvings — nothing else about it is used.',
   'The same unrolling prices any recurrence of this shape, and what decides the answer is how the level costs behave as you descend. T(n) = T(n/2) + 1 keeps one subproblem doing constant work, so every level costs 1 and the total is log₂ n — binary search. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + 1 has level costs 1, 2, 4, ⋯, a geometric series dominated by its final term, so the leaves decide it and the total is n. Level costs equal, growing geometrically, or shrinking geometrically: those three cases are what the master theorem packages, and each is a geometric series you have already summed — the fine print in its general statement is there for combining costs that fall into none of the three patterns.'],
 m: 'This is why the merge step is not where the performance lives. Making the merge twice as fast halves one level’s cost and so halves the total — a constant factor. Changing how many recursive calls you make moves the exponent instead, which is exactly what Karatsuba multiplication does. Schoolbook multiplication splits each number in half and forms four half-size products: T(n) = 4T(n/2) + n, whose level costs 4ᵈ·(n/2ᵈ) = 2ᵈ·n double every level, so the leaves decide it and the total is 4^(log₂ n) = n². Karatsuba assembles the same product from three half-size multiplications: T(n) = 3T(n/2) + n, whose level costs now grow by 3/2 a level instead of 2 — the leaves still decide it, but there are fewer of them, and the total is 3^(log₂ n) = n^(log₂ 3) ≈ n^1.585. The same reading tells you at a glance that a recursion of depth log n holds log n stack frames, which is why merge sort never blows the stack and a badly pivoted quicksort can.'
},

develop: {
 q: 'Why does checking a single iteration of a loop prove the whole loop correct?',
 a: 'A loop invariant is a claim about the state that you show is true before the loop starts and is preserved by one pass. Those two facts are precisely the base case and the inductive step of an induction on the number of completed iterations, so the claim survives every iteration, at whatever count the loop happens to stop. Add a reason the loop stops at all, and the invariant read at that stopping point is the answer you wanted. One iteration checked; all of them concluded.',
 s: ['Fix a loop and a statement I about the program state — the invariant. Establish two things about it: I is true just before the first iteration, and whenever I is true before an iteration whose loop condition holds, I is true again after that iteration finishes.',
   'Let P(k) be “I holds after k completed iterations”. The first fact is P(0); the second says P(k) forces P(k+1) for every k the loop reaches. Induction — which, as the induction lesson showed, rules out a smallest failing case rather than merely persuading — gives P(k) for every k. The loop is therefore never in a state violating I, whatever the input and however many times it runs.',
   'That is only half of correctness, because a loop that never stops satisfies its invariant forever and computes nothing. So supply a measure: a quantity that is always a non-negative integer, strictly decreases on every iteration, and whose reaching zero makes the loop condition fail. A strictly decreasing sequence of non-negative integers cannot continue forever — it would have to pass below zero — so the loop terminates.',
   'Watch it work on something small enough to check every line. To find the maximum of a[0..n−1], with n ≥ 1 so that the array is not empty, set m = a[0] and i = 1, and while i < n do m = max(m, a[i]) then i = i + 1. Invariant: 1 ≤ i ≤ n, and m is the largest of a[0..i−1]. Before the first pass i = 1 and m = a[0], the largest of a one-element stretch, and 1 ≤ 1 ≤ n. If the invariant holds before a pass then i < n, so a[i] exists; comparing makes m the largest of a[0..i], and i + 1 is still at most n — the invariant again with i one higher. The measure n − i is a non-negative integer falling by one each pass.',
   'On exit you hold both facts at once: the invariant is true, and the loop condition is false. The failed condition gives i ≥ n and the invariant gives i ≤ n, so i = n exactly, and m is the largest of a[0..n−1] — which is the specification, so the algorithm is correct. No particular values were ever mentioned, so the argument covers every array simultaneously, including the ones no test suite would think to write down.'],
 m: 'This is the fifth step of the design method with the hand-waving taken out, and it is why the second step — write the brute force first — pays for itself: the slow version is an executable specification the clever version can be checked against on random inputs. The invariant is also the first thing to write down when a loop is wrong, because most off-by-one errors are a mismatch between what the invariant claims at the boundary and what the index arithmetic actually maintains, and the measure argument is what tells you a while loop over a shrinking search range cannot hang. Tests can exhibit a bug and can never establish its absence; the argument is what covers the inputs you did not try.'
},

amortized: {
 q: 'Why is appending to a doubling array honestly O(1) when a single append can copy the entire array?',
 a: 'The expensive appends are rare in a very particular way: each resize costs twice what the previous one cost, and doubling read backwards is halving, so every earlier resize put together costs less than the last one alone. A geometric series whose biggest term sits at the end sums to under twice that term — so all the copying over the array’s whole life comes to under 2n, a constant amount per element. The growth factor is what does the work here; the accounting only reports it.',
 s: ['Fix the rules. The array carries a capacity; an append into a non-full array costs one write; an append into a full array of capacity c first copies those c elements into fresh storage of capacity 2c, costing c, and then writes. Start at capacity 1 and perform n appends.',
   'Resizes are triggered when the array is full at sizes 1, 2, 4, 8, ⋯, so the copies performed are 1 + 2 + 4 + ⋯ + 2ᵐ, where 2ᵐ is the largest such size reached. A resize at size 2ᵐ happens only if an append arrives while the array holds 2ᵐ items, which needs n > 2ᵐ.',
   'Sum that series the way the sequences lesson did: 1 + 2 + ⋯ + 2ᵐ = 2ᵐ⁺¹ − 1 < 2·2ᵐ < 2n. Add the n writes and the total cost of all n appends is under 3n. Divide by n: the cost per append averages under 3 — a constant that does not grow with n, which is the entire claim.',
   'Doubling is what earned that, not the averaging. Grow by a fixed k instead, and resizes happen at sizes k, 2k, 3k, ⋯, so the copies total k + 2k + ⋯ + n, a triangular sum of roughly n²/(2k). Divide by n and the cost per append is about n/(2k), which grows without bound however large k is. The same accounting applied to arithmetic growth correctly reports a disaster, so the accounting is not what is smuggling in the good news.',
   'Nothing anywhere in the argument assumed anything about the data, the order of operations, or a distribution over inputs: every sequence of n appends whatsoever costs under 3n. That is what makes the bound amortised rather than average-case. The banker’s view restates it in a line — charge each append 3 tokens, spend 1 on its write and bank 2; when a full array of capacity c must be copied, the appends made since the previous resize (c/2 of them, once the array has grown past its opening rounds) have banked at least c tokens, which pays for the copy with nothing borrowed and no balance ever negative.'],
 m: 'Every dynamic array in wide use grows geometrically for exactly this reason — Python lists, Go slices, C++ vector, Java ArrayList — and the arguments over whether the factor should be 2 or 1.5 are about reusing freed memory blocks, not about asymptotics, since any factor above 1 gives a geometric series and a constant per element. The bound is also worth reading for what it does not promise: it bounds the total, never the individual operation, so one append in that sequence really does take time proportional to n. When a system cares about the worst single latency rather than the throughput — an audio callback, an order path — amortised O(1) is the wrong guarantee, and the fix is a structure that copies a few old elements on every append instead of all of them at once.'
},

pnp: {
 q: 'Why does someone else proving a problem NP-hard tell you anything about your problem, which nobody has ever studied?',
 a: 'Hardness travels along translations. If you can turn instances of A into instances of B cheaply and without disturbing the answers, then any fast solver for B is also a fast solver for A — translate, then solve. Read backwards, that says B is at least as hard as A. The only technical thing needing care is that “cheap translation followed by fast solve” is still fast overall, and it is, because a polynomial of a polynomial is a polynomial. That one observation is the engine that carries hardness from a handful of problems to tens of thousands.',
 s: ['Two definitions, stated exactly. P is the class of decision problems having an algorithm whose running time on inputs of size n is bounded by some polynomial in n. NP is the class where every yes-instance has a certificate of polynomial size that some polynomial-time algorithm can check. P ⊆ NP falls out immediately: if you can decide the answer yourself in polynomial time, you can check a proposed certificate by ignoring it and deciding.',
   'A polynomial-time reduction from A to B is a transformation f, computable in time at most p(n) on inputs of size n, such that x is a yes-instance of A exactly when f(x) is a yes-instance of B. Write A ≤ₚ B. Nothing has been assumed about how hard either problem is.',
   'Now suppose B has an algorithm running in time q(m) on inputs of size m, where q may be taken non-decreasing — a bound that holds at size m holds at every smaller size too, so replacing q by its running maximum costs nothing. Solve A like this: given x, compute f(x), then run B’s algorithm on it. The answer is correct because f preserves answers. The cost is p(n) for the transformation plus q(|f(x)|) for the solve, and |f(x)| ≤ p(n), because an algorithm that runs for p(n) steps cannot write more than p(n) symbols of output — so the solve costs at most q(p(n)).',
   'So the whole thing costs at most p(n) + q(p(n)). Substituting one polynomial into another yields a polynomial — nᵃ into nᵇ gives n^(ab) — and a sum of polynomials is a polynomial. Hence A ∈ P. Take the contrapositive: if A has no polynomial-time algorithm then neither does B. Hardness flows forward along the arrow, which is why getting the direction of the reduction right is the whole skill.',
   'NP-hard means every problem in NP reduces to it this way, so a polynomial-time algorithm for one NP-hard problem would drag the whole of NP into P. Two things are honestly assumed rather than proved here. That no such algorithm exists — P ≠ NP — is a conjecture, so NP-hardness is transferred evidence of difficulty, not an impossibility theorem. And the transfer needs a first link: Cook and Levin supplied it by showing SAT is NP-hard, encoding the entire step-by-step computation of an arbitrary polynomial-time verifier as a Boolean formula that is satisfiable exactly when some certificate would have been accepted. That encoding is the one genuinely hard construction in the subject and is only sketched here; every later hardness proof is one more application of steps 2 to 4.'],
 m: 'This is what makes an NP-hardness proof a practical decision rather than an academic ornament. It is usually short — exhibit a translation from a known-hard problem into yours — and once it lands, the decades already spent failing to speed up SAT, TSP and knapsack become evidence about your scheduling problem too. What the theorem does not license is despair: it is a statement about the worst case over all inputs and about exact answers, so restricting the inputs you actually receive, accepting a solution with a proved approximation ratio, or handing the instance to a SAT solver that exploits real-world structure are all left open by it — though for some problems a separate hardness-of-approximation theorem closes the middle door, which is a thing to check rather than assume. What it does rule out is the plan of rewriting the same exponential search in a faster language.'
},

representation: {
 q: 'Why does the degree sum come out to exactly twice the edge count, no matter what the graph looks like?',
 a: 'Count one collection in two different ways and the two answers have to agree, because they are counts of the same thing. The collection here is the set of edge-ends: every edge owns two of them, and every vertex has as many sitting on it as its degree. Adding up vertex by vertex gives the degree sum; adding up edge by edge gives 2E; so the two are equal, and the argument never once looks at the shape of the graph.',
 s: ['Work with a simple graph: every edge joins two distinct vertices and no pair is joined twice. Call a pair (v, e) an incidence when e is an edge and v is one of its two endpoints — think of it as one end of one edge, resting on a vertex — and let I be the set of all incidences in the graph.',
   'Count I by grouping the incidences according to their vertex. The incidences at v are exactly the ends of the edges touching v, and there are deg(v) of them, since that is what degree means. So |I| = Σ deg(v), summed over every vertex.',
   'Count the same set I by grouping according to edge instead. Each edge contributes exactly two incidences, one for each of its endpoints, and appears in no other group. So |I| = 2E.',
   'Both lines counted the same finite set, so Σ deg(v) = 2E. Nothing was assumed about connectivity, about degrees or about shape; the only two inputs were “an edge has two ends” and “a vertex’s degree counts the ends resting on it”.',
   'A corollary drops out. 2E is even, so the degree sum is even. Split that sum into the even-degree vertices and the odd-degree ones: the first part is a sum of even numbers and so is even, which forces the second part to be even as well. A sum of odd numbers is even only when there is an even number of them — so every graph has an even number of odd-degree vertices, and a graph with exactly one odd-degree vertex simply cannot exist.'],
 m: 'Double counting — one set, two groupings — is the workhorse of combinatorics, and it pays off here immediately in code. An adjacency list stores each edge at both of its endpoints, so the total length of all the lists is 2E: that is why walking every neighbour of every vertex costs O(V + E) rather than O(V·E), and why the average degree 2E/V is the number that decides between a list and a V×V matrix. The odd-degree corollary is a free integrity check on any graph you parse or generate, and it is the first half of the classic result that a route crossing every bridge exactly once needs either zero or two odd-degree vertices.'
},

paths: {
 q: 'Why is the first time BFS reaches a vertex guaranteed to be by a fewest-edges route, when it never compares one path against another?',
 a: 'BFS empties an entire layer before it begins the next, so it discovers vertices in order of distance rather than in order of luck. The claim to prove is that the kth layer is exactly the set of vertices at distance k — nothing arrives early, nothing arrives late — and the proof is an induction on k in which each half is one line from the definition of distance.',
 s: ['Fix a start vertex s and define d(v) to be the smallest number of edges on any path from s to v. Define the layers the way BFS builds them: L₀ = {s}, and Lₖ₊₁ is every vertex adjacent to something in Lₖ that does not already sit in one of L₀, …, Lₖ. The claim is that Lₖ = {v : d(v) = k} for every k.',
   'Base case: d(v) = 0 means a path of no edges at all from s, which only s has, so {v : d(v) = 0} = {s} = L₀. Now assume as inductive hypothesis that L₀, …, Lₖ are exactly the vertices at distances 0, …, k.',
   'Nothing arrives early. Take v ∈ Lₖ₊₁. It lay in none of L₀, …, Lₖ, and by the hypothesis those layers hold every vertex at distance 0 through k, so d(v) > k. It also has a neighbour u ∈ Lₖ, with d(u) = k, so a shortest path to u with the edge uv tacked on the end reaches v in k + 1 edges — and it repeats no vertex, since every vertex on a shortest path to u is itself at distance at most k while v is not. That is a genuine path, so d(v) ≤ k + 1, and with d(v) > k it pins d(v) = k + 1 exactly.',
   'Nothing arrives late. Take any v with d(v) = k + 1 and a shortest path s = v₀, v₁, …, vₖ₊₁ = v. Its prefix reaches vₖ in k edges, so d(vₖ) ≤ k; and d(vₖ) < k is impossible, since a shorter route to vₖ plus the final edge would beat d(v) = k + 1. So d(vₖ) = k, putting vₖ in Lₖ by the hypothesis, while v itself lies in no layer up to k because its distance is k + 1. Being an undiscovered neighbour of a vertex of Lₖ is precisely the entry condition for Lₖ₊₁, so v ∈ Lₖ₊₁.',
   'The two halves give Lₖ₊₁ = {v : d(v) = k + 1}, and induction carries it to every k. A FIFO queue implements exactly this layering — everything in layer k was enqueued before anything in layer k + 1, so it comes out first — which is why recording d(v) at the moment of first discovery is correct and never needs revising. The whole argument leans on every edge costing 1: with weights, a two-edge route can be cheaper than a one-edge route, the layers stop matching distance, and the plain queue must give way to Dijkstra’s priority queue.'],
 m: 'This is the reason BFS runs in O(V + E) with no correction pass: first discovery is final, so each vertex is settled once and each edge examined once, with no relaxation loop revisiting old decisions. It also tells you exactly where the trick stops working and what replaces it — Dijkstra takes the cheapest unsettled vertex rather than the oldest, and its correctness needs non-negative weights for the same reason, since one negative edge would let a route already dismissed as expensive turn out cheap later. Practically it is why unweighted shortest path never needs Dijkstra’s log factor, and why the parent pointers BFS leaves behind form a shortest-path tree for every reachable vertex at once.'
},

trees: {
 q: 'Why must a tree on V vertices have exactly V − 1 edges — why can it not be one more or one fewer?',
 a: 'Every tree with more than one vertex has a vertex hanging off the end. Snip it off along with its single edge and what remains is a smaller tree: one vertex fewer, one edge fewer. Repeat until a single vertex with no edges is left, and you will have removed exactly one edge for every vertex removed. The one thing that needs proving rather than picturing is that such an end vertex always exists, and the longest path in the tree supplies it.',
 s: ['Take the definitions literally: a tree is connected (some path joins every pair of vertices) and acyclic (no path returns to its start without reusing an edge). We prove E = V − 1 by induction on V. Base case V = 1: a lone vertex is connected, carries no edges, and 1 − 1 = 0.',
   'Now let V ≥ 2 and hunt for a leaf. The graph is finite, so among all simple paths — those repeating no vertex — there is one of greatest length; call it v₀, v₁, …, vₖ. If that longest path had no edges at all, the graph would have no edges whatever, and with V ≥ 2 no path could join two distinct vertices, contradicting connectivity. So k ≥ 1.',
   'v₀ has no neighbour besides v₁. A neighbour u lying off the path would make u, v₀, v₁, …, vₖ a longer simple path, contradicting the choice of longest. A neighbour vᵢ with i ≥ 2 would close the cycle v₀, v₁, …, vᵢ, v₀, contradicting acyclicity. So deg(v₀) = 1, and v₀ is a leaf.',
   'Delete v₀ and its one edge. Deleting can never create a cycle, so what remains is acyclic. It is also still connected: a path between two surviving vertices could only pass through v₀ by entering and leaving along its single edge, which would use that edge twice, and a path does not do that. So no path between survivors ever needed v₀, and every pair of them is still joined.',
   'What remains is therefore a tree on V − 1 vertices with E − 1 edges, and the inductive hypothesis gives E − 1 = (V − 1) − 1, so E = V − 1. Two companion facts follow from the same two definitions: joining two vertices u and v that were not already adjacent creates a cycle, because the tree already held a path from u to v and the new edge closes it; and removing any edge uv disconnects u from v, because a surviving path between them plus the removed edge would have been a cycle in the original tree.'],
 m: 'The count is the stopping rule inside the MST algorithms: Kruskal and Prim both halt after accepting exactly V − 1 edges, and Kruskal’s “add it only if it makes no cycle” test is a union–find query asking whether the two endpoints were already joined. The two companion facts explain why a spanning tree is the leanest connected network there is — deleting an edge that lies on a cycle keeps the graph connected, so repeating that on any connected graph strips it down to a spanning tree, which proves that joining V vertices always costs at least V − 1 edges. It is also why a tree structure with n nodes holds n − 1 parent pointers, and why one extra link anywhere turns a traversal into an infinite loop unless you track what you have visited.'
},

coloring: {
 q: 'Why can every graph be coloured using only one more colour than its busiest vertex has neighbours?',
 a: 'The bound is not an estimate; it is the description of a procedure that cannot get stuck. Colour the vertices one at a time in whatever order you like, always giving the current vertex the lowest-numbered colour that none of its already-coloured neighbours has taken. A vertex has at most Δ neighbours, so at most Δ colours can be blocked — hand it a palette of Δ + 1 and something is always left over.',
 s: ['Write Δ for the largest degree in the graph. A proper colouring gives every vertex a colour so that no edge has the same colour at both ends, and χ(G) is the fewest colours for which one exists. Take a palette of Δ + 1 colours, numbered 1 to Δ + 1.',
   'Fix any order of the vertices v₁, v₂, …, vₙ — any order at all, chosen however you please — and colour them in that order. When vᵢ’s turn comes, look at those of its neighbours that already carry a colour, and give vᵢ the lowest-numbered palette colour not appearing among them.',
   'That step can never fail. vᵢ has at most Δ neighbours in the whole graph, so at most Δ colours are blocked, while the palette holds Δ + 1. At least one colour therefore survives, and there is always something available to choose.',
   'The finished colouring is proper. Take any edge: one of its two endpoints was coloured later than the other, and at that later moment the earlier endpoint already carried its colour, so the rule explicitly avoided it. Every edge gets checked exactly once, when its second endpoint is reached, and none is missed.',
   'The procedure terminates with every vertex coloured out of Δ + 1 colours, so χ(G) ≤ Δ + 1 for every graph whatsoever. The bound is genuinely attained: the complete graph on n vertices has Δ = n − 1 and needs all n colours, since every pair is adjacent; and a cycle of odd length has Δ = 2 and needs 3, since alternating two colours round an odd loop leaves the last vertex clashing with the first. For connected graphs those two families are the only ones that attain it — that is Brooks’ theorem, a real result that this argument does not give you.'],
 m: 'Register allocation is this exact procedure, and the argument tells you two practical things at once: with Δ + 1 registers a greedy pass can never stall, and the number of colours it actually spends depends on the order, so what you get is an upper bound rather than the true χ. That matters because computing χ exactly is NP-hard, so real allocators spend their effort on the ordering — repeatedly pulling out a vertex with fewer than k live neighbours and colouring it last as it is pushed back, which is step three again with k in place of Δ + 1 — and spill to memory only when no such vertex remains. Be warned about the other line in the table: “planar implies four colours” is not provable by any argument of this size, having been settled by a computer check of well over a thousand configurations. The honest hand-sized result is six colours, which follows by induction from the fact that every simple planar graph has a vertex of degree at most 5, and with considerably more work, five.'
},

dags: {
 q: 'Why does having no cycles guarantee that a valid build order exists, rather than merely failing to rule one out?',
 a: 'One direction is free: a cycle would force some task to be scheduled before itself. The other direction needs a construction, and all of it rests on a single observation — in a finite graph with no cycles, something always has no prerequisites. Walk backwards along the arrows and you must eventually either stop or revisit a vertex, and revisiting is precisely a cycle. Put that prerequisite-free vertex first, delete it, repeat: that is simultaneously the proof and Kahn’s algorithm.',
 s: ['A topological order is a listing of all the vertices in which every edge u → v has u appearing before v. The easy direction first: if the graph holds a cycle v₁ → v₂ → ⋯ → vₖ → v₁, each edge demands that its tail come before its head, so v₁ would come before v₂, before …, before v₁ — that is, strictly before itself. No listing does that, so a graph with a cycle has no topological order at all.',
   'The key lemma for the other direction: a finite directed graph with no cycle has at least one vertex with no incoming edge. Suppose instead that every vertex had an incoming edge. Then you could start anywhere and walk backwards — from uᵢ step to any uᵢ₊₁ with an edge uᵢ₊₁ → uᵢ — and the walk could always continue, never being forced to stop.',
   'Continue that walk for V steps, producing V + 1 vertices u₀, u₁, u₂, … in order. Only V distinct vertices exist, so by pigeonhole two of them coincide; take the first repetition, uᵢ = uⱼ with i < j and uᵢ, …, uⱼ₋₁ all distinct. Read that stretch forwards: uⱼ → uⱼ₋₁ → ⋯ → uᵢ is a cycle, contradicting acyclicity. So a vertex with no incoming edge must exist.',
   'Now induct on the number of vertices. With one vertex the single-item listing works. Otherwise take a source s supplied by the lemma, and delete it together with its outgoing edges; deleting can never create a cycle, so what remains is an acyclic graph on V − 1 vertices, which by the inductive hypothesis has a topological order.',
   'List s first, then that order, and check every edge of the original graph against it. Edges leaving s point into the remainder, all of which comes after s. Edges inside the remainder are respected by the inductive order. Edges into s do not exist, by the choice of s. So the listing is a topological order — and combined with step one, a topological order exists exactly when the graph is acyclic.'],
 m: 'Kahn’s algorithm is step four executed rather than argued: keep every vertex’s in-degree, emit any vertex whose in-degree has fallen to zero, decrement its successors, and repeat — O(V + E), because each edge is decremented once. Step three is what lets the tool give a useful error rather than a shrug: if the algorithm stalls with vertices left over, every remaining vertex has an incoming edge from within the leftovers, so that subgraph provably contains a cycle, and walking backwards from any of its vertices finds one to print. The non-uniqueness matters too — several vertices can have in-degree zero simultaneously, and that set is exactly the work a parallel build may run at the same time. The same acyclicity is what makes memoised recursion terminate: a dependency graph with a cycle is a computation waiting on itself.'
},

bipartite: {
 q: 'Why is “every k workers between them know at least k shifts” enough to guarantee everyone gets assigned, and not merely necessary?',
 a: 'That the condition is necessary is a counting triviality — k matched workers occupy k distinct shifts, every one of which they must know. Sufficiency is the theorem, and it turns on a single question: is some group of workers exactly at the limit, knowing no more shifts than it has members? If no group is that tight, every group has slack, and you may match one worker arbitrarily because the slack absorbs the damage. If some group S is tight, it must consume its shifts entirely, so solve S on its own and then solve the rest with those shifts removed — and tightness is exactly what makes the condition survive on both halves.',
 s: ['Setup. The graph has workers A on one side and shifts B on the other, with edges only across. For S ⊆ A write N(S) for the set of shifts known by at least one member of S. A complete matching of A pairs every worker with a shift he knows, no two workers sharing a shift. Hall’s condition says |N(S)| ≥ |S| for every S ⊆ A.',
   'Necessity. Given a complete matching, the |S| partners of the workers in S are distinct shifts, and each is known by its own worker, so each lies in N(S). Hence |N(S)| ≥ |S|. This is what fails for the two workers who both know only shift A: there |N(S)| = 1 < 2 = |S|, so no clever assignment can exist.',
   'Sufficiency, by induction on |A|, assuming Hall’s condition holds throughout. Base case |A| = 1: taking S = A gives |N(A)| ≥ 1, so the single worker has a shift to take. For |A| ≥ 2 assume the theorem for every smaller left side, and split on whether some non-empty proper subset of A is tight.',
   'Case one: every non-empty S ⊊ A has slack, |N(S)| ≥ |S| + 1. Pick any worker a — he knows at least two shifts, since {a} is such a subset — pick any shift b that he knows, match them, and delete both from the graph. For any non-empty S ⊆ A ∖ {a}, deleting b removes at most one shift from N(S), leaving at least (|S| + 1) − 1 = |S| of them. So Hall’s condition still holds on a left side of size |A| − 1, and the inductive hypothesis matches everybody else.',
   'Case two: some non-empty S ⊊ A is tight, |N(S)| = |S|. Every subset of S inherits Hall’s condition from the whole graph, so induction gives S a complete matching into N(S), which by equal counts uses up every shift in N(S). Now delete S and N(S) and test Hall on what is left. Take any T ⊆ A ∖ S. A neighbourhood of a union is always the union of the neighbourhoods, so N(S ∪ T) = N(S) ∪ N(T); and because S and T are disjoint |S ∪ T| = |S| + |T|, so Hall applied to S ∪ T gives |N(S) ∪ N(T)| ≥ |S| + |T|. Discounting the |S| shifts of N(S) leaves at least |T| shifts known to T lying outside N(S) — exactly T’s neighbours in the reduced graph. So Hall’s condition holds for A ∖ S there, and induction, on a left side smaller than A because S is non-empty, matches it. The two matchings use disjoint workers and disjoint shifts, so together they match all of A — and as the two cases exhaust the possibilities, the induction is complete.'],
 m: 'The theorem is what lets a matching algorithm explain itself. When an augmenting-path method such as Hopcroft–Karp stops short of matching everybody, take a worker u left unmatched and collect S, the workers reachable from him along alternating paths: every shift adjacent to S is already matched, to a worker who is himself in S, so |N(S)| = |S| − 1 and the output is not a bare “no solution” but a certificate anyone can check — these k people between them cover only k − 1 slots. Gather S from all the unmatched workers at once and (A ∖ S) ∪ N(S) is a smallest vertex cover, which is precisely the minimum cut of the max-flow formulation, and why assignment, shift scheduling and ad-slot allocation collapse to one algorithm with one diagnosis. The lesson’s other equivalence has a shorter reason: a cycle in a bipartite graph alternates between the two sides, so it must have even length; and conversely, run BFS and colour each vertex by the parity of its layer, and if any edge joins two vertices of equal parity, the two tree paths back to where they last met, plus that edge, close a cycle of odd length — so a graph is two-colourable exactly when it holds no odd cycle.'
},

'binary-search': {
 q: 'Why is it safe to throw away half the array when you have looked at only one element of it?',
 a: 'The algorithm never claims to know where the target is. It keeps a much weaker promise — if the target is anywhere in the array at all, it is inside the current window — and that promise is cheap to maintain. Sortedness is what makes a single comparison enough to shrink the window without breaking the promise, and the promise still standing when the window empties is what makes a “not found” answer trustworthy rather than a shrug.',
 s: ['State the promise precisely, as something to be checked before and after every pass: if the target occurs in the array, its index lies in the window lo … hi. Before the first pass the window is the whole array, so it plainly holds.',
   'Assume it holds at the start of a pass and look at a[mid] for any mid inside the window. If a[mid] equals the target we have found an occurrence and stopped, so only two cases remain.',
   'If a[mid] < target then, because the array is sorted, every index i ≤ mid has a[i] ≤ a[mid] < target, so no index at or below mid can hold the target. Discarding them leaves the window mid+1 … hi and the promise survives untouched. The mirror argument covers a[mid] > target and the window lo … mid−1. Sortedness is used exactly here and nowhere else — on an unsorted array this step is simply false, and the method collapses with it.',
   'Termination, and what an empty window means. mid always lies inside the window, so each pass discards at least the element at mid and the width strictly decreases; a positive whole number cannot decrease forever, so eventually lo > hi and the window is empty. The promise still holds, and an empty window cannot contain the target — so the target is genuinely absent, and returning NOT_FOUND is a conclusion rather than a guess.',
   'The count comes from the same shrinking. Taking mid at the middle leaves at most ⌊w/2⌋ of a window of width w, so after k passes the width is at most n/2ᵏ, and the loop runs only while the width is at least 1. So it runs at most as many times as you can halve n before reaching 1: ⌊log₂ n⌋ + 1 comparisons, which is 10 for a thousand items and 20 for a million.'],
 m: 'The promise is a loop invariant, and this is the template for arguing any loop correct: name a property true at the start, show one pass preserves it, then combine it with the exit condition to get the answer you wanted. The argument also shows that binary search needs less than a sorted array — it needs only some yes/no test that flips from no to yes exactly once across the range, because that is all step three ever used. This is why “binary-search the answer” works: the smallest buffer that fits, the highest request rate the service survives, the first commit that broke a test. None of them involve a sorted array, and all of them halve just as fast.'
},

sorting: {
 q: 'Why can no comparison sort ever beat n log n — how can anyone rule out an algorithm nobody has invented yet?',
 a: 'You can reason about all of them at once by looking only at what they are permitted to learn. An algorithm that touches the data solely through comparisons hears one bit per question, and it must end up telling apart the n! possible orderings of its input. Counting how many outcomes a fixed number of yes/no answers can possibly distinguish gives the bound, and no cleverness inside the algorithm can manufacture outcomes it never asked about.',
 s: ['Fix n distinct items and take any algorithm whose only access to their values is asking “is this one smaller than that one?”. Its next action at every moment depends only on the answers heard so far, so its entire behaviour is a binary tree: each internal node is one comparison, its two edges are the two possible answers, and each leaf is the rearrangement the algorithm finally performs.',
   'Two different input orderings cannot end at the same leaf. If they did, the algorithm would have heard identical answers throughout and so would apply the identical rearrangement to both — and one fixed rearrangement of positions puts the items in order for at most one of two different orderings. Since all n! orderings must be sorted correctly, the tree has at least n! leaves.',
   'A binary tree in which no path has more than h edges has at most 2ʰ leaves. Induct on h: at h = 0 the tree is a single leaf, and a tree of height h is a root with at most two subtrees of height at most h−1, giving at most 2·2ʰ⁻¹ = 2ʰ. So 2ʰ ≥ n!, which is h ≥ log₂(n!).',
   'Bound log₂(n!) from below with no approximation formula. Among the factors n, n−1, …, 2, 1, keep only the larger half, those from ⌈n/2⌉ up to n: there are more than n/2 of them and each is at least n/2, while every discarded factor is at least 1. Hence n! ≥ (n/2)^(n/2), and taking logarithms gives log₂(n!) ≥ (n/2)·log₂(n/2) = (n/2)(log₂ n − 1).',
   'h is the length of the longest path, which is the number of comparisons the algorithm makes on its worst input. So every comparison sort whatsoever is forced, on some input, to make at least (n/2)(log₂ n − 1) comparisons, an amount growing like n log n. Merge sort spends about n log₂ n on every input, so the bound is attained: merge sort is not merely good but optimal in this model, up to a constant factor.'],
 m: 'A lower bound is permission to stop searching, which is why a standard library spends its effort on constants, cache behaviour and small-case cutoffs rather than hunting for an asymptotically faster comparison sort. It also states exactly how to go faster, which is to leave the model: counting sort and radix sort never compare two keys, they use key values directly as addresses, so the tree argument says nothing about them and they can run in O(n) when the keys are small integers. And it names the currency the whole course keeps returning to — one comparison is one bit, naming which of n! orderings you were handed takes log₂(n!) bits, and no algorithm can pay out fewer bits than its answer costs.'
},

hashing: {
 q: 'Why is a hash lookup called O(1) when the whole table might, for all you know, be piled into a single bucket?',
 a: 'The constant-time claim is an average, and — this is the part usually left unsaid — an average over the choice of hash function rather than over the data. Once that is pinned down the argument is short: a lookup costs one probe plus one step for each other key sharing the bucket, and the expected number of sharers is a sum of small collision probabilities. Adding them needs nothing beyond the fact that averages add.',
 s: ['Fix the key x being looked up. With chaining, the work is walking x\'s bucket: one step for the bucket itself, plus one step for each other stored key that landed there. So the entire question is the average size of that pile.',
   'State the assumption plainly, because it does all the work and it is not free. Choose the hash function h at random from a family with the property that for any two distinct keys x and y, the probability that h(x) = h(y) is at most 1/m. That is what a universal family means; such families exist and are cheap to build. It is an assumption about the function you picked, not a hope about the data you were sent.',
   'For each stored key y ≠ x, write Xᵧ = 1 if h(y) = h(x) and 0 otherwise. The number of keys sharing x\'s bucket is exactly the sum of the Xᵧ, and the average value of a quantity that is only ever 0 or 1 is precisely the probability that it is 1 — so each Xᵧ averages at most 1/m.',
   'The average of a sum is the sum of the averages, always, whether or not the terms influence one another. With at most n keys stored, the expected pile is therefore at most n/m = α, the load factor, and the expected cost of a lookup is at most 1 + α.',
   'Nothing in that bound mentions n by itself; it mentions only the ratio n/m. So resizing whenever α climbs past a fixed ceiling such as 0.75 — doubling m and rehashing — holds 1 + α below a constant forever, and the lookup stays O(1) on average however large the table grows.'],
 m: 'What the argument does not claim matters as much as what it does. It bounds an average, and the worst case really is Θ(n), reached when every key lands in one bucket. That is not hypothetical: an attacker who knows your hash function can compute such a set of keys and submit them as form fields, which is why production hash functions are seeded with a per-process random value — that seed is what restores the “chosen at random” premise step two needed. The doubling in step five is paid for by the geometric-series argument from the sequences track: rehashing at sizes 1, 2, 4, …, n moves fewer than 2n keys over the table\'s whole life, a constant per insertion. And the pile in step three is pigeonhole\'s revenge — once n > m some bucket must hold more than one key, so collisions are never a defect to be engineered away, only a cost to be kept small.'
},

'graph-algos': {
 q: 'Why can Dijkstra declare a vertex finished the moment it comes off the queue, without waiting to see the rest of the graph?',
 a: 'Any route to that vertex not yet discovered has to leave the settled region somewhere, and the moment it does it stands at an unsettled vertex whose tentative distance has already been recorded. The popped vertex had the smallest tentative distance of all unsettled vertices, so that escape point is at least as far off — and with no negative edges the remainder of the route can only add to the total. The undiscovered route therefore cannot be shorter, and there is nothing left to wait for.',
 s: ['Set up the bookkeeping. d[v] is the length of the best route to v found so far, with d[start] = 0 and everything else ∞. Each round pops the unsettled vertex u of smallest d, marks it settled, and for every edge u→v replaces d[v] by d[u] + w(u,v) if that is smaller. Every weight w is assumed to be at least 0, and d values only ever decrease.',
   'The claim, proved by induction on the order in which vertices are settled: at the moment u is popped, d[u] is already the true shortest distance from start. Check the first pop first, because the induction has to start somewhere: it is start itself with d = 0, and since no edge has negative weight no route can measure less than 0, so 0 really is the shortest distance and the claim holds. For any later pop u, assume the claim held for every vertex settled earlier, and suppose for contradiction that some genuine path P from start to u is strictly shorter than d[u].',
   'Walk along P. It begins at start, which was settled in the very first round, and ends at u, which is not settled yet, so somewhere it steps from a settled vertex x to an unsettled vertex y; take the first such step.',
   'Two facts meet at y. First, x was settled in an earlier round, and settling x relaxed the edge x→y, so from that round onwards d[y] ≤ d[x] + w(x,y); d values never rise, so it still holds now. Second, the induction hypothesis says d[x] is the true shortest distance to x, and P\'s own prefix from start to x is one particular route there, so d[x] is at most the length of that prefix — an inequality, not an equality, since P need not approach x the cheapest way. Chaining the two gives d[y] ≤ (length of P as far as x) + w(x,y) = length of P as far as y.',
   'The remainder of P, from y onwards to u, is built from edges of weight at least 0, so it can subtract nothing: length of P ≥ length of P as far as y ≥ d[y]. Combined with the supposition that P is shorter than d[u], this gives d[y] < d[u]. But y is unsettled, and u was chosen as the unsettled vertex with the smallest d, so d[u] ≤ d[y]. The two statements cannot both hold, so no shorter P exists and d[u] was already correct — and the induction carries the conclusion to every vertex in turn. Non-negativity was used twice and nowhere else: once in step two to know that d[start] = 0 cannot be beaten, and once in the first sentence here. Allow a negative edge and a long prefix can be redeemed by a discount further along, so y can sit genuinely further out than u while lying on a shorter route to it, and popping u settles nothing at all.'],
 m: 'The proof doubles as the specification. Dijkstra is precisely the algorithm resting on “a settled distance can never later be improved”, so any change that breaks that premise breaks the algorithm rather than merely slowing it. Negative weights need Bellman–Ford, which settles nothing and instead relaxes every edge V−1 times, one round for each edge a shortest path could possibly contain. A* adds to each key a heuristic estimate h of the distance still to go, and it is worth being exact about what the argument demands of h: not merely that h never overestimates, but that it is consistent, h(x) ≤ w(x,y) + h(y) on every edge — which is precisely the statement that the reweighted costs w(x,y) + h(y) − h(x) are still non-negative, so the tail of P still subtracts nothing and step five survives. A heuristic that only never overestimates does still return a shortest path, but only if the algorithm is permitted to reopen a vertex it had already settled, which is the guarantee consistency buys back. The proof also explains the shared skeleton in the lesson\'s code: the same loop with a stack goes depth-first and with a queue is BFS, because when every edge costs the same, a plain queue already is a priority queue.'
},

dp: {
 q: 'Why does writing answers down turn an exponential recursion into a linear one — where was all that work actually going?',
 a: 'The naive recursion is not doing an exponential amount of thinking; it is doing an exponential amount of repeating. Count the nodes of its call tree and the same small handful of subproblems appears over and over, in numbers that grow as fast as the answer being computed. A table makes the second and every later request for a subproblem free, so the total collapses from the number of calls to the number of distinct calls.',
 s: ['Count the naive calls exactly rather than estimating. Let L(n) be the number of base-case calls that fib(n) makes. L(1) = L(2) = 1, and otherwise L(n) = L(n−1) + L(n−2) — the same recurrence with the same starting values, so L(n) = Fₙ, the nth Fibonacci number itself.',
   'Every non-base call makes exactly two further calls, so the call tree is a binary tree whose internal nodes all have two children, and such a tree has exactly one fewer internal node than it has leaves. The total number of calls is therefore 2Fₙ − 1: the algorithm is as slow as its own answer is large, and by the recurrences lesson Fₙ grows like φⁿ with φ ≈ 1.618.',
   'Yet only n distinct subproblems ever occur — fib(1) through fib(n). So the exponential is not depth and not difficulty, it is duplication: fib(n−2) is computed by both branches, fib(n−3) by three separate calls, and the multiplicity keeps compounding on the way down.',
   'Now add the table and follow a single subproblem k. The first call to fib(k) that gets past the table check runs the body and, on its way out, stores the answer; every later call to fib(k) finds that entry and returns at the check, doing constant work. So the body runs at most once per distinct k — at most n times in total, no matter how many times it is called.',
   'Price the computation by charging each unit of work to the state whose body performed it. The total is (number of distinct states) × (work per state), plus the constant-time work of calls that returned at the check, and those number at most two per body execution. For fib that is n states at constant work each, so O(n). This is the general accounting for every dynamic program, written top-down with a memo or bottom-up over a table: count the states, price one, multiply.'],
 m: '“States × work per state” is what lets you cost a dynamic program before writing it, and it is where the design decisions become visible: edit distance has n·m states at constant work each, so O(nm), and shrinking the state is the only way to shrink the bill. The argument also fixes what a state has to be — the key must determine the answer completely, so if a value depends on how you arrived rather than where you are, the key is missing a parameter and the table will hand back confident nonsense. And it prices only what it prices: this shows a dynamic program is fast, not that it is right. Correctness is the separate claim of optimal substructure, that an optimal whole is assembled from optimal parts, and it is proved by cut and paste — if a better sub-solution existed you could splice it in and improve the whole, so the supposed optimum was not one.'
},

greedy: {
 q: 'Why should taking the best-looking option right now ever produce the best possible finish, and how would you prove it rather than hope?',
 a: 'A greedy choice is safe when you can show that any optimal solution may be edited to contain it without getting worse. Then the greedy first move is not a gamble — it is a move some optimal solution was making anyway, and what remains is a smaller problem of exactly the same kind. Interval scheduling is where this can be carried out in full, and it is also where you can see precisely which property the argument leans on, the property coin change turns out to lack.',
 s: ['The problem: from a set of intervals, each with a start and a finish, choose as many as possible that do not overlap. The greedy rule: among the intervals still compatible with what you have already taken, always take the one that finishes earliest.',
   'The exchange lemma — some optimal solution contains g, the earliest-finishing interval of all. Take any optimal solution O and let f be its earliest-finishing member. Any other member j of O does not overlap f, so either j finishes at or before f starts, or j starts at or after f finishes; the first case would put j\'s finish strictly before f\'s, which is what choosing f ruled out. So every member of O other than f starts at or after f finishes.',
   'Now swap g in for f. Since g finishes no later than f, every one of those other members starts at or after g finishes too, so all of them are compatible with g. And g is not among them: a member other than f starts after f finishes, so it cannot finish as early as g does, unless it is f itself. Replacing f by g therefore leaves a valid selection of exactly the same size, and so an optimal one.',
   'Committing to g leaves a subproblem of the same shape, and induction finishes the job. The intervals still usable are precisely those starting at or after g finishes, and an optimal solution containing g must pair g with an optimal selection of that residual set — a larger residual selection could be spliced in to beat the optimum, which is impossible. Induct on the number of intervals: with none available greedy returns the empty set, which is optimal; otherwise the lemma supplies an optimal solution of the form g plus an optimum for the residual set, and greedy\'s remaining picks are exactly what the rule does on that strictly smaller residual set, which by the induction hypothesis is an optimum for it. Greedy\'s answer therefore has the same size as an optimal one, which is what being optimal means.',
   'Every line above rested on one concrete fact: an interval finishing earlier rules out no future interval that a later-finishing one would have permitted, so the greedy choice leaves a superset of the options. That is a property of this particular problem and not a law of greed. Take coins {1, 3, 4} and the amount 6. The greedy first move takes 4 and leaves 2, which cannot be made with fewer than two more coins — three coins altogether. Taking 3 first leaves 3, finished by a single further coin — two coins altogether. No exchange lemma is available here, because the largest coin does not leave the richest remainder. Greed is a proof obligation, not a strategy.'],
 m: 'This is why the greedy algorithms that survive in libraries all carry the same two-part paper trail — an exchange argument for the first choice, an induction for everything after it — and why Kruskal, Huffman, Dijkstra and interval scheduling are on that list while 0/1 knapsack and longest path are not. The danger is that a failing greedy fails quietly: coin change over {1, 3, 4} returns three coins, raises nothing and looks entirely reasonable, so the proof is the only defence you have. And when the exchange lemma cannot be established, the subproblem structure from step four is still there to be exploited — which is exactly what dynamic programming does, trying every possible first choice instead of betting everything on one.'
},

vectors: {
 q: 'Why is the length of a 768-dimensional vector still √(Σvᵢ²), when nobody can picture the triangle?',
 a: 'You do not need to see the space to reason inside it. Any three points lie in a single plane, and inside a plane ordinary geometry applies unchanged — so peel one coordinate off at a time. A point in n dimensions, its shadow with the last coordinate flattened to zero, and the origin form a right triangle in some plane, and Pythagoras there trades an n-dimensional length for an (n−1)-dimensional one plus a square. Repeat until nothing is left, and the sum of squares is simply what has piled up.',
 s: ['Take as given three facts you can draw and check in three dimensions. The coordinate axes are mutually perpendicular. A line perpendicular to every axis of a flat region is perpendicular to every line in that region — a pole square to two floorboards stands square to the whole floor. And inside any single plane the geometry is the plane geometry you already know, Pythagoras included. Everything below is those three facts applied over and over.',
   'Let P = (v₁, v₂, …, vₙ) and let Q = (v₁, …, vₙ₋₁, 0) be its shadow with the last coordinate flattened to zero. The origin O, the point Q and the point P lie in one plane, because any three points do.',
   'The segment from Q to P is parallel to the nth axis — only the last coordinate changes along it — while the segment from O to Q lies entirely inside the flat region where the last coordinate is zero. By the second fact the nth axis is perpendicular to every line in that region, and so is anything parallel to it, so the corner at Q is a right angle, with legs of length ‖OQ‖ and |vₙ|.',
   'Pythagoras inside that plane gives ‖OP‖² = ‖OQ‖² + vₙ². An n-dimensional length has been reduced to an (n−1)-dimensional one, at the cost of one square.',
   'Now do the same to Q, which has only n−1 live coordinates, then to its shadow, and so on down to the one-dimensional case, where the length of (v₁) is |v₁| and its square is v₁². Each round deposits exactly one square, so ‖v‖² = v₁² + v₂² + ⋯ + vₙ² and ‖v‖ = √(Σvᵢ²). That is induction on the dimension: the base case is the line, and the inductive step is one right triangle.'],
 m: 'Every geometric word used about embeddings — length, distance, direction, unit vector — is defined by this one formula, and evaluating it costs a single pass of n multiply-adds and one square root, however large n is. Normalising a non-zero v by dividing by ‖v‖ is legitimate for exactly the reason it was in the right-triangle lesson: scaling every coordinate by a positive c scales the length by c, because c leaves each square as c², so dividing by ‖v‖ discards size and leaves direction untouched. The argument also runs the other way, and that reading is worth having. A course that simply declares √(Σvᵢ²) to be the definition of length in n dimensions is not exercising a free choice — the argument above shows it is the only definition that agrees with Pythagoras inside every plane, so the definition is forced rather than stipulated. Where the picture stops helping is worth being clear about too: the algebra is exact in 768 dimensions, the intuition you carry from three is not, and the next lesson supplies the instrument, the angle, that keeps working after your mental image has given up.'
},

dotproduct: {
 q: 'Why does multiplying matching components and adding them up have anything at all to do with the angle between two vectors?',
 a: 'Σuᵢvᵢ is pure arithmetic and cos θ is pure geometry, so an identity linking them has to be a bridge, and there is only one place the two sides can meet: the third side of the triangle that u and v span. Its length can be computed twice over — once geometrically by the law of cosines, once algebraically by expanding a sum of squares — and setting the two computations equal leaves the identity standing with everything else cancelled.',
 s: ['Take u and v both non-zero, and draw them from a common origin; if either were the zero vector there would be no ray and so no angle to speak of. Two things carry over from the previous lesson: length is √(Σ of squares), and the distance between two points is the length of the vector between them, since sliding both points by the same amount changes no coordinate difference. Their two tips and the origin are three points, so they lie in a plane, and inside that plane they form a triangle with sides ‖u‖, ‖v‖ and ‖u − v‖. Define θ to be the angle of that triangle at the origin — defined geometrically, with no dot product anywhere in the definition.',
   'The geometric computation: the law of cosines applied to that triangle gives ‖u − v‖² = ‖u‖² + ‖v‖² − 2‖u‖‖v‖cos θ. It was proved in the trig track entirely inside a plane, from coordinates and cos² + sin² = 1, so it is available in any plane sitting inside any number of dimensions. If the three points happen to be collinear the triangle is degenerate; check θ = 0 and θ = π against the formula directly and it survives both.',
   'The algebraic computation: by the length formula from the previous lesson, ‖u − v‖² = Σ(uᵢ − vᵢ)² = Σuᵢ² − 2Σuᵢvᵢ + Σvᵢ² = ‖u‖² + ‖v‖² − 2(u·v).',
   'The two lines compute the same number. Cancel the shared ‖u‖² + ‖v‖² and divide by −2: u·v = ‖u‖‖v‖cos θ. Nothing anywhere assumed a particular number of dimensions.',
   'Divide through by ‖u‖‖v‖ — legitimate precisely because both were assumed non-zero — and cos θ is left standing alone, with both lengths gone. That is cosine similarity, and it is why it reports direction and nothing else. Two consequences follow immediately: u·v = 0 exactly when cos θ = 0, so perpendicularity has an arithmetic test needing no angles at all; and since |cos θ| ≤ 1 always, |u·v| ≤ ‖u‖‖v‖ always, which is precisely what pins cosine similarity inside [−1, 1]. The zero vector is the one case the geometry cannot reach, and it is handled by convention: its dot product with everything is 0, and cosine similarity against it is simply left undefined.'],
 m: 'Cosine similarity is now defensible rather than merely conventional: it is the cosine of a real angle, so doubling an embedding\'s magnitude cannot change how similar it is to anything, because the length divides out. The identity also explains what a layer is doing. y = Wx is one dot product per row, so each row of W is a direction asking the input "how much of you points along me?", and a large output means alignment rather than mere size — which is why rows of a weight matrix can be read as learned features. Nothing here is circular: θ was defined as an angle in a triangle rather than through the dot product, and the law of cosines it leans on was itself derived from coordinates and cos² + sin² = 1, with no dot products involved, so the trig track really was paying into this the whole way along.'
},

matrices: {
 q: 'Why is matrix multiplication that peculiar row-times-column rule, instead of simply multiplying entry by entry?',
 a: 'A matrix is not a grid of numbers that happens to support an operation. It is bookkeeping for a function, and the product is defined to be the bookkeeping for doing one function and then the other. Once you insist on that, there is no freedom left anywhere: write out what B(Ax) actually is, component by component, and the row-times-column rule falls out, with the shape rule and the failure of commutativity attached to it.',
 s: ['Assume only that the map is linear: A(x + y) = Ax + Ay and A(cx) = c(Ax). Rotation was shown to have exactly these two properties in the trig track, and a neural layer, before its bias and its activation, has them too.',
   'A linear map is completely pinned down by what it does to the n basis directions e₁, …, eₙ, since every x is x₁e₁ + ⋯ + xₙeₙ and linearity forces Ax = x₁(Ae₁) + ⋯ + xₙ(Aeₙ). So store the vectors Ae₁, …, Aeₙ as the columns of a grid — write aᵢⱼ for component i of Aeⱼ — and nothing about A has been lost. Reading off component i of that sum gives (Ax)ᵢ = Σⱼ aᵢⱼxⱼ, which is row i dotted with x.',
   'Now compose. Let C be the map "do A, then do B", so that Cx = B(Ax) for every x. Apply step 2 twice: (Cx)ᵢ = Σₖ bᵢₖ(Ax)ₖ = Σₖ bᵢₖ(Σⱼ aₖⱼxⱼ).',
   'Swap the order of the two finite sums — legal, because it is the same finite list of products added up in a different order — and collect the coefficient of each xⱼ: (Cx)ᵢ = Σⱼ (Σₖ bᵢₖaₖⱼ)xⱼ. To read the entry off with no ambiguity, feed in x = eⱼ, which kills every term but one: (Ceⱼ)ᵢ = Σₖ bᵢₖaₖⱼ, and by step 2 that is exactly the entry cᵢⱼ. It is row i of B dotted with column j of A. The rule was never chosen; it was computed.',
   'Two of the remaining rules come free. The index k runs over the columns of B and the rows of A simultaneously, so those counts must agree — that is the shape rule (m×n)(n×p) = (m×p), and it is really the demand that A\'s outputs be the right kind of thing to be B\'s inputs. And AB ≠ BA because doing one thing and then another is not the same as reversing them: a quarter turn anticlockwise followed by a reflection in the x-axis sends (1,0) to (0,−1), while reflecting first and then turning sends it to (0,1).'],
 m: 'The shape rule stops being something to remember once it reads as "A\'s output space is B\'s input space", and a dimension-mismatch error becomes what it actually is — a function handed the wrong kind of argument. Associativity is free from the same reading, since (AB)C and A(BC) both mean "do C, then B, then A", and it has cash value: with two 1000×1000 weight matrices and one input vector, (W₁W₂)x costs about a billion multiplications while W₁(W₂x) costs about two million, for an identical answer. The reading also explains an architectural fact — two linear layers stacked with nothing in between are a single matrix, and so have the expressive power of one layer, which is exactly what the nonlinearity between them is there to prevent.'
},

'gradient-descent': {
 q: 'Why is the negative gradient the best direction to step in, rather than merely a direction that happens to work?',
 a: 'The gradient is a list of rates, each measured while every other parameter is held still, so at first glance it says nothing about diagonal directions where several parameters move at once. But a diagonal move is just those separate moves happening together, and to first order their effects simply add — which makes the rate of change along any direction the dot product of the gradient with that direction. The previous lesson already settled which direction maximises a dot product, and that settles this one.',
 s: ['Start from what a partial derivative means: ∂L/∂θᵢ is the rate at which the loss changes when θᵢ alone is nudged and everything else stands still. The gradient ∇L is nothing more than those n numbers stacked into a vector.',
   'Pick any direction, a unit vector u, and walk along it: define g(h) = L(θ + hu), an ordinary one-variable function whose derivative at h = 0 is the rate of change of the loss in direction u. Each coordinate θᵢ + huᵢ changes at rate uᵢ, so the chain rule applied once per coordinate and summed gives g′(0) = Σᵢ (∂L/∂θᵢ)uᵢ = ∇L · u. This is the step that requires L to be differentiable at θ — partial derivatives merely existing is not enough — and it is what "the effects add to first order" means precisely. Nothing in the computation used the length of u, so it holds for any vector, not only unit ones.',
   'Suppose ∇L ≠ 0, since where the gradient vanishes there is no direction to prefer to first order anyway. Then the previous lesson\'s identity applies to that dot product: ∇L · u = ‖∇L‖‖u‖cos φ = ‖∇L‖cos φ, since u has length 1, where φ is the angle between the direction chosen and the gradient.',
   'A cosine can be no larger than 1 and no smaller than −1, and both extremes are actually reached here — by u = ∇L/‖∇L‖ and by u = −∇L/‖∇L‖, which are genuine unit vectors. So the rate of change lies between −‖∇L‖ and +‖∇L‖ whatever direction you pick; it is largest along ∇L, which is steepest ascent, most negative along −∇L, and exactly zero for any direction perpendicular to the gradient.',
   'That is why the update reads θ ← θ − η∇L. Run the step 2 computation with the vector −∇L in place of u: the derivative at h = 0 of L(θ − h∇L) is ∇L · (−∇L) = −‖∇L‖², so L(θ − η∇L) = L(θ) − η‖∇L‖² + r(η), where the remainder satisfies r(η)/η → 0 as η → 0. With ∇L ≠ 0 the middle term outweighs the remainder for all small enough η, so the loss strictly falls, by an amount proportional to ‖∇L‖², and it stops falling only where ∇L = 0.'],
 m: 'Both classic failure modes become readable rather than mysterious. "Small enough η" is a promise about a neighbourhood, not a number anyone hands you: take a step long enough to leave the region where the first-order picture holds and you can land higher than you started, which is exactly the loss that bounces instead of descending. And ∇L = 0 says only that the surface is momentarily flat — a minimum, a maximum and a saddle all satisfy it — so the argument guarantees you go downhill, never that you arrive at the lowest point on the surface. There is a third thing worth knowing: "steepest" is measured with the length from the vectors lesson, and that length treats every parameter as being on the same scale, so when one feature is in millimetres and another in kilometres the steepest direction in those coordinates is a poor path. That is the honest reason feature normalisation and the per-parameter step sizes of an optimiser like Adam earn their keep.'
},

bayes: {
 q: 'Why does Bayes\' theorem let you reverse a conditional, and why does a 99% accurate test leave you only about 9% likely to be ill?',
 a: 'P(A|B) and P(B|A) are not two views of one number. They are two different numbers, each obtained by dividing the same overlap by a different total. Writing that overlap both ways and eliminating it between the two equations is the entire theorem — and once the answer is written out, the reason a good test can give a bad answer is sitting in plain view: the two totals can differ by a factor of a thousand.',
 s: ['Fix what conditioning means. P(A|B) asks for the chance of A once B is known to have happened, so B has become the whole world: throw away every outcome outside B, and rescale what remains so that its total probability is 1. The scaling factor is 1/P(B), and the part of A that survives the throwing-away is the overlap A ∩ B. Hence P(A|B) = P(A∩B)/P(B), and the rescaling is meaningless unless P(B) > 0.',
   'The very same definition read the other way round gives P(B|A) = P(A∩B)/P(A) whenever P(A) > 0, which rearranges to P(A∩B) = P(B|A)P(A). The overlap is one quantity carrying two names.',
   'Substitute the second into the first: P(A|B) = P(B|A)P(A)/P(B), valid whenever P(A) and P(B) are both non-zero. That is Bayes\' theorem, and it is nothing more than a shared quantity eliminated between two equations — no new assumption entered beyond the two divisions being legal.',
   'The denominator is rarely handed to you directly, so split B according to whether A happened; the two cases exclude each other and between them cover everything, so P(B) = P(A∩B) + P(¬A∩B) = P(B|A)P(A) + P(B|¬A)P(¬A), the last step needing P(A) and P(¬A) both non-zero — true for any disease that is neither impossible nor universal. Every quantity on the right is a forward probability, the kind that can actually be measured.',
   'Now the disease. Let A be "ill" and B be "tests positive", with P(A) = 0.001, P(B|A) = 0.99 and P(B|¬A) = 0.01. The numerator is 0.99 × 0.001 = 0.00099. The denominator is 0.00099 + 0.01 × 0.999 = 0.01098. The ratio is about 0.09. Read the denominator\'s two terms against each other and the reason is plain: the 1% of false positives is drawn from a pool almost a thousand times larger than the pool of ill people, so it produces about ten times as many positives as the test\'s 99% success rate does.'],
 m: 'The posterior computed here is the fraction of positives that are real, which is precision — and precision moves with the base rate, while the two numbers quoted for the test do not: "catches 99% of the ill" and "clears 99% of the well" are properties of the test alone, measured on each group separately. Accuracy is neither of those. It is a base-rate-weighted blend of the two, which is exactly why it flatters a rare-event classifier: on this population you can answer "well" every single time, catch nobody at all, and still score 99.9% accurate. The calculation also tells you what to change: pushing the false positive rate from 1% down to 0.1% moves the answer from 9% to about 50%, while pushing the 99% hit rate all the way to a perfect 100% moves it from 9.0% to 9.1%. And the "naive" in naive Bayes names the assumption that makes P(B|A) computable when B is a whole message rather than one word — treat the words as independent given the class, and multiply their probabilities. That is plainly false for real text, since "free" and "offer" travel together, and it is useful anyway, because the product usually ranks the classes correctly even when the numbers themselves are badly wrong.'
},

entropy: {
 q: 'Why does −Σ pᵢ log₂ pᵢ count bits, when it is only a formula about probabilities?',
 a: 'Bits are a counting resource: ℓ of them name at most 2^ℓ things, and no cleverness gets around that. So to find what a source truly costs, count the messages it actually produces. Code one long stretch of output rather than one symbol at a time, and something remarkable narrows: almost all the probability collects on the messages whose symbol counts match the probabilities, and there are only about 2^(NH) of those among a far larger crowd. Numbering just the messages that really occur costs NH bits, which is H per symbol.',
 s: ['The only lower bound needed is pigeonhole: a string of ℓ bits can name at most 2^ℓ distinct things, so telling M things apart needs at least log₂ M bits. Anything shorter forces two messages to share a name, and one of them can then never be recovered.',
   'The uniform case is exact and needs nothing more. With n equally likely outcomes, naming one costs log₂ n bits, and the formula agrees: H = −Σ (1/n)log₂(1/n) = log₂ n. All the work lies in showing the same formula is right when the outcomes are not equally likely.',
   'So do not code one symbol — code a block of N independent draws from the same distribution. By the law of large numbers, as N grows the probability approaches 1 that outcome i appeared about pᵢN times. Almost all the probability therefore sits on the messages carrying those counts, and each of those messages has essentially the same probability as the others, namely about 2^(−NH).',
   'Count them. Filling N slots with pᵢN copies of each outcome — take those counts to be whole numbers, which costs nothing as N grows — can be done in N!/∏ᵢ(pᵢN)! ways, and Stirling\'s approximation log₂ m! ≈ m log₂ m − m log₂ e turns the logarithm of that count into (N log₂ N − N log₂ e) − Σᵢ(pᵢN log₂(pᵢN) − pᵢN log₂ e). Since Σpᵢ = 1, the two N log₂ e terms cancel and so do the two N log₂ N terms, leaving −N Σᵢ pᵢ log₂ pᵢ = NH. The messages that actually occur therefore number about 2^(NH).',
   'Apply step 1 to that count: numbering 2^(NH) messages needs NH bits and no fewer, which is H bits per symbol, and it is achievable because numbering them is itself a code. H is simultaneously the floor and the target. What is assumed here is Stirling\'s approximation, the law of large numbers, and that the atypical messages — real, but of vanishing total probability — can be given long codewords whose contribution to the average washes out as N grows. Making that last part airtight is Shannon\'s source coding theorem; this is its skeleton, not a substitute for it.'],
 m: 'Cross-entropy is this same quantity with the code built from the wrong distribution. If the model believes ŷ and you build the code that would be optimal for ŷ — length log₂(1/ŷᵢ) for outcome i — while the world actually draws from y, the average length you pay is −Σ yᵢ log₂ ŷᵢ, which is the loss exactly. It cannot fall below H(y), and for the reason step 1 already gave: a code built from ŷ is still a code, so the counting bound that made H(y) the floor for every code applies to this one too. The gap between the two is a genuine excess — it is the Kullback–Leibler divergence, non-negative and zero only when ŷ = y, a fact this argument makes plausible rather than proves — so minimising cross-entropy is minimising a real cost and it bottoms out precisely at ŷ = y. This also explains the shape of the penalty instead of leaving it a quirk: a codeword for an outcome the model gave probability 0.001 is about 10 bits long, against 1 bit for one it gave 0.5, so confidence in a wrong answer costs what it costs because you built a long codeword for the thing that then happened. The same H being the floor on compression is why already-compressed data will not shrink again: the entropy has been extracted, and there is nothing left to take.'
},

};
